window.GUIDE_SEARCH = [{"id":1,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"What is RAG, and how does a retrieval service fit into an AI application?","text":"What is RAG, and how does a retrieval service fit into an AI application? Retrieval-Augmented Generation combines externally retrieved evidence with a language model's generation. A user question becomes a search request; relevant passages become context; a generator writes an answer conditioned on that context. the retrieval service implements reusable ingestion, retrieval, evidence, and operational pieces. A host still supplies the conversational application and its answer-generation policy. Separate the offline path, which builds a searchable corpus, from the online path, which retrieves evidence for a question. This lets a source change trigger indexing without retraining the language model. The original RAG paper combines parametric model memory with a retrieved non-parametric index; this reference design is an engineering subsystem, not a reproduction of that training architecture. Retrieval can miss the right passage, and a generator can ignore a correct passage. The evidence assembler exposes explicit failure states and citations, but cannot prove every generated claim is true. External knowledge improves freshness and traceability at the cost of ingestion, search latency, access control, and evaluation work. Small stable tasks may need only a prompt or structured database query. Trace one document through ingestion and one question through search. Draw the boundary where your own generator must be called."},{"id":2,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"Why can an LLM sound confident while being wrong?","text":"Why can an LLM sound confident while being wrong? A Large Language Model predicts token sequences using learned patterns. Fluent continuation and factual verification are different capabilities. If the prompt lacks the relevant fact, or includes ambiguous evidence, a plausible continuation can still be false. Confidence in the prose is not a calibrated probability that the service request is correct. In the reference design, the retrieval and evidence layers supply inspectable material before a host invokes generation. an evidence-status type distinguishes missing evidence from a failed backend, giving the application a basis for abstaining or asking a follow-up question. Presenting a citation does not establish that the citation supports the attached sentence. the retrieval service preserves evidence identity, while claim-to-evidence checking and appropriate abstention remain host responsibilities. Restricting answers to supplied evidence improves auditability but can reduce coverage. Allowing general model knowledge expands coverage while weakening the promise that every answer reflects the indexed corpus. Ask one answerable, one ambiguous, and one unanswerable question. Write separate expected behaviors before evaluating any generated output."},{"id":3,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"How are search, retrieval, and generation different responsibilities?","text":"How are search, retrieval, and generation different responsibilities? Search ranks records against a query. Retrieval includes query preparation, backend selection, constraint enforcement, candidate fetching, and optional reranking. Generation turns the resulting evidence and instructions into a new answer. Treating these as one function hides whether an error came from missing data, ranking, or answer synthesis. A hybrid retriever and the strategy classes orchestrate search; an evidence assembler builds a bounded evidence bundle. Their return types are useful without a chat model. This separation also supports source verification tools and ordinary search interfaces. A fallback that silently generates when search fails can convert an infrastructure outage into misinformation. Use strict retrieval and propagate backend failure into the host's response state. Explicit stages require more wiring and more status handling. They also let you benchmark retrieval independently and switch generation providers without rebuilding the corpus. Design three response payloads: successful evidence, successful empty search, and backend failure. Explain which may safely reach generation."},{"id":4,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"When should I use RAG instead of fine-tuning or a SQL query?","text":"When should I use RAG instead of fine-tuning or a SQL query? Use retrieval when answers depend on a changing collection and need source attribution. Use a structured query when the task is exact aggregation over authoritative fields. Consider fine-tuning when the persistent problem is behavior, style, or task adaptation rather than missing up-to-date facts. These approaches can be combined. The retrieval service's document and evidence abstractions suit passage retrieval. A question such as total approved invoices this month should usually reach a database computation; nearest passages cannot guarantee an exact total. Fine-tuning is not implemented by this subsystem. A semantic similarity score can make an approximate answer look exact. The host must route tasks appropriately and preserve authorization on both document search and structured tools. Task routing adds complexity but avoids forcing every question through a vector index. A single retrieval path is easier to prototype and easier to misuse. Classify ten realistic product questions into passage retrieval, database computation, general explanation, or behavior adaptation. Defend two borderline cases."},{"id":5,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"What is the difference between a document, a chunk, a vector, and evidence?","text":"What is the difference between a document, a chunk, a vector, and evidence? A document is a source-level unit of text and metadata. A chunk is a searchable subdivision. An embedding vector is a numerical representation of that subdivision. Evidence is selected, attributed text prepared for a consuming model or user. These objects have different lifetimes and should not share one overloaded identity field. The canonical Chunk, Document, and SearchResult dataclasses separate ingestion and search shapes; evidence types add revision, source, span, and citation information. A chunk hit becomes useful evidence only after selection and attribution. Confusing a chunk ID with a document ID breaks deletion, citation lookup, and version switching. the retrieval service carries metadata through the pipeline, but hosts must supply stable source identities and meaningful provenance. More explicit types require conversion at boundaries. They reduce accidental field loss and make changes to storage independent of the user-facing citation contract. Annotate a sample result with its source ID, document ID, revision, chunk ID, text, vector dimension, and evidence label. Identify which fields can change after reindexing."},{"id":6,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"What do dense and sparse retrieval mean?","text":"What do dense and sparse retrieval mean? Dense retrieval compares learned vectors, making semantic paraphrases discoverable. Sparse or lexical retrieval scores token-based signals, making exact names, identifiers, and unusual terms discoverable. They capture different notions of relevance. Neither should be described as understanding the user's authorization or guaranteeing truth. The retrieval service separates a vector index and a lexical index capabilities and composes them in hybrid retrieval. PostgreSQL offers native full-text search; some other adapters approximate lexical ranking with token overlap. Those are different implementations, not equivalent language models. Dense retrieval can confuse related concepts; lexical retrieval can miss synonyms. Hybrid fusion reduces dependence on one signal but adds its own ranking choices and cannot recover absent source material. Two signals increase implementation and evaluation cost. A keyword-only strategy can be preferable for exact support codes or a prototype with no embedding service. Create queries containing a product code, a paraphrase, and a misspelling. Predict which retrieval stage should help, then inspect both ranked lists."},{"id":7,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"What does grounding actually guarantee?","text":"What does grounding actually guarantee? Grounding means giving an answer process explicit evidence it can use. It is a relationship between a service request and supporting material, not a property conferred by vector search alone. A grounded workflow must preserve provenance, show the relevant revision, and handle insufficient evidence honestly. An evidence assembler deduplicates, merges eligible spans, budgets rendered context, and creates citation labels. revision-aware source lookup can retrieve a bounded span from a retained revision. These mechanisms make verification possible rather than automatically performing every verification step. A wrong retrieved passage, stale document, or unsupported inference can survive into the answer. Host-side citation checks and evaluation are still necessary; delimiter escaping is only one mitigation against untrusted source instructions. Strong attribution needs snapshot retention and richer metadata. Minimal text-only search is cheaper but cannot support reliable historical verification. Write an answer with three factual claims. Map each claim to an exact evidence span; flag any sentence whose support requires an unstated inference."},{"id":8,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"How do relevance, correctness, faithfulness, and freshness differ?","text":"How do relevance, correctness, faithfulness, and freshness differ? Relevance asks whether material addresses the question. Correctness asks whether the service request is true. Faithfulness asks whether the answer follows the provided evidence. Freshness asks whether the evidence reflects an appropriate time or revision. An answer can be faithful to an obsolete policy and therefore be incorrect for today's user. The reference design separates retrieval evaluation from answer evaluation and carries freshness and revision fields in evidence. These signals allow a host to assess different failure modes rather than collapse quality into a single score. High retrieval recall does not establish answer correctness. A heuristic overlap score also cannot establish that a reasoning step is valid or that the source itself is authoritative. Multiple metrics complicate release decisions but expose regressions that one average hides. The right acceptance criteria depend on the product's questions and corpus. Construct four answers that each fail a different dimension. Explain which measurement and which code boundary would reveal each failure."},{"id":9,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"What are the main cost and latency stages in a RAG request?","text":"What are the main cost and latency stages in a RAG request? Typical stages are query transformation, query embedding, candidate search, fusion, reranking, evidence assembly, and host generation. Ingestion has separate extraction, chunking, embedding, and storage costs. The relative expense depends on providers, data size, network placement, and configuration; this reference design does not establish universal timings. Separating candidate_k from final_k permits a larger search pool without necessarily sending every hit to generation. Telemetry records stage events and durations, and the embedding executor bounds concurrency and provider call duration when configured. Increasing query variants, candidate counts, and retries together can multiply load. No single semaphore controls an entire fleet or gives an end-to-end request deadline. Higher candidate recall often costs more latency and money. Optimize against a quality target using measured stage distributions, not guessed millisecond service requests. Create a latency budget spreadsheet with p50 and p95 per stage and an overall deadline. Mark every number as a hypothesis until you measure it."},{"id":10,"slug":"foundations","chapter":"RAG foundations","title":"What does domain-agnostic mean, and what does it not solve?","text":"What does domain-agnostic mean, and what does it not solve? Domain-agnostic means the core can work with different application schemas, sources, settings, and storage implementations through explicit interfaces. It does not mean every domain has identical chunking needs, permissions, legal retention rules, or quality thresholds. Those knowledge rules remain part of the host's product design. The retrieval service receives settings, sessions, version storage, embedders, and retrieval strategies rather than importing a host's models. The same core can serve a knowledge corpus or a support corpus while their adapters and evaluation datasets differ. Generic abstractions can hide important domain constraints if the host never models them. A tenant filter is useful only when trusted identity selects the correct scope and every storage path respects it. Reusability reduces coupling but requires deliberate integration work. A specialized application may be quicker initially and harder to reuse safely later. Compare an HR policy assistant and a public FAQ assistant. List five knowledge rules that differ even if they use identical the retrieval service components."},{"id":11,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"Why separate a reusable retrieval subsystem from a complete application?","text":"Why separate a reusable retrieval subsystem from a complete application? The package supplies composable RAG infrastructure while the host owns application models, authentication, routing, deployment, and generation. That boundary allows an existing backend to adopt retrieval without letting a library control its domain schema or request lifecycle. Expose retrieval, ingestion, and evidence contracts independently from web routes and business models. The host binds authentication, persistence, and generation policy. This makes components testable without implying that every backend is interchangeable. Integration errors move to the seams: missing scope policy, incorrect transaction ownership, or a fake embedder accidentally used for real search. Examples and contract tests help, but the host must review its wiring. You gain control and portability while doing more assembly. A packaged application would offer faster initial setup with more predetermined choices. Draw a ports-and-adapters diagram showing which side owns users, SQL tables, provider credentials, evidence, and chat responses."},{"id":12,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"How does dependency inversion appear in the reference design?","text":"How does dependency inversion appear in the reference design? A fluent builder assembles collaborators through readable configuration methods and a final construction step. It can provide safe local defaults while requiring explicit production dependencies. The builder is a convenience facade; the lower-level contracts remain useful independently. The ingestion coordinator accepts its collaborators in the constructor. the version repository receives the active session for mutations, and a SQL adapter interface abstracts database operations. You can replace a collaborator with a controlled test implementation without rewriting orchestration. An adapter can satisfy a Python method signature while violating atomicity or isolation semantics. Structural compatibility is weaker than behavioral compatibility, so tests need to exercise the promised guarantees. Interfaces add indirection and documentation work. They pay off when deployment stacks differ or when failure paths need deterministic testing. Replace version storage with a tiny recording fake. Verify the order of find, close, create, delete, and upsert calls without assuming that call order alone proves rollback safety."},{"id":13,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"Why use Protocol instead of requiring every adapter to inherit a base class?","text":"Why use Protocol instead of requiring every adapter to inherit a base class? Python Protocol supports structural typing: an object can satisfy a contract by providing the required members. This is useful when a host already has settings or session providers and should not need to inherit library-specific classes. Retrieval settings and several storage seams express the attributes or methods consumers need. Some interfaces also use runtime_checkable, but runtime checks do not fully validate annotations, returned values, or transactional behavior. Duck typing can postpone integration errors until execution. Validate configuration at startup and run adapter contract tests; do not interpret isinstance against a protocol as a complete certification. Protocols reduce inheritance coupling while offering less shared implementation. Abstract base classes remain useful when construction rules or common behavior must be enforced. Supply a dataclass as settings, then remove a required attribute. Compare static type feedback with the eventual runtime failure."},{"id":14,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"What does the Fluent Builder pattern contribute here?","text":"What does the Fluent Builder pattern contribute here? A retrieval-service builder assembles collaborators through readable configuration methods and an asynchronous build step. The local constructor chooses in-memory defaults while still requiring an embedding function. The result is a convenient facade over the lower-level components. This keeps a small experiment short while allowing chunkers, stores, version stores, and settings to be replaced. The builder is composition convenience; it does not make an in-memory setup durable or create a learned local embedding model. Hidden defaults can be carried into production. Review the resolved store, embedding dimension, scope policy, and state retention before deploying an instance built for local use. A builder is easier to discover than a large constructor but adds another assembly surface to maintain. Direct constructors are clearer for unusual production wiring. Run the offline quickstart, then rewrite its setup with explicit collaborators. Describe which defaults you would replace for a multi-worker service."},{"id":15,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"How are Factory, Registry, Strategy, and Adapter different patterns?","text":"How are Factory, Registry, Strategy, and Adapter different patterns? A factory constructs a collaborator. A registry maps names to implementations. A strategy selects interchangeable behavior such as dense, lexical, or hybrid retrieval. An adapter translates an external API into the reference design's contract. The patterns solve different problems and often appear together. Store factories and registries choose backend implementations; retrieval strategies compose search capabilities; backend classes translate vendor operations. This allows configuration-driven construction without forcing every search behavior into a storage class. A name-based registry can defer typos or missing optional dependency failures until runtime. Validate names and dependencies during startup, and avoid loading plugins based on arbitrary untrusted input. Configurable assembly improves extension but increases possible combinations. Support a documented matrix rather than assuming every registered backend supports every strategy and filter. Explain how adding a new vector backend differs from adding a query reranker. Identify the construction, contract, and behavior changes separately."},{"id":16,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"Why split the vector-store interface into smaller capability protocols?","text":"Why split the vector-store interface into smaller capability protocols? A broad store interface is convenient when one backend supports every operation. Separate vector search, lexical search, fetch, and write capabilities let callers depend only on the operations they need. A keyword-only route should not require an embedding provider. The retrieval service's retrieval strategies compose vector and lexical capabilities, permitting different backends for different signals. Capability declarations describe supported filters and guarantees before the strategy executes. A backend may advertise a capability it implements incompletely. Mandatory constraints should fail closed, and contract tests should verify semantics rather than only checking that methods exist. More interfaces create more concepts, but avoid fake implementations of unsupported methods. A single-store facade remains helpful for simple applications. Design a lexical-only adapter. List the interfaces it needs and the vector-specific methods it should never have to emulate."},{"id":17,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"What does a SQL adapter interface abstract, and what remains PostgreSQL-specific?","text":"What does a SQL adapter interface abstract, and what remains PostgreSQL-specific? A SQL adapter interface abstracts session construction, execution, fetching, and advisory locking. It lets orchestration avoid direct dependence on one SQL library. It does not make PostgreSQL full-text functions or pgvector operators portable to an arbitrary database engine. The PostgreSQL vector adapter still owns database-specific SQL. An alternative driver can implement the factory contract while preserving PostgreSQL semantics; a different database usually needs a different storage adapter and capability declaration. Replacing the driver without matching transaction and lock behavior can invalidate ingestion guarantees. A no-op advisory-lock implementation is not an equivalent production replacement. The extra seam improves driver flexibility and testability but carries semantic obligations. Direct driver calls would be simpler and more tightly coupled. Write down the minimum behavior an async database driver adapter must preserve for a locked ingestion transaction. Include failure and rollback cases."},{"id":18,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"Why use dataclasses and explicit converters at API boundaries?","text":"Why use dataclasses and explicit converters at API boundaries? Dataclasses make fields, defaults, and meanings discoverable without requiring a validation framework. Explicit converters keep dictionary-based adapters compatible while typed consumers work with Document, SearchResult, RetrievalRequest, and RetrievalResponse. As_dicts and typed retrieval bridges separate legacy shapes from newer APIs. This supports gradual adoption without forcing every host and backend to migrate simultaneously. Type annotations alone do not validate hostile input, and conversions can lose fields such as reranker scores if an older result type does not represent them. Validate transport input and choose the typed response when you need its richer score information. Compatibility layers cost code and can preserve ambiguity. Removing them simplifies internals but may break downstream callers; a migration policy must be explicit. Convert one typed hit to a dictionary and back. Check identity, metadata, distance, RRF score, and reranker score separately."},{"id":19,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"How does the Null Object pattern appear in telemetry?","text":"How does the Null Object pattern appear in telemetry? A a null event sink implements the event-sink contract while doing nothing. Consumers can emit an event without checking whether telemetry is configured at every call site. This keeps optional observability out of the main control flow. Constructors select a null sink when no sink is supplied. A real sink can later export sanitized events, and the business path can retain the same call structure. A null sink can make missing production instrumentation invisible. Also inspect whether a supplied sink can raise or block; optional observability should not accidentally become an uncontrolled availability dependency. Fewer branches improve readability, while silent default behavior requires explicit operational verification. An application may choose mandatory instrumentation at its composition root. Run the same retrieval with a recording sink and a null sink. Verify equal results and inspect which dimensions the recording sink receives."},{"id":20,"slug":"architecture","chapter":"Architecture and design patterns","title":"How should I defend the architecture in an interview without inventing author intent?","text":"How should I defend the architecture in an interview without inventing author intent? Describe observed boundaries, explain the problems they address, and distinguish inference from documented intent. For example, injected sessions support host transaction ownership; you can infer a portability benefit without claiming the author measured it against every ORM or framework. Tie architectural explanations to an invariant and a failure scenario. For example, caller-owned transactions allow a business update and an outbox record to commit together. Explain the consequences without inventing historical author intent. Repeating comments as guarantees can mislead. Some comments simplify current behavior: the generic hybrid strategy awaits stages sequentially, and sliding-window chunks use UUIDs rather than universally deterministic IDs. Qualified service requests sound less absolute but survive technical scrutiny. A good design defense acknowledges obligations pushed to host adapters and conditions under which another design would win. Prepare a five-minute defense with three source references, two explicit limitations, and one alternative architecture you would choose under different requirements."},{"id":21,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"Why distinguish discovery, fetching, extraction, and ingestion?","text":"Why distinguish discovery, fetching, extraction, and ingestion? Discovery enumerates source items. Fetching obtains bytes. Extraction converts those bytes into usable text and provenance. Ingestion chunks, embeds, and indexes the result. Keeping these boundaries separate lets the same extraction or indexing logic serve several source systems. Connector records carry discovery and load status, while a source-document record carries content and source metadata into the pipeline. A PDF extraction failure is therefore different from an empty bucket or an embedding provider outage. Flattening every failure into an empty list can cause a later reconciliation to delete healthy knowledge. The connector contract represents errors and whether a result is authoritative; the pipeline refuses non-authoritative a typed scan result mutation. Rich status handling takes more code than returning strings. It supports safe retries, deletion decisions, and actionable diagnostics. Trace a PDF in an object bucket through all four stages. Specify the returned status when each stage fails and which stages can be retried independently."},{"id":22,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"What makes a source scan authoritative?","text":"What makes a source scan authoritative? An authoritative scan represents the complete intended snapshot under its configured scope and traversal rules. A successful first page is not a complete snapshot. Authority also depends on whether errors, limits, or missing pages could have hidden existing documents. A typed scan result distinguishes completeness and status; sync uses that authority to infer removals. The ordinary ingestion pipeline skips a non-authoritative a typed scan result rather than replacing its current indexed content with a partial view. Treating a timeout or permission error as authoritative emptiness can erase knowledge. Conversely, never trusting a complete empty scan leaves deleted content searchable. Both require explicit semantics. Conservative authority rules may delay deletion after a source outage. That delay is preferable to interpreting an incomplete traversal as proof of absence, but access revocation needs a separate immediate control. Simulate a three-page source where page two fails. Explain why the successfully fetched documents do not prove that all missing documents were deleted."},{"id":23,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"How should pagination and checkpoints work for incremental ingestion?","text":"How should pagination and checkpoints work for incremental ingestion? A cursor identifies where enumeration should continue; a checkpoint records safely processed progress. They are not interchangeable. Saving a cursor before the corresponding data is activated can skip work after a crash, while saving too late can repeat work. Connectors expose page and full-load methods, and sync checkpoints are guarded by the example fencing token. Idempotency and complete-snapshot reconciliation make repeated pages safer than silently losing them. Sources may mutate between pages, causing duplicates or omissions even if every request succeeds. Stable ordering, source-side snapshot tokens where available, deduplication, and periodic full reconciliation are integration concerns. Full scans simplify reconciliation but cost more source I/O. Incremental feeds are faster but require careful resume and deletion semantics. Draw crashes immediately before activation, after activation, and after checkpoint saving. State which records will repeat and why no record should be skipped."},{"id":24,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"How does the directory connector reduce filesystem risks?","text":"How does the directory connector reduce filesystem risks? Directory ingestion must constrain traversal to an intended root, apply inclusion knowledge rules, bound resource use, and avoid unsafe path or symlink behavior. The connector implements explicit open, target, and read helpers rather than blindly opening every discovered string. The directory adapter also records intentional skips separately from scan errors. That distinction preserves the meaning of completeness: a deliberately excluded file is not the same as an inaccessible file that should have been included. A local filesystem is not automatically trusted. Permission changes and concurrent file replacement can occur during scanning, and host deployment permissions still determine what the process can access. Strict traversal and file knowledge rules can exclude legitimate sources and need configuration. Broad scanning is easier to start and harder to secure or make reproducible. Read the directory connector's _open and _target helpers. Build a small temporary corpus containing ignored files, nested directories, and a symlink, and predict the reported skips."},{"id":25,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"Why does an HTTP connector need SSRF protection?","text":"Why does an HTTP connector need SSRF protection? Server-Side Request Forgery occurs when a server fetches a destination chosen through untrusted input and reaches internal services or metadata endpoints. A document URL can be an attack input just as an API parameter can. The HTTP connector validates destinations, pins requests to an approved public IP, disables automatic redirects, and checks redirects explicitly. It rejects attempts to turn off its network protections. Website crawling requires explicit allowed domains. Domain checking alone cannot stop DNS rebinding or redirect-based bypasses. Egress policy, deployment DNS behavior, and the actual client transport also matter. Treat source fetching as a separate trust boundary. Public-only fetching limits access to legitimate private knowledge services. Supporting private services safely requires a deliberately trusted integration rather than a blanket bypass of URL validation. Inspect the checks before and after a redirect. Explain why allowing example.com does not authorize a redirect to a loopback address."},{"id":26,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"How should HTTP rate limits, redirects, and partial crawls affect indexing?","text":"How should HTTP rate limits, redirects, and partial crawls affect indexing? Rate limits and timeouts indicate unavailable or incomplete source access, not document deletion. Redirects may change origin and therefore change what credentials may safely be forwarded. Crawl limits bound work but can also prevent a complete snapshot. The connector tracks statuses such as rate-limited or incomplete traversal, checks redirect destinations, and rejects authenticated cross-origin redirects. Sync can use result authority to decide whether removal inference is permissible. Retrying an incomplete scan without bounds can overload the source; accepting it as complete can remove data. A bounded source request still needs a host scheduling and retry policy for repeated jobs. Tight crawl limits improve predictability while reducing corpus coverage. Completeness must reflect the limit rather than presenting the bounded subset as the whole source. Design a crawl that reaches its configured limit exactly. Determine from the connector's terminal state whether it is authoritative, rather than guessing from the number of returned pages."},{"id":27,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"What identity and consistency issues arise with object storage?","text":"What identity and consistency issues arise with object storage? Object keys identify source items, but content can change under the same key. ETags, version identifiers, metadata, and content hashes can describe different aspects of revision identity. An ETag should not automatically be treated as a universal content checksum. An object-storage adapter separates listing and reading and returns provenance through a source-document record. Its full-load path must account for continuation tokens and errors before a scan can authorize removal of absent objects. Listing and fetching can observe different moments. A host needing exact historical verification should retain source versions or snapshots rather than assuming the latest object body matches a previous citation. Versioned reads and snapshot retention cost storage and integration work. Reading only current objects is simpler but weakens reproducibility. Change one object between listing and download. Describe which metadata lets you detect or explain the observed revision and which guarantees the source must provide."},{"id":28,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"Why is text extraction part of the retrieval quality problem?","text":"Why is text extraction part of the retrieval quality problem? Search cannot recover information that extraction discarded or scrambled. PDF reading order, tables, headings, repeated headers, and image-only pages can distort the indexed text. Extraction quality therefore sets an upper bound on downstream retrieval quality. Extractors are separate adapters and extractor identity participates in ingestion fingerprints. Changing extraction behavior can require reindexing even if the underlying file bytes did not change. A successful parse may still yield semantically broken text. The reference design does not supply a universal OCR or table-understanding guarantee; inspect output and use host-selected extractors where needed. Rich document parsing improves structure at the cost of dependencies, compute, and more failure modes. Plain text extraction is easier to operate but can lose relationships that answers depend on. Extract a two-column page and a table. Compare the text to the source visually, then write a retrieval question that fails if cell relationships are lost."},{"id":29,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"Which source metadata must survive into evidence?","text":"Which source metadata must survive into evidence? Preserve stable source and document identity, revision, title or section, location or offsets where available, and the relevant isolation scope. These fields support deletion, attribution, source opening, and debugging. They are not decorative labels added after search. The ingestion coordinator merges document and chunk metadata and stamps the actual source name. It also forwards external_id, provenance, location, and extractor information. Evidence types then normalize attribution fields for consumers. Metadata may contain secrets or untrusted URLs. Sanitizing a displayed URI is not the same as safely fetching it, and arbitrary metadata should not automatically be logged or rendered as HTML. Rich metadata adds storage and schema complexity. Omitting it makes early indexing easy but later provenance reconstruction unreliable. Trace the metadata of two documents with different sources through one ingestion run. Verify that every chunk retains the correct source instead of inheriting one global source label."},{"id":30,"slug":"sources","chapter":"Sources, connectors, and extraction","title":"Why are watchers insufficient without reconciliation?","text":"Why are watchers insufficient without reconciliation? A watcher provides low-latency change notifications, but notifications can be missed during downtime, buffer overflow, or process failure. A periodic reconciliation compares the source's current authoritative state against indexed state and repairs divergence. The directory module includes watcher and periodic reconciler helpers. Sync plans classify additions, changes, removals, metadata-only updates, and unchanged items so a full comparison need not re-embed everything. Assuming an event stream is complete leaves permanently stale knowledge after a missed event. Deletion events also need an explicit target; absence of an event is not proof that an item still exists. Combining watchers with scans spends extra source I/O but improves eventual correctness. Full scans alone are simpler when freshness requirements are modest. Stop the watcher, edit and delete files, then resume it. Describe how a scheduled reconciliation repairs changes that the live event stream never delivered."},{"id":31,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"Why chunk documents at all?","text":"Why chunk documents at all? Chunking creates retrieval units small enough to match a question and fit a context budget. A whole manual can contain many unrelated topics; embedding it as one vector blends signals and returning all of it wastes context. A tiny fragment can lose the conditions that make its meaning correct. The retrieval service injects a Chunker into ingestion. This makes chunking a replaceable strategy whose version and configuration participate in snapshot comparison, rather than an untracked preprocessing script. A boundary can separate a rule from its exception or a table heading from its values. Overlap and section-aware parsing mitigate some boundary loss, but only corpus-specific evaluation reveals whether the chosen units preserve meaning. Smaller chunks improve targeting and increase index size; larger chunks preserve context and can dilute relevance. There is no universally best chunk size. Split the same policy into small and large chunks. Use questions about exceptions and compare whether each retrieved unit contains enough information to answer safely."},{"id":32,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"Does a sliding-window chunker count tokens, words, or characters?","text":"Does a sliding-window chunker count tokens, words, or characters? It counts whitespace-separated words using text.split(). chunk_size and overlap therefore measure words. They do not define a model tokenizer's context size, and they do not behave identically for every language or code-heavy input. The implementation steps through words by chunk_size minus overlap and rejoins each slice. This simple algorithm has no model dependency and is suitable as a transparent baseline. Treating 600 words as 600 tokens can overflow a downstream budget. Languages without whitespace and punctuation-heavy text can also produce surprising retrieval units. The evidence layer has a separate tokenizer abstraction for context accounting. Word splitting is fast and dependency-free, while token-aware or structure-aware splitting can better match actual model and document constraints. Chunk an English paragraph, a Python file, and Arabic text with identical settings. Count actual model tokens separately and compare the ratios."},{"id":33,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"How does overlap affect recall and ingestion cost?","text":"How does overlap affect recall and ingestion cost? Overlap repeats material near neighboring boundaries so that a question spanning a split still has a chance to retrieve a coherent chunk. The stride is chunk_size minus overlap. With long text, the repetition factor is roughly chunk_size divided by stride, ignoring the final partial window. For size 600 and overlap 100, the stride is 500 and the approximate repeated-word factor is 1.2. This is a mathematical estimate of chunked input, not a measured provider billing factor or a quality guarantee. Excessive overlap produces near-duplicate hits and increases embedding work. Evidence deduplication and eligible span merging reduce redundant context, but they do not undo ingestion cost. More overlap can improve boundary coverage while reducing diversity among top hits. Tune against a labeled question set rather than maximizing repetition. Calculate chunk counts for 1,100 words at several overlaps, then verify them with sliding_window_chunk. Explain why the loop stops after the final covered window."},{"id":34,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"Why validate that overlap is smaller than chunk_size?","text":"Why validate that overlap is smaller than chunk_size? The stride must be positive for the window to advance. An overlap equal to the chunk size creates a zero stride; a larger overlap creates an invalid or backward progression. These are algorithm validity constraints, not optional quality preferences. Both the function and the chunker constructor validate positive size and overlap in the interval from zero inclusive to size exclusive. Invalid configuration fails early before ingestion mutates versions or spends embedding calls. A malformed chunking configuration can block all ingestion or unexpectedly produce no chunks. Startup validation and focused boundary tests make such failures easier to diagnose than late failures inside a scheduled job. Strict validation rejects configurations that a permissive implementation might silently repair. Silent repair would conceal the actual indexing configuration and make snapshot identity less meaningful. Exercise zero size, negative overlap, overlap equal to size, and empty text. Distinguish invalid configuration from a valid empty result."},{"id":35,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"How does a section-aware chunker preserve semantic structure?","text":"How does a section-aware chunker preserve semantic structure? It splits Markdown on H2 headings, reconstructs a section with its title, and delegates that section to an inner chunker. Section titles therefore participate in embedded text rather than existing only as metadata. The inner strategy still controls subdivision of a long section. Composition reuses the sliding-window algorithm while adding section, chunk index, and sub-chunk index metadata. File reading remains in the source adapter; the chunker operates on text. A regular expression for H2 headings is not a complete Markdown parser. Unusual markup and headings inside fenced code can create misleading boundaries. Evaluate actual corpus syntax before relying on section semantics. This wrapper is lightweight and understandable, while a full syntax-tree parser handles richer structure at higher implementation and dependency cost. Use a document with a preamble, H2 sections, H3 headings, and a fenced code sample containing a heading-like line. Inspect the exact emitted chunk text."},{"id":36,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"Are chunk IDs deterministic in this implementation?","text":"Are chunk IDs deterministic in this implementation? Chunk IDs may be content-derived, position-derived, randomly generated, or scoped to an index generation. No choice is universally stable. Define identity around the operations it must support: deduplication, revision tracking, source lookup, and safe replacement. Store document revision, span, chunker profile, and generation separately where needed. Test repeated chunking and document edits; stable identity requires an explicit contract rather than an assumption about UUIDs. Conflating position metadata with the actual store ID can break deduplication, deletion, and citation mapping. Comments about position-based chunk identifiers should be interpreted in the relevant adapter context, not generalized to every chunker. Random physical IDs avoid some collisions but need lifecycle tracking. Deterministic IDs simplify repeat writes but must include enough scope and revision information to avoid accidental replacement. Chunk identical input twice, edit a paragraph, and compare the identity guarantees of content-based, positional, and generation-scoped IDs."},{"id":37,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"Why must chunker configuration be part of an ingestion fingerprint?","text":"Why must chunker configuration be part of an ingestion fingerprint? The same source text produces a different index when chunk size, overlap, section handling, or algorithm version changes. Hashing only file content would incorrectly classify that index as current and skip the required rebuild. The snapshot builder records chunker identity, version, and sanitized configuration alongside source and embedding information. snapshot_matches therefore represents compatibility of the indexing process rather than only equality of input bytes. A custom chunker that hides meaningful configuration can keep stale vectors active after a behavior change. A version property is useful only if maintainers update it when output semantics change. Detailed fingerprints trigger more rebuilds and improve reproducibility. Minimal fingerprints save work while making silent configuration drift more likely. Ingest unchanged text, change overlap, and ingest again. Verify that the snapshot changes even though the source hash remains equal."},{"id":38,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"What would parent-child or hierarchical retrieval add?","text":"What would parent-child or hierarchical retrieval add? Parent-child retrieval searches fine-grained child chunks, then expands selected hits into a larger parent section or document for context. Hierarchical retrieval can first choose a topic or summary and then search within it. These are design extensions, not a default pipeline implemented by this reference design. The retrieval service's metadata, fetch capabilities, and evidence assembly seams provide useful building blocks, but a host must implement expansion, identity mapping, authorization, and evaluation. Expansion should occur before final budgeting so its actual rendered size is counted. Parent expansion can expose unauthorized neighboring text or overwhelm context. A child being authorized does not automatically authorize every parent span or sibling chunk. More context may resolve ambiguity while increasing latency and irrelevant material. A simple section-aware chunker may be sufficient for a small structured corpus. Design an extension that expands a hit to its section. State which metadata is required and where scope checks and budget checks must run."},{"id":39,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"How should chunking change for code, tables, and multilingual documents?","text":"How should chunking change for code, tables, and multilingual documents? Use boundaries that preserve the relationships questions depend on: functions and classes for code, row and column context for tables, and language-appropriate segmentation for text. A word window is a baseline, not proof that all these formats retain their semantics. The Chunker protocol lets a host substitute a parser-based strategy without changing the entire ingestion pipeline. Metadata can preserve symbols, table identifiers, or locations for evidence presentation. Combining unrelated rows or truncating a function's preconditions can generate a misleading answer from faithfully quoted text. Arbitrary structure metadata also needs stable versioning and source lookup support. Specialized chunkers improve some domains while increasing maintenance and creating less comparable retrieval units. Maintain a shared evaluation interface across them. Build three questions that require a function's error branch, a table's unit header, and a multilingual synonym. Compare a structural chunker with the word-window baseline."},{"id":40,"slug":"chunking","chapter":"Chunking and document structure","title":"How do I tune chunking scientifically?","text":"How do I tune chunking scientifically? Hold the corpus, query set, embedding profile, backend settings, and evaluation procedure fixed while varying chunking. Measure retrieval recall and rank, evidence fit, answer faithfulness, index size, and embedding volume. Record configurations so the result is reproducible. The retrieval service separates chunking from embedding and evaluation, making controlled experiments possible. Its snapshots help identify configuration changes, but they are not an experiment tracker by themselves. Optimizing on a handful of visible questions overfits the benchmark. Split tuning and held-out questions and include boundary cases, exact identifiers, long sections, and unanswerable queries. A full grid search costs indexing time. Start with a small set of plausible configurations and investigate failure examples before expanding the search. Compare three sizes with two overlaps and publish a table containing quality, indexed chunk count, and estimated embedding input. Explain the winning configuration's remaining failure cases."},{"id":41,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"What is an embedding, and why is its dimension not enough to identify it?","text":"What is an embedding, and why is its dimension not enough to identify it? An embedding maps input text to a vector whose geometry supports a similarity task. Dimension is the number of coordinates, not an identity for the learned space. Two providers can both return 1,536 numbers while placing meanings in incompatible coordinate systems. An embedding profile records provider, endpoint, model, revision, dimension, normalization, metric, task mode, and preprocessing configuration. Its canonical hash gives the indexing configuration a stable identity that can be carried into snapshots and caches. Mixing incompatible document and query vectors can produce plausible but meaningless nearest neighbors without a shape error. Dimension validation catches only one class of incompatibility. Rich profile identity makes model migrations safer but requires reindexing discipline and additional metadata. Reusing an index based only on equal dimension is cheaper and unreliable. Define two profiles with the same dimension and different models. Explain why a successful database insert would not prove they can share one search space."},{"id":42,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"Why distinguish document and query embedding modes?","text":"Why distinguish document and query embedding modes? Some embedding models are trained asymmetrically: a short question and a longer passage use different task instructions or modes but remain in a compatible retrieval space. Symmetric models use the same treatment for both. The correct mode is part of the model's intended retrieval contract. A task mode represents DOCUMENT, QUERY, and SYMMETRIC. Query-vector helpers can call embed_query, while document processing can use document embedding methods. Profile compatibility deliberately allows different task modes for an otherwise matching pair. A generic callable may erase mode distinctions unless the adapter implements them correctly. Also, the compatibility helper does not compare preprocessing_config, so custom instruction compatibility needs additional validation. Task-aware interfaces improve provider fidelity but add API surface. A plain callable remains convenient for simple symmetric embedders and offline demonstrations. Inspect a custom adapter's query and document paths. Verify the instructions each sends and explain whether profile equality or query compatibility is the appropriate check."},{"id":43,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"How do cosine, dot product, and L2 differ?","text":"How do cosine, dot product, and L2 differ? Cosine compares direction, dot product combines alignment and magnitude, and L2 measures Euclidean separation. For unit-normalized vectors, squared L2 equals two minus twice the dot product, and dot product equals cosine similarity. Without normalization, those ranking relationships need not hold. Profile enums describe metric and normalization. Stores translate the chosen metric into their backend semantics, while result scores distinguish lower-is-better distance from higher-is-better relevance. A threshold calibrated for one metric cannot safely be reused for another. Unknown dimensions and unnormalized vectors can also make low-level helpers behave unexpectedly if callers bypass validation. Cosine often fits directional semantic comparisons; dot product may intentionally use norm information. The right choice follows the embedding model and index configuration, then empirical evaluation. Compare vectors [1,0], [2,0], and [0,1] under all three measures. Predict which pairs tie and why normalization changes the answer."},{"id":44,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"What validation should happen before storing embedding responses?","text":"What validation should happen before storing embedding responses? Check one vector per input, the expected dimension, and finite numeric values. Preserve input-output order. A response with fewer vectors must fail rather than silently pair only a prefix of the chunks with embeddings. A bounded embedding executor validates response count and geometry; the ingestion coordinator uses strict zip alignment and validates each chunk vector before mutation. Preparation failures therefore occur before the version-switching critical section. Valid shape does not prove semantic quality or correct provider task mode. A constant vector can pass shape validation and still destroy retrieval usefulness, so quality evaluation remains separate. Validation costs a pass over returned coordinates but prevents durable index corruption that is expensive to diagnose later. Skipping it is only a superficial throughput optimization. Use fake providers returning too few vectors, wrong dimensions, NaN, and identical finite vectors. Identify which failures validation catches and which require a retrieval test."},{"id":45,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"How do retries and exponential backoff help without fixing every outage?","text":"How do retries and exponential backoff help without fixing every outage? Retries can recover transient network, rate-limit, and service failures. Exponential backoff delays repeated attempts; jitter reduces synchronized retry bursts. Permanent authentication, configuration, and dimension errors should fail promptly because identical retries are unlikely to help. A bounded embedding executor classifies exceptions, honors exposed Retry-After hints, applies a retry ceiling, and validates responses. Cancellation is propagated rather than treated as another retryable failure. Classification includes message-pattern heuristics and treats unknown errors as transient. Misclassification can waste calls, and Retry-After can extend operation duration. The host still needs an overall deadline and provider-specific error handling where necessary. More retries improve recovery probability while increasing tail latency, cost, and load during an outage. Bound them against the request's purpose and budget. Simulate two transient failures followed by success, then a permanent failure. Record attempts and delays using injected clock and sleep functions."},{"id":46,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"What do concurrency limits, queue bounds, and backpressure actually mean here?","text":"What do concurrency limits, queue bounds, and backpressure actually mean here? A semaphore limits simultaneous work inside one execution scope. A bounded queue limits waiting items. Backpressure controls how producers react when capacity is exhausted. None alone establishes a fleet-wide capacity limit. Combine a bounded producer queue, explicit worker concurrency, request deadlines, and shared provider quotas where needed. Instrument queue time separately from provider latency; an async batch is not a durable job. Many waiting tasks or many process-local executors can still overwhelm memory or a shared provider quota. The host needs admission control and, when appropriate, a bounded job queue and shared quota policy. Small concurrency limits protect providers and increase waiting latency. Larger values improve throughput until the actual service or connection pool saturates. Launch many calls through one executor and then through several instances. Measure in-flight operations and explain why a process-local semaphore is not a global rate limiter."},{"id":47,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"How does embedding caching preserve isolation and compatibility?","text":"How does embedding caching preserve isolation and compatibility? Cache identity combines isolation scope, content hash, embedding profile hash, and task mode. Reusing a vector is safe only when these semantic inputs match the deployment's policy. A content hash alone is insufficient for model changes or tenant isolation. A scoped embedding cache uses scoped keys by default, copies vectors on storage and retrieval, supports LRU capacity and optional TTL, and requires explicit opt-in for cross-tenant sharing. It is an in-process component, not a distributed cache. Cross-tenant reuse can leak equality signals or complicate retention obligations. The class is not thread-safe, and TTL expiry alone is not immediate revocation. The host must align cache scope and purge behavior with its privacy policy. More isolation reduces hit rate while keeping independent data lifecycles manageable. Shared caching saves calls but needs a justified policy. Cache identical content in two scopes with different profiles and task modes. Verify the allowed hits and test scope invalidation."},{"id":48,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"How should an embedding model migration be rolled out?","text":"How should an embedding model migration be rolled out? Treat a vector-space change as a new index generation. Build and validate new vectors without mixing them into the old query space, evaluate quality, activate the new generation, and retain a rollback plan. Query embeddings must switch compatibly with the active documents. Profiles_require_reindex compares canonical hashes, while ingestion snapshots and sync generation mechanisms support detecting configuration changes and controlling visibility. They provide building blocks, not a complete deployment orchestrator for every backend. Switching only the query model can silently degrade retrieval. Reusing caches or thresholds calibrated for the old profile can also invalidate conclusions even if dimensions remain equal. Parallel generations need extra storage and embedding cost but permit verification before cutover. In-place replacement is cheaper and harder to roll back safely. Write a migration checklist with a shadow evaluation, profile hash check, activation condition, and rollback trigger. State how queries choose the active profile."},{"id":49,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"Why can a hashing embedder demonstrate plumbing but not semantic intelligence?","text":"Why can a hashing embedder demonstrate plumbing but not semantic intelligence? The quickstart hashes tokens into a small vector and normalizes it. Shared words produce shared coordinates, making an offline demonstration deterministic and cheap. It has no learned semantic representation and cannot reliably associate paraphrases that share little vocabulary. A retrieval-service builder.local requires an injected embedder; core does not ship pretrained neural weights. The example deliberately supplies a fake so build, ingest, and search can run without API keys or external services. Treating success on that example as proof of production retrieval quality confuses system integration with representation learning. Hash collisions can also make unrelated text appear close in a tiny space. A fake is excellent for controlled tests and onboarding, while a learned provider is needed to evaluate realistic semantic behavior. Keep those purposes distinct. Search with a synonym-only paraphrase and compare it to an exact-word query. Explain the difference using the hashing algorithm rather than attributing it to an LLM."},{"id":50,"slug":"embeddings","chapter":"Embeddings and resilient execution","title":"How should I measure embedding throughput and cost responsibly?","text":"How should I measure embedding throughput and cost responsibly? Track documents and tokens processed, batches, retries, cache hits, provider latency, and validation failures. Estimate total spend using actual provider usage where available; heuristic token estimates are useful for scheduling but are not billing records. Embedding metrics and telemetry support operational accounting, while the executor splits batches and can enforce a soft estimated-input cap. Separating preparation from transactions prevents slow provider calls from unnecessarily occupying ingestion locks. Average latency hides retries and tail behavior. A low per-batch cost can become expensive when overlap, query expansion, and repeated full indexing multiply input volume. Detailed metrics improve tuning while high-cardinality labels and raw text logging create overhead and privacy risks. Prefer aggregated, sanitized dimensions. Compare a cold ingestion with an unchanged-source rerun and a model-change rerun. Attribute work saved by snapshot skipping separately from cache hits."},{"id":51,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"What does a vector database store besides vectors?","text":"What does a vector database store besides vectors? A useful retrieval record contains identity, text or a text reference, a vector, and filterable metadata. Production systems also need revision and scope information, deletion semantics, and an explicit persistence model. A fast nearest-neighbor API alone does not provide those guarantees. The retrieval service's store contracts combine or separate writing, vector search, lexical search, fetch, and deletion. Backend capability declarations describe which operations and constraints the adapter actually supports. An index that returns similar vectors but cannot enforce a required tenant filter is unsuitable for that request. Capability validation rejects unsupported mandatory constraints instead of silently ignoring them. A general-purpose database can unify transactional metadata and vectors; a specialist engine can offer different scaling and operational features. Compare the adapter's concrete guarantees rather than category labels. Create a decision matrix for persistence, filters, lexical search, transactions, generation visibility, and deletion latency for two candidate backends."},{"id":52,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"Why use PostgreSQL with pgvector and full-text search?","text":"Why use PostgreSQL with pgvector and full-text search? PostgreSQL can combine relational filters, vector distance, lexical ranking, and host-managed transactions in one database. This is attractive when the application already depends on PostgreSQL and wants retrieval to participate in existing data governance. The PostgreSQL vector adapter builds table-agnostic SQL from validated identifiers and uses database-specific search operations. The host supplies schema and sessions; the adapter does not turn every database into PostgreSQL or own every application migration. Database extension setup, indexes, pool sizing, and query plans still need operational care. The presence of pgvector does not prove that an approximate index is configured or used for a particular query. One database reduces cross-system coordination but couples workloads and capacity. A separate vector service isolates scaling while introducing another consistency and authorization boundary. Explain what an EXPLAIN plan would need to show before you claim a vector query uses an ANN index. Keep that distinct from the Python adapter's API."},{"id":53,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"What is ANN, and how is it different from exact nearest-neighbor search?","text":"What is ANN, and how is it different from exact nearest-neighbor search? Approximate Nearest Neighbor search trades guaranteed exact ranking for faster search over large vector collections. Exact search compares against all eligible vectors and provides a useful correctness baseline. Algorithms such as HNSW and IVF have different memory, tuning, and recall behavior. The retrieval service delegates vector operations to backends. An in-memory scan is useful for small controlled examples; a production backend may use an approximate index depending on its configuration and query plan. ANN is not universally forced by this library. ANN can miss relevant candidates before reranking ever sees them. Metadata filters and small candidate pools can interact with index behavior, so recall must be measured under actual constraints. Higher search effort usually improves recall and costs latency or memory. Exact search may be sufficient for small corpora or useful as an offline oracle. Compare approximate results with exact results on the same authorized subset. Plot recall versus latency while varying backend search effort."},{"id":54,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"Why are native lexical search and token-overlap fallback not equivalent?","text":"Why are native lexical search and token-overlap fallback not equivalent? PostgreSQL full-text search provides language configuration, query parsing, and ranking facilities. The shared fallback tokenizes lowercase ASCII alphanumeric sequences and scores query-token overlap. It is a lightweight approximation, not BM25 and not equivalent multilingual search. The scoring helper makes non-PostgreSQL backends usable in hybrid examples while capability declarations distinguish native and approximate lexical support. This preserves a common result shape without pretending the underlying ranking models are identical. Non-ASCII text and linguistic variation may behave poorly in the fallback. An evaluation that covers only English exact-word examples can miss this limitation. A portable fallback avoids another search service, while native full-text infrastructure generally offers richer lexical behavior. Choose according to corpus language and quality requirements. Tokenize an Arabic query and an English product code using the fallback. Inspect the actual tokens before interpreting any hybrid score."},{"id":55,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"How does the reference design prevent SQL injection in dynamic queries?","text":"How does the reference design prevent SQL injection in dynamic queries? SQL values use bound parameters, while table and column names require separate identifier validation because SQL drivers cannot bind identifiers as ordinary values. the retrieval service validates dynamic names before interpolation into backend SQL. The validation helper restricts identifiers to a simple allowlisted syntax. the PostgreSQL vector adapter applies validation when constructing operations and keeps values in parameter dictionaries. This is safer than escaping arbitrary user-provided SQL fragments. A valid identifier is not proof of authorization to access that table. Hosts must select trusted schema configuration and limit database permissions. Application transport validation also remains necessary. Strict names exclude qualified or unusual identifiers unless explicitly supported. That constraint simplifies safe SQL construction at the cost of some schema flexibility. Compare a malicious value, a malicious table name, and a syntactically valid but unauthorized table name. Explain which layer must stop each one."},{"id":56,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"What does session injection buy, and when does atomicity fail?","text":"What does session injection buy, and when does atomicity fail? Session injection lets several operations join a transaction owned by the host or pipeline. When version mutations and vector writes truly use the same transactional database session, rollback can preserve the previous active state if a write fails. The ingestion coordinator forwards the critical session into version storage and store upsert. Its guarantee depends on both adapters honoring that session. A remote vector service or in-memory store may not participate in the SQL transaction. Passing a session argument that an adapter ignores does not create cross-system atomicity. The sync path instead uses prepared generations and manifest visibility, with durable adapter obligations. Shared transactions simplify consistency within one database. Cross-service storage can scale independently but needs explicit visibility, retries, reconciliation, and failure recovery. Draw a rollback scenario for PostgreSQL-only storage and another for SQL versions plus a remote vector engine. Identify the write that cannot be rolled back in the second design."},{"id":57,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"How do Qdrant, Chroma, FAISS, and in-memory adapters differ operationally?","text":"How do Qdrant, Chroma, FAISS, and in-memory adapters differ operationally? They expose a common retrieval surface but use different storage and execution models. In-memory state is process-local. FAISS is an indexing library whose surrounding metadata and persistence need attention. Service-backed engines introduce network and server configuration. Adapter support, not brand identity, determines available guarantees. Each backend has its own implementation and optional dependency. The factory and capability layer let callers choose a backend while rejecting unsupported constraints instead of claiming complete interchangeability. Assuming identical durability, filter semantics, or transaction participation across these adapters can violate correctness. Inspect persistence and metadata behavior in the selected implementation and deployment. Lightweight local execution reduces setup; service-backed deployment supports different scale and operations needs. Portability often means accepting a common subset or explicitly branching on capabilities. Compare three public backend configurations. Record where vectors and metadata live, which constraints they enforce, and what survives a process restart."},{"id":58,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"Why are strict error semantics important for storage operations?","text":"Why are strict error semantics important for storage operations? A failed query and a successful query with zero matches mean different things. Lenient APIs may log an error and return an empty list or zero count; that is convenient for some callers but dangerous when the host interprets emptiness as authoritative. Retrieval supports strict mode and a typed retrieval error type. Mandatory capability and scope failures are designed to propagate rather than become silent fallbacks. Evidence assembly can represent backend failure distinctly when the host preserves that information. Forgetting strict mode can make an outage look like missing knowledge or an empty index. Health and deletion decisions should not infer success solely from an empty return value. Strict propagation requires explicit application error handling. Lenient fallback improves continuity only when the product can tolerate ambiguity and communicates it honestly. Make the backend raise on search and count. Compare strict and lenient behavior and design the corresponding UI states."},{"id":59,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"How should deletion differ from physical garbage collection?","text":"How should deletion differ from physical garbage collection? Logical deletion removes permission or visibility immediately; physical cleanup later reservice requests index storage. Separating the two prevents asynchronous vector deletion from remaining an access-control dependency. Sync manifests can tombstone documents, and a synchronous retrieval adapter validates status, generation, scope, and chunk membership before returning hits. Garbage collection removes retired or uncommitted vectors after visibility decisions. Calling the raw vector store bypasses manifest checks. A delayed physical deletion is safe only if every reader uses the required visibility boundary and source lookup also respects revocation. Manifest gating adds reads and coordination but permits conservative cleanup and safer recovery. Immediate physical deletion alone is simpler but can fail or lag. Leave a tombstoned document's vectors physically present. Verify that the supported sync retrieval path returns no evidence and explain why raw nearest-neighbor access must remain internal."},{"id":60,"slug":"storage","chapter":"Vector storage and database design","title":"What does a trustworthy backend capability contract look like?","text":"What does a trustworthy backend capability contract look like? It states supported filter operators and features such as document fetch, deletion, scope enforcement, generation visibility, and transaction guarantees. Unsupported mandatory requirements produce an error before returning potentially unsafe results. Store capability declarations validates both requested constraints and filter shapes. This makes backend selection a correctness decision rather than a best-effort call to methods that may silently ignore arguments. Declarations are assertions by adapter code. A falsely advertised capability is still a bug, and permissive host defaults can avoid requesting a guarantee that the product actually needs. Fail-closed behavior reduces surprising leakage but can reject configurations that previously appeared to work. That incompatibility is useful when the earlier behavior was unsafe or incomplete. Build an adapter that rejects range filters and transactions. Send requests requiring each feature and verify rejection occurs before any evidence is returned."},{"id":61,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"What happens inside a typed retrieval request?","text":"What happens inside a typed retrieval request? RetrievalRequest carries the query, candidate and final counts, threshold, filters, isolation scope, expected generation, metadata projection, session, and mandatory capabilities. It makes execution constraints explicit instead of hiding them in loosely interpreted keyword arguments. A hybrid retriever coerces legacy calls into this form and validates supported constraints before delegating to a store or strategy. Effective filters and required metadata help preserve scope checks across the execution path. A well-typed request can still contain an unauthorized scope if the host trusts the user's input. Authentication must establish which scope the caller is allowed to request. A richer request object improves composability and inspection but requires a stable public contract and deliberate defaults. A small positional API is easier for toy usage. Construct one request for tenant A with a larger candidate pool and a smaller final result set. Explain every field that affects authorization, ranking, or visibility."},{"id":62,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"Why separate candidate_k from final_k?","text":"Why separate candidate_k from final_k? Candidate_k determines how many initial hits later stages can inspect; final_k determines how many results the caller receives. A reranker cannot promote a relevant passage that the candidate stage never retrieved. Fetching only five candidates and returning five wastes much of the value of reranking. The strategy API carries both counts through retrieval and applies final slicing after optional reranking and scope validation. Evidence budgeting is yet another limit and should not be confused with either count. Very large candidate pools increase backend work, reranker cost, and exposure of material to external services. Candidates sent to a reranker must already respect applicable permissions. More candidates can improve recall with diminishing returns. Tune using recall, final ranking quality, and latency rather than assuming larger is always better. Compare candidate pools of 10, 30, and 100 while holding final_k at five. Track whether missing answers were absent from the pool or simply ranked too low."},{"id":63,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"How does Reciprocal Rank Fusion work here?","text":"How does Reciprocal Rank Fusion work here? RRF sums reciprocal rank contributions from independent result lists. This implementation uses 1/(60 + rank) with one-based ranks, then sorts by descending sum and breaks ties by document ID. It avoids directly adding incomparable raw distance and keyword scores. The shared rrf_merge helper constructs rank maps for vector and lexical lists. A document appearing in both gets two contributions; a document missing from a stage receives no contribution from that stage. RRF discards score magnitudes, so a near-tie and a decisive first-place win contribute identically at the same rank. Candidate truncation also limits which documents can benefit from fusion. Rank fusion is simple and robust across scoring scales, while weighted or learned fusion may capture more signal at the cost of calibration and training data. Calculate scores for a document ranked first in one stage and another ranked tenth in both. Verify the ordering with rrf_merge and inspect deterministic ties."},{"id":64,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"Are dense and lexical stages executed concurrently?","text":"Are dense and lexical stages executed concurrently? Dense and lexical search can run concurrently when they are independent and the backend supports the combined load. They can also run sequentially or inside one SQL statement. Choose deliberately and measure the actual execution path. Parallelize independent I/O with a bounded task group and preserve separate failure outcomes. A combined database query has its own query plan; Python-level concurrency cannot be inferred from its SQL shape. Assuming stage concurrency understates latency. Conversely, parallelizing blindly can violate a shared database session's rules or increase load beyond intended limits. Sequential execution is easier to reason about with one session. Parallel execution may reduce latency for independent backends but requires separate safe resources, cancellation, and error policy. Instrument start and finish events for both stages. Propose a parallel variant only after specifying which sessions and connections may be used concurrently."},{"id":65,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"When does a reranker help, and what can it not fix?","text":"When does a reranker help, and what can it not fix? A reranker reorders a candidate set using a more expensive relevance model, often evaluating the query and passage together. It helps distinguish closely related passages after a fast first-stage search. It cannot recover a document that never entered the candidate pool. Hybrid retrieval accepts an optional Reranker protocol. a model-backed reranker is one adapter, while a host can supply another implementation. Typed responses preserve richer score information where required. External reranking may transmit document content to another provider, add latency, or fail. Authorization and data-handling policy must apply before transmission, and timeout or failure behavior should be explicit. Reranking spends more per candidate to improve final ordering. It may be unnecessary for exact identifier queries or small corpora with strong lexical matches. Inspect ten candidates with similar vocabulary but different policy conditions. Compare fused ordering with reranked ordering and classify whether improvement justifies added cost."},{"id":66,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"How should query rewriting and multi-query retrieval be bounded?","text":"How should query rewriting and multi-query retrieval be bounded? Query rewriting can make a conversational or ambiguous question more searchable; multi-query retrieval explores alternative phrasings. Both add work and can drift away from the original intent. Bounds should cover variant count, text length, time, and downstream calls. A bounded query transformer wraps a host-provided transformer with limits and a timeout. Query generation should be an explicit optional dependency with measured cost and failure behavior. Generated variants may invent identifiers or broaden the scope of meaning. Scope filters must remain fixed across variants, and deduplication does not by itself calibrate rankings across all variants. Expansion can improve recall for difficult language while multiplying embedding and search cost. Start with a baseline and add it only for demonstrated failures. Supply a transformer returning duplicates, blank strings, too many variants, and a slow response. Explain which limits protect cost and which protect meaning."},{"id":67,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"How does multi-query merging differ from a fully calibrated global ranker?","text":"How does multi-query merging differ from a fully calibrated global ranker? The generic hybrid strategy fuses each query variant's dense and lexical results, extends a candidate list, and deduplicates by ID in first-seen order before optional reranking. That is not a learned global calibration of all variant scores. The implementation is a bounded composition mechanism. Its optional reranker is the natural place to compare the combined candidates against the original question, but absent that stage the ordering reflects implementation choices. The first query variant can dominate which duplicate representation and ordering survive. More variants therefore need not improve final ranking and can increase noise. First-seen deduplication is simple and deterministic. A second fusion across variants or a global reranker adds complexity and should be justified by evaluation. Reverse the order of two transformer variants and compare final results without a reranker. Explain any changed ordering using the execution policy."},{"id":68,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"Why must filters be applied during each retrieval stage?","text":"Why must filters be applied during each retrieval stage? If unauthorized documents consume the initial top-k pool, post-filtering can leave too few permitted results and may send forbidden text to later components. Applying constraints inside dense and lexical stages improves both isolation and eligible-candidate recall. Strategies pass effective filters to each backend, validate filter support, and run scope defense checks before returning results. These checks complement authorization at the transport or host boundary. A backend that ignores a filter can still leak data. Defense in depth detects some invalid results, but tests must also ensure a reranker or external processor does not receive unauthorized candidates before that final check. Filter-aware search can affect query plans and approximate recall. Security requirements take precedence; tune backend indexes rather than dropping constraints for speed. Place many high-similarity forbidden records ahead of a permitted record. Verify eligible recall and inspect exactly which records reach the reranker."},{"id":69,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"How should I interpret distance thresholds and relevance scores?","text":"How should I interpret distance thresholds and relevance scores? Distance is a geometric measurement, not a truth probability. RRF is a rank-fusion score, not a calibrated confidence. Reranker scores have provider-specific meaning. A distance threshold must match the selected metric and embedding profile. Result types preserve distance, keyword score, and fusion or reranker scores separately rather than pretending they share a universal numerical scale. The retriever can apply a configured threshold and explicit final result limit. Displaying 0.92 as 92 percent correct misleads users. A low distance can retrieve an obsolete, unauthorized, or merely related passage; those require separate checks. Thresholding removes weak candidates but can reduce coverage and varies across corpus changes. Product confidence should be evaluated empirically using multiple signals and abstention policy. Collect score distributions for answerable and unanswerable questions. Evaluate a threshold's false acceptance and false rejection rates before choosing a UI label."},{"id":70,"slug":"retrieval","chapter":"Retrieval, fusion, and reranking","title":"How would you debug a relevant document missing from the final answer?","text":"How would you debug a relevant document missing from the final answer? Walk the chain: was the source discovered, extracted, indexed under the active profile and generation, eligible under filters, retrieved into either candidate list, retained by fusion, ranked high enough, selected into evidence, and actually used by generation? Each step can independently remove useful information. The retrieval service exposes distinct modules and result shapes for these stages. That separation supports inspection of intermediate artifacts rather than guessing that every wrong answer is an embedding problem. Debug logging can expose queries or private passages. Prefer controlled fixtures and sanitized IDs, and do not bypass scope checks simply to make a document appear during investigation. Stage-by-stage diagnosis takes longer than tweaking a single constant but targets the actual cause and prevents compensating errors. Create a failure report containing the first stage where an expected passage disappears, the supporting observation, and one focused change to test."},{"id":71,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"Why does evidence assembly deserve a separate module?","text":"Why does evidence assembly deserve a separate module? Search ranking and prompt construction optimize different things. Ranking selects likely relevant hits; assembly must remove redundancy, preserve source identity, fit a budget, and communicate whether usable evidence exists. A list of raw top-k records does not meet all those needs. An evidence assembler coerces supported hit shapes, deduplicates, merges eligible spans, balances source diversity, selects items, and renders stable citation labels. This deterministic layer can be tested independently of an unpredictable generator. Assuming all retrieved text is safe prompt instruction invites source-driven manipulation. The bundle treats text as evidence and escapes selected delimiters, while host prompt policy and tool permissions remain necessary. An extra layer adds types and decisions but centralizes budgeting and attribution. Ad hoc prompt concatenation is shorter and easier to get subtly wrong. Feed duplicate and overlapping hits from two sources into the assembler. Inspect both selected text and the counters explaining what was removed or merged."},{"id":72,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"How is the context budget calculated?","text":"How is the context budget calculated? Available evidence capacity equals total budget minus reserved instructions, user input, and answer tokens, floored at zero. The assembler must account for rendered framing and citation text, not only passage body tokens. A positive capacity does not imply that even one candidate will fit. A context budget exposes reserved_total, available_evidence_tokens, and is_valid. Assembly checks the rendered form as items are added and returns insufficient_budget when no candidate fits. Reserving too little for generation can cause provider truncation; counting with an approximate tokenizer cannot guarantee an actual model limit. The host must include its complete message framing and any tool schema costs. Larger answer reservations reduce evidence capacity. Larger evidence budgets increase cost and can include irrelevant or distracting material. For a 16,000-token window, reserve 2,000 instructions, 1,000 user input, and 3,000 answer tokens. Compute evidence room, then explain what additional host framing must be counted."},{"id":73,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"Why is an approximate token estimator not a strict context guarantee?","text":"Why is an approximate token estimator not a strict context guarantee? It estimates from characters and whitespace-separated words. Actual model tokenization depends on its vocabulary and encoding, so a heuristic can undercount some inputs even when intended to be conservative. The class explicitly marks is_exact as false. The retrieval service offers an exact token-counting adapter for a model-specific counter. That adapter is only as exact as the supplied counting function and the host's representation of the final serialized request. A function labeled exact but counting the wrong model or omitting message wrappers still gives unsafe limits. Multilingual text, code, and unusual characters deserve explicit verification. Approximation keeps core dependency-free and supports offline use. Exact counting adds a tokenizer dependency or integration step and stronger budget fidelity. Compare heuristic and actual counts for prose, code, Arabic, and long identifiers. Explain why agreement on one example does not establish a global upper bound."},{"id":74,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"How do deduplication and span merging differ?","text":"How do deduplication and span merging differ? Deduplication removes repeated hits representing the same chunk. Span merging combines compatible overlapping or contiguous material from the same source context, preserving a larger coherent excerpt. They solve redundancy at different identity and text-boundary levels. An evidence assembler has separate _deduplicate and _merge_spans stages and records counts. Proper merging depends on source and revision information plus usable offsets; arbitrary neighboring ranked hits are not automatically one passage. Merging across revisions or unrelated documents can fabricate a source excerpt that never existed. Missing or inconsistent offsets should not be guessed into a false provenance claim. Merging can restore context and save repeated framing, but may create a large item that fails the budget. Keeping chunks separate provides finer selection at the cost of repetition. Construct overlapping spans from the same document revision and one from a different revision. Predict which may merge and verify the emitted provenance."},{"id":75,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"Why balance evidence across sources?","text":"Why balance evidence across sources? A single document can occupy the candidate pool with many similar chunks. Source diversity can expose corroborating or contrasting material and reduce redundant context. It is a selection heuristic, not a guarantee that independent sources agree or are trustworthy. The assembler ranks within sources and interleaves them before budget selection. This can preserve diversity even when a long source produces many high-scoring chunks. Diversity may demote the only authoritative source in favor of weaker material. Source quality and authority are product knowledge rules that may need explicit weighting or filtering outside the default heuristic. Pure relevance order maximizes one ranking signal; diversity spreads context coverage. Evaluate questions requiring one detailed source separately from questions requiring synthesis across sources. Give one source eight high-ranked chunks and another one relevant chunk. Compare evidence selection with the product's expected authority policy."},{"id":76,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"What failure states should an evidence consumer handle?","text":"What failure states should an evidence consumer handle? An evidence-status type includes success, empty_search, backend_failure, insufficient_budget, stale_index, and revision_unavailable. These represent different next actions: answer, clarify, retry, change capacity, refresh indexing, or explain that a historical source cannot be retrieved. The assembler explicitly returns backend and stale-index states rather than assembling ungrounded material. Revision-aware lookup raises a distinct error instead of silently substituting the example document. Flattening every state into no answer hides outages and can trigger unsafe generation fallbacks. Success also means usable selected evidence, not proof of a correct final answer. Rich states require more UI and API branches but let users respond appropriately. A single boolean is simpler and loses important operational meaning. Write user-facing messages for each status and specify which states may be retried automatically without changing the user's question."},{"id":77,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"What makes a citation reproducible?","text":"What makes a citation reproducible? A reproducible citation identifies a stable source, document, revision, and bounded location or span. A URL pointing only to the latest page may no longer support an old answer after an update. Display labels such as [1] are local presentation, not durable source identities. Revision-aware source lookup retrieves retained snapshots and rejects unavailable revisions. Evidence items carry revision and span fields, while source-reference helpers encode identifiers for later verification. Historical snapshots can contain sensitive material even after current content changes. Retention and revocation knowledge rules must apply to lookup as well as search, and a host must provide durable snapshot storage when needed. Retention improves auditability and costs storage and policy management. Current-only lookup is lighter but must admit when a historical request cannot be verified. Cite revision A, update the source to B, and attempt lookup. Verify that A is returned if retained and that missing A never silently becomes B."},{"id":78,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"What does delimiter escaping mitigate, and what does it leave open?","text":"What does delimiter escaping mitigate, and what does it leave open? Escaping selected instruction-like tags and code fences reduces the chance that source text visually or syntactically breaks out of an evidence wrapper. It does not recognize every malicious natural-language instruction or make a model incapable of following one. Escape_untrusted_delimiters replaces known markers in evidence rendering, and URI sanitization removes embedded credentials or disallowed active schemes. These are narrow input-handling mechanisms within a larger trust model. A passage can say ignore previous instructions without using any special delimiter. Host instructions must classify evidence as untrusted data, and tool permissions must prevent source text from gaining authority. Escaping preserves useful text with modest complexity but can alter literal code examples. More aggressive sanitization may damage legitimate content and still cannot solve all prompt injection. Compare a tag-based injection and a plain-language injection. Explain why both should remain untrusted even if only one changes during escaping."},{"id":79,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"How can evidence compression preserve usefulness without fabricating support?","text":"How can evidence compression preserve usefulness without fabricating support? Compression shortens selected material to fit a budget. Extractive compression selects original spans; abstractive compression generates a shorter restatement. The latter can change meaning or omit conditions, so provenance and faithfulness require explicit checks. An evidence assembler accepts an optional compressor, passes a copy of the item, checks selected provenance fields, recounts the rendered result, and falls back when compression fails. No universal learned compressor is installed by default. Preserving source ID and revision does not prove the compressed words accurately represent the source. The host must evaluate the compressor and decide whether the compressed text is quoteable or should be labeled as a summary. Compression increases context coverage but adds cost and potential distortion. Dropping a low-value item is often safer than aggressively rewriting essential evidence. Compress a rule containing an exception. Verify both metadata invariance and semantic preservation; show a failure that passes metadata checks but changes the rule."},{"id":80,"slug":"evidence","chapter":"Evidence, context, and citations","title":"How should the host turn evidence into a safe answer policy?","text":"How should the host turn evidence into a safe answer policy? The host should specify when to answer, how to cite, when to ask for clarification, and when to abstain. It should pass authorized evidence as data, preserve status signals, and check that important service requests are supported. This policy is part of the application, not an automatic property of the retrieval service. The bridge returns a structured EvidenceBundle that a generator or source-viewing UI can consume. Its explicit status and attribution fields make a disciplined policy feasible without binding the subsystem to one chat model. A host that discards bundle status, regenerates citation identifiers, or allows source text to choose tools can undermine the safeguards upstream. Streaming service requests before evidence selection is settled creates another verification gap. Strict evidence-only answering improves traceability but can feel less helpful for general questions. Clearly separated modes can preserve user expectations. Draft a generation policy for answerable, ambiguous, empty, stale, and failed retrieval cases. Create one acceptance example for each branch."},{"id":81,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"What are the phases of the ingestion workflow?","text":"What are the phases of the ingestion workflow? The pipeline loads source documents, verifies load authority, computes a snapshot, checks whether it is unchanged, chunks, embeds, and validates. It then enters a critical session to recheck the snapshot, retire the old version, create a new version, and upsert chunks. Slow provider work precedes the mutation-critical section. This reduces the time an internally owned session and advisory lock are held for version changes. The read-only initial snapshot check uses a short session when the caller did not supply one. A caller-supplied session may already have an open transaction throughout preparation. The library cannot undo that host decision, so do not claim remote calls are outside every possible transaction in all integrations. Preparation can be duplicated by concurrent workers, but database contention is lower. Serializing all work under a lock saves duplicate embeddings while holding scarce resources longer. Instrument session entry, embedding start, lock acquisition, upsert, and commit. Compare internally owned and caller-owned session paths."},{"id":82,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"Why is there a second snapshot check under the lock?","text":"Why is there a second snapshot check under the lock? Two workers can both read an old snapshot, prepare identical new vectors, and then reach the write phase. The second check detects that another worker already activated the intended snapshot and avoids duplicate version mutation. _commit_ingestion reads the active version again before retiring anything. This is double-checked coordination: the early check avoids unnecessary work in the common case; the protected check handles races during preparation. The recheck is only serialized when the relevant locking and transaction semantics are actually enforced. Without a supplied lock key or equivalent host coordination, concurrent writes may still race. A second read costs a small amount of database work and does not prevent duplicate provider calls. It protects the authoritative mutation rather than promising exactly-once preparation. Pause two workers after their initial checks, let one commit, then release the second. Verify only one new active version appears when the lock contract is honored."},{"id":83,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"What belongs in an ingestion snapshot?","text":"What belongs in an ingestion snapshot? The snapshot should represent the inputs that determine indexed content: source manifest hash, extractor behavior, chunker identity and configuration, and embedding configuration or profile. A change to any of these can make existing vectors stale despite unchanged source text. Read the active revision again inside the protected activation transaction. If it changed while preparation ran, compare the new state and either discard redundant work or retry under a defined conflict policy. Omitting hidden preprocessing or a model revision can cause false unchanged matches. Including unstable or secret-bearing fields can trigger needless rebuilds or expose credentials. Comprehensive snapshots improve repeatability while requiring disciplined adapter metadata. A simple content hash is easier but cannot detect pipeline changes. List every transformation between source bytes and stored vectors in your host. Identify which are represented in the snapshot and add a documented version for any custom transformation."},{"id":84,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"What is idempotency, and what is idempotent in a reference design?","text":"What is idempotency, and what is idempotent in a reference design? An idempotent operation can be repeated without producing additional logical effects after the intended state is reached. Ingestion uses snapshot comparison to skip unchanged work and rechecks before mutation. That does not mean every low-level call executes once or always reuses identical physical chunk IDs. Serialize a stable fingerprint from source revision, extraction and chunking versions, model profile, and indexing policy. Compare fingerprints only after defining which changes require a rebuild. Billing side effects, external writes, or custom version-store behavior may not be idempotent. The host must use stable operation identity and ensure repeated processing does not create duplicate application effects. Logical idempotency enables robust retries at the cost of hashes, state checks, and identity tracking. Exactly-once execution across external services is a much stronger claim. Run unchanged ingestion twice and count provider calls, active versions, and stored chunks. Then repeat after a forced failure immediately before commit."},{"id":85,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"Who commits a caller-supplied database session?","text":"Who commits a caller-supplied database session? The caller owns a supplied session and its transaction. The pipeline yields it into the critical section but does not commit it. When the pipeline opens its own session, it commits after successful critical work. This ownership rule prevents a library from unexpectedly committing unrelated host changes. _transacting_session implements the distinction and can acquire the advisory lock on a caller session when a lock key is supplied. The lock lifetime still follows the actual database and transaction behavior. A host that forgets to commit loses intended changes; a host that keeps the transaction open across slow preparation can retain connections and locks too long. Session ownership must be clear at integration boundaries. Caller ownership supports composing RAG writes with business operations, while library ownership simplifies isolated ingestion. Mixing the models implicitly is error-prone. Add an unrelated host row before calling ingestion with the same session. Roll back and verify whether both mutations obey the host's intended transaction."},{"id":86,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"What does an advisory lock protect, and what does it not protect?","text":"What does an advisory lock protect, and what does it not protect? An advisory lock coordinates cooperating processes using the same lock key and database locking mechanism. It can serialize the mutation phase for one source. It does not force unrelated code paths to honor the lock or create a distributed transaction with an external vector service. The pipeline obtains the lock through a SQL adapter interface when lock_key is supplied. Lock-key derivation and consistent source scope are therefore part of the host's correctness contract. Inconsistent keys allow concurrent writers to bypass each other; overly broad keys serialize unrelated sources. A lock also does not guarantee that the shortest possible transaction duration has been measured. Fine-grained keys improve throughput while increasing key-management complexity. A global ingestion lock is simple but can become a bottleneck. Define lock keys for two tenants sharing a source name. Explain why including or excluding tenant scope changes both correctness and contention."},{"id":87,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"How should authoritative emptiness differ from extraction failure?","text":"How should authoritative emptiness differ from extraction failure? An authoritative empty dataset can mean that previously indexed content has been removed. A failed or incomplete read means the source state is unknown. The pipeline defaults empty input to a no-op and requires purge_on_empty to retire current content deliberately. Non-authoritative a typed scan result is rejected before mutation. For a valid empty source, the purge path closes the active version and deletes its chunks inside the transacting session. Enabling purge for an adapter that reports failures as an ordinary empty list can destroy visibility of healthy content. Returning zero chunks is not enough information to decide deletion safely. Conservative defaults retain old knowledge during uncertainty but need a separate deletion and revocation policy. Aggressive replacement is simpler and more dangerous during source outages. Test a genuine empty source, an inaccessible source, and a parser returning no chunks from nonempty input. Explain why each path should have different effects."},{"id":88,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"Why abort when nonempty documents produce zero chunks?","text":"Why abort when nonempty documents produce zero chunks? Recording a new active snapshot with no useful chunks could make later runs classify the same source as already indexed and skip it indefinitely. Aborting before version mutation preserves the previous active content and allows a later retry after the chunker or extraction issue is fixed. The pipeline explicitly checks for no chunks before embedding and before entering the critical section. This is a small guard protecting a large lifecycle invariant: an active snapshot should correspond to successfully prepared content. A host may misinterpret the returned zero as successful deletion or successful replacement. Diagnostics and status handling should distinguish aborted preparation from an authoritative empty purge. Retaining old content favors availability while freshness is unresolved. Some products should additionally mark it stale instead of silently treating it as current. Inject a chunker that returns an empty list for a nonempty source. Verify the old version remains active and that a subsequent valid chunker can ingest it."},{"id":89,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"How does rollback interact with old-version retirement and new-vector upsert?","text":"How does rollback interact with old-version retirement and new-vector upsert? The critical sequence retires the active version, deletes its chunks, creates the new version, stamps metadata, and upserts new chunks. With a shared transactional backend, a failure should roll all of that back. With nontransactional collaborators, this sequence alone cannot guarantee atomic replacement. Passing one session is the mechanism for joining transactional writes. In-memory or remote adapters require their own consistency design; the manifest-based sync system addresses a different visibility model. Deleting old content before a remote upsert fails can leave an unavailable or inconsistent index. Do not assume a Python context manager can roll back writes in an external service. In-place transactional replacement is compact in one database. Generation preparation and activation use more storage and state but better fit cross-system operations. Inject an upsert failure after retirement. Test actual visibility and active-version state with your production adapter combination, not only a recording fake."},{"id":90,"slug":"ingestion","chapter":"Ingestion and transaction ownership","title":"Why are fixed claims such as microsecond swaps or sub-5-ms commits unreliable?","text":"Why are fixed claims such as microsecond swaps or sub-5-ms commits unreliable? The critical section includes reads, version mutations, deletion, and upsert, not merely a pointer assignment. Its duration depends on chunk count, database load, indexes, network latency, and adapter behavior. No timing benchmark in an illustrative implementation proves a universal duration. Moving remote embedding out of internally owned mutation sessions is a sound architectural mechanism. That mechanism supports a narrower claim about resource occupancy, not an invented service-level objective. Designing connection pools or latency budgets around fictional timings can cause overload. Measure lock wait, transaction duration, upsert volume, and retry behavior under representative workloads. Shorter transactions reduce contention but preparation may still dominate end-to-end ingestion time. Optimization should target measured bottlenecks without weakening atomicity. Benchmark the critical section across increasing chunk counts. Report distributions and test conditions, and keep provider time separate from database time."},{"id":91,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"Why does sync need more than the basic ingestion pipeline?","text":"Why does sync need more than the basic ingestion pipeline? Sync reconciles a changing collection over repeated scans and distributed workers. It must classify document changes, track active generations, prevent stale activation, manage removals, checkpoint progress, and clean abandoned writes. A one-shot replace operation does not cover all of those concerns. A sync coordinator composes source scans, manifests, fencing state, embeddings, vector storage, optional reconciliation jobs, and garbage collection. It prepares generations before committing visibility through manifests. The coordinator's logic depends on durable adapters implementing atomic fencing and batch compare-and-swap. The provided in-memory implementations demonstrate semantics within a process, not a production multi-process coordination service. The richer protocol improves recoverability while adding state and adapter obligations. Small single-process applications may reasonably use the simpler pipeline. Compare the state required to replace one file with the state required to sync a million-object collection across two workers and intermittent source failures."},{"id":92,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"What are leases and fencing tokens, and why are both needed?","text":"What are leases and fencing tokens, and why are both needed? A lease grants temporary ownership; a fencing token is a monotonically increasing epoch that lets authoritative storage reject obsolete owners. A worker can pause beyond its lease and later resume, so merely checking lease time before a write is insufficient. A sync-state repository provides lease acquisition, renewal, token validation, and a fenced commit context. The coordinator validates tokens after preparation and performs activation inside the fenced context. A stale worker may already have written prepared vectors, but it must not activate them or overwrite checkpoints. Correctness depends on checking authority at the authoritative mutation boundary, not only in worker memory. Fencing requires shared durable state and atomic enforcement. A simple expiring lock is easier to implement but vulnerable to resumed zombie workers. Pause worker A, expire its lease, let B acquire a newer epoch, then resume A. Identify every operation A may still perform and every authoritative write that must reject it."},{"id":93,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"Why does a separate token check followed by a write still race?","text":"Why does a separate token check followed by a write still race? Another worker can take ownership after the check and before the write. This is a time-of-check to time-of-use race. The authority check and authoritative mutation need one protected context or equivalent atomic database condition. The a sync-state repository protocol explicitly requires fenced() to exclude takeover while committing. A production adapter can use transactional row locking, advisory locking, or equivalent compare-and-swap semantics; a no-op context is not sufficient. Implementing validate_token correctly but fenced incorrectly creates false confidence. Unit tests that never interleave takeover with commit will miss the race. Holding a guard during activation can increase contention. Keep expensive embedding outside it and protect only the necessary visibility and checkpoint operations. Write an interleaving with a successful token check, a takeover, and an obsolete write. Then show which atomic guard makes that history impossible."},{"id":94,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"What does compare-and-swap do for manifest activation?","text":"What does compare-and-swap do for manifest activation? Compare-and-swap updates state only if its current version matches the expected version. It detects that another operation changed the document while new content was being prepared. Batch compare-and-swap applies the intended set without a partially updated manifest snapshot. The coordinator records expected fences, prepares vectors, and calls compare_and_swap_many during activation. Readers use a manifest snapshot to validate which generation and chunk IDs are visible. A loop of individually successful per-document swaps is not equivalent to an atomic batch when collection consistency is required. Adapter contracts must make the promised visibility semantics real. Batch atomicity simplifies reader consistency while placing stronger demands on durable storage. Per-document activation permits partial progress with weaker whole-collection simultaneity. Simulate two prepared updates where one expected fence is stale. Verify that a batch failure does not leave only the other document activated."},{"id":95,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"How do document and collection activation modes differ?","text":"How do document and collection activation modes differ? Document mode can activate successful document updates even when another document fails preparation. Collection mode aims to avoid publishing a partially prepared collection and advances collection-generation state after activation. They express different consistency and availability choices. The coordinator's preparation loop records errors; collection mode raises on preparation failure, while document mode can continue. The activation path updates manifests and collection state under the supplied coordination contracts. Cross-store atomicity is not created automatically by selecting collection mode. Durable adapters must preserve the fenced and batch visibility obligations, including failure between manifest commit and generation advancement. Collection consistency can delay fresh data because one bad document blocks a run. Document mode improves partial availability while exposing mixed document update times. Make one of three documents fail embedding. Compare reports and visible generations in both modes and state which behavior your product requires."},{"id":96,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"How do metadata-only changes avoid unnecessary re-embedding?","text":"How do metadata-only changes avoid unnecessary re-embedding? If content and embedding-relevant processing remain unchanged, a metadata update can reuse stored vectors while writing records with new metadata. This saves provider work without pretending that all changes are harmless to vector identity. Sync plans distinguish metadata_only updates. The coordinator fetches existing embeddings by active chunk IDs and passes them into preparation instead of calling the embedder again for that case. Misclassifying a semantic preprocessing change as metadata-only reuses invalid vectors. Missing stored vectors also need an explicit failure path rather than silently substituting arbitrary values. This optimization reduces cost but requires accurate fingerprints and a fetch-capable backend. Re-embedding everything is simpler and more expensive. Change a display title, then change chunker configuration, then change an access field. Explain which changes can reuse vectors and which still require immediate authorization updates."},{"id":97,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"How does a synchronous retrieval adapter protect against stale and tombstoned vectors?","text":"How does a synchronous retrieval adapter protect against stale and tombstoned vectors? It asks the backend for scoped active candidates, oversamples, reads a manifest snapshot, and rejects results whose document is absent or non-active, whose generation differs, or whose chunk ID is not in the active manifest. Physical presence is not sufficient for visibility. This read-time gate decouples authorization and active-state checks from eventual vector cleanup. It also checks returned metadata rather than trusting that every backend filter was honored perfectly. Its finite oversampling can still return fewer than the requested number of valid hits. Also, callers using the raw store bypass this protection, so integration must preserve the intended reader path. Additional manifest reads and filtering improve consistency while adding latency and possible underfilling. A stronger backend-native visibility index could reduce that overhead but needs equivalent guarantees. Insert old-generation, tombstoned, wrong-scope, and valid records. Verify which survive and show a case where oversampling still cannot fill final_k."},{"id":98,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"What is the transactional outbox pattern, and what must the host implement?","text":"What is the transactional outbox pattern, and what must the host implement? The outbox writes a job in the same transaction as the application change it represents. A separate worker later processes that job. This prevents a committed business change from losing its intended background work because a process crashed before sending an external message. A durable job repository and a job processor are host-implemented contracts. enqueue behavior with a supplied session must join the caller's transaction; protocol documentation describes safe claiming, including SKIP LOCKED semantics for a SQL implementation. A separate job insert after commit is not a transactional outbox. Likewise, the sync coordinator's optional reconciliation enqueue should not automatically be described as atomic with every remote vector write. An outbox adds polling, retries, and retention but avoids fragile dual writes. It still requires idempotent processing because a crash can cause a job to run again. Draw crashes before business commit, after commit, and after processing but before completion marking. Explain how an outbox avoids lost work while permitting duplicate processing."},{"id":99,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"Does SKIP LOCKED provide exactly-once job execution?","text":"Does SKIP LOCKED provide exactly-once job execution? No. It helps concurrent SQL workers claim different available rows without waiting on already locked rows. A worker can still finish external work and crash before recording completion, causing a later retry to repeat that work. The job system tracks status, attempts, lock ownership, and stale-job reclamation. These mechanisms support recovery, while the processor must ensure repeated execution has safe logical effects. Reclaiming too early can overlap a still-running worker; never reclaiming can strand jobs forever. Production adapters must enforce ownership on completion and failure transitions, not just during initial selection. At-least-once processing is practical with idempotent writes and fencing. Claiming exactly-once behavior across unrelated external services would require a much stronger design. Make the processor succeed, then simulate failure in completion marking. Run reclamation and show that the second execution does not duplicate the application's intended effect."},{"id":100,"slug":"coordination","chapter":"Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery","title":"How do shutdown, retries, checkpoints, and garbage collection work together?","text":"How do shutdown, retries, checkpoints, and garbage collection work together? The drainer checks for stale jobs, processes pending work, survives failed passes, and uses a stop event for shutdown. Sync cancels heartbeats, releases leases, and cleans retired or uncommitted vectors in a finally path. Checkpoints record committed progress under the example token. These are recovery mechanisms for different lifetimes: process shutdown, task execution, authority ownership, and storage cleanup. A completion report alone cannot replace all of them. Cleanup can itself fail, and process termination may prevent finally blocks from running. Durable reconciliation must discover leftovers on a later run; a successful logical activation should not be undone by deleting committed IDs as abandoned work. Conservative cleanup leaves temporary storage overhead while protecting visible data. Aggressive deletion reduces space and can make recovery unsafe. Build a crash matrix for preparation, vector write, manifest activation, checkpoint, and cleanup. For each point identify visible state and the next repair action."},{"id":101,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"How are authentication, authorization, and retrieval filtering different?","text":"How are authentication, authorization, and retrieval filtering different? Authentication establishes who is calling. Authorization decides what that identity may access or do. Retrieval filtering constrains the records searched and returned. A tenant_id filter supplied by an untrusted caller is not authentication or authorization. The tool bridge composes scope authorization with retrieval and source lookup. MCP identity can come from trusted transport context or a host callback rather than a model-supplied client identifier. Allowing a request to choose both its identity and scope creates an impersonation path. The host must establish trusted identity and map it to allowed collections before issuing constrained retrieval. Central authorization adds integration work but prevents duplicated, inconsistent policy across tools. Filters remain necessary for efficient authorized search even after permission is granted. Draw an API request with identity, requested collection, and requested scope. Mark which values are trusted and where each becomes constrained."},{"id":102,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"Are the remote transport defaults production-secure by themselves?","text":"Are the remote transport defaults production-secure by themselves? Development defaults are not evidence of production security. A remotely reachable service needs verified authentication, resource authorization, explicit allowed origins, request limits, safe outbound access, and a reviewed secret policy. Fail closed for protected capabilities when identity or policy configuration is absent. Loopback binding reduces development exposure; remote deployment introduces a different trust boundary. Deploying with permissive defaults can make private knowledge broadly accessible. CORS also does not authenticate non-browser callers; restricting it alone is insufficient. Permissive local defaults simplify experimentation. Explicit validators, deny-by-default scope mappings, bounded origins, TLS, and deployment policy require more setup and give a meaningful remote trust boundary. Construct a configuration that rejects missing tokens and unknown clients. Test an authenticated user requesting another tenant's collection as well as an unauthenticated caller."},{"id":103,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"What should the knowledge-service adapter centralize?","text":"What should the knowledge-service adapter centralize? It provides one framework-neutral boundary for search, bounded evidence, source lookup, collection selection, and health or management operations. Transport adapters should translate requests into that boundary rather than each rebuilding retrieval and permission logic. Collection configuration supplies retriever and source-lookup configuration; a knowledge-service adapter composes authorizer, assembler, and health checker. This resembles a facade or application-service boundary over the lower-level components. A central facade is only protective if all exposed routes use it correctly. Direct backend endpoints or source lookup shortcuts can bypass the common policy. Centralization reduces duplicated integration logic but can become overly broad if every domain behavior is added to it. Keep application-specific decisions in the host. Compare a chat endpoint and an agent tool endpoint. Identify which validation and authorization behavior should be shared through the bridge and which presentation behavior should differ."},{"id":104,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"What is MCP, and what does a retrieval adapter expose?","text":"What is MCP, and what does a retrieval adapter expose? Model Context Protocol is a protocol for exposing tools and resources to model-enabled clients. the retrieval service's MCP adapter wraps the bridge with read tools such as search_knowledge, get_source, and knowledge_status. Management operations require explicit enabling conditions. The adapter keeps protocol concerns outside retrieval logic and obtains identity through trusted integration hooks. It supports SDK variation through an adapter layer rather than making the core depend on MCP. A protocol does not make a tool inherently authorized or safe. A model can request an operation, but the server must enforce permissions, budgets, and management policy independently of the model's wording. Standardized tool access broadens integration while introducing another exposed surface and dependency. A simple host API may be sufficient for one application. Inspect the registered tools and their input schemas. Explain why a tool description is useful for model behavior but cannot enforce a security rule."},{"id":105,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"Why are read and management tools separate opt-ins?","text":"Why are read and management tools separate opt-ins? Searching knowledge and changing or revoking knowledge have different consequences. A client that may read a collection should not automatically be able to ingest arbitrary sources, trigger expensive sync, or remove access for other users. The MCP server defaults to read tools, while management registration and the bridge's allow_management setting provide separate opt-in controls. Those controls complement, rather than replace, identity-based authorization. Enabling a management tool globally without per-caller policy can give an agent excessive authority. Source text must never grant that authority by instructing the model to call a privileged tool. Separate permissions and configuration increase setup but support least privilege. A single unrestricted tool surface is simpler and broadens both accidental and malicious impact. Define reader, operator, and administrator roles. Map each to search, source lookup, sync, and revocation, then state where the host enforces each permission."},{"id":106,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"Why is prompt injection an authorization problem as well as a prompting problem?","text":"Why is prompt injection an authorization problem as well as a prompting problem? Retrieved documents may contain instructions aimed at the model rather than information for the user. If the model can turn those instructions into privileged actions, untrusted content has crossed an authority boundary. Prompt wording alone should not determine which actions are permitted. Evidence rendering escapes some markers, bridge operations apply authorization, and management tools are opt-in. These controls address different layers: presentation, data access, and action authority. Natural-language attacks can survive escaping, and a model can misinterpret legitimate text. Server-side tool validation and least privilege must remain effective even when the model requests the wrong action. Tighter permissions can limit autonomous workflows. Grant narrow capabilities for a task rather than broad access based on assumed model compliance. Put a malicious instruction in a test document asking for another tenant's source. Verify the server refuses the operation even if a simulated model follows the instruction exactly."},{"id":107,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"Why must source lookup enforce authorization separately from search?","text":"Why must source lookup enforce authorization separately from search? A citation reference can be copied, guessed, or reused after access changes. Possession of an identifier is not proof that the caller may read the referenced document. Historical snapshots can also retain text no longer visible in current search. Revision-aware source lookup supports scoped access, revocation, bounded spans, and explicit revision availability. The bridge authorizes source retrieval rather than treating a citation label as an unrestricted download token. Protecting search while exposing an unguarded get_source endpoint leaks the same underlying corpus. Retention policy must distinguish audit storage from permission to disclose that storage. Rechecking permissions adds work to every citation open, but preserves access changes over time. Caching source responses also needs scope and revocation-aware keys. Obtain a valid citation, revoke access, then try opening it again. Verify both search and retained-source lookup reject disclosure."},{"id":108,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"What does the built-in rate limiter control, and what does it not control?","text":"What does the built-in rate limiter control, and what does it not control? It tracks request timestamps and active counts per client within one process, enforcing a sliding-window request ceiling and concurrency limit. It is not a shared quota service across many replicas and does not automatically price embedding or generation tokens. RateLimiter uses in-memory dictionaries and an asyncio lock. Remote transport configuration can inject it as one guard around tool work, while embedding execution has a different semaphore for provider calls. Multiple replicas multiply an apparent per-client limit unless they coordinate. Failed release logic can also strand active slots, so acquire and release must be paired even on exceptions and cancellation. In-process limits are fast and simple. Fleet-wide limits need shared state or an API gateway and introduce another availability dependency. Send requests for one identity through two limiter instances. Explain the combined effective quota and design a shared policy for a multi-replica deployment."},{"id":109,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"How should telemetry avoid becoming a data leak?","text":"How should telemetry avoid becoming a data leak? Prefer counts, durations, sanitized error categories, and controlled identifiers. Raw questions, passages, credentials, and user identifiers can expose private knowledge through logs, traces, and analytics systems even when retrieval itself is authorized. A redaction policy defaults raw query, document text, user identifiers, and unmasked errors to disabled. Event construction sanitizes errors and supports host-controlled redaction. This does not imply that every ordinary logger in the reference design or host is automatically covered. Exception strings, URLs, and custom metadata can carry secrets. A redacted event sink cannot sanitize unrelated logs or a provider's own diagnostics. Less raw data makes debugging harder and reduces exposure. Use temporary, controlled debugging fixtures rather than permanently recording production passages. Raise an error containing an API key, source URL, and query text. Inspect events and ordinary logs separately and document remaining exposure paths."},{"id":110,"slug":"security","chapter":"Security, tools, and trust boundaries","title":"What security acceptance tests matter most for this integration?","text":"What security acceptance tests matter most for this integration? Prioritize cross-tenant access, unsupported mandatory filters, source lookup after revocation, management permissions, malicious source instructions, SSRF redirects, and backend failures. These test authority boundaries rather than merely validating that a happy-path search returns text. The reference design has focused tests around retrieval constraints, connectors, tool bridges, and sync. A host must extend those checks to its own identity provider, deployment routes, and durable adapters. A mock that always applies filters can conceal an actual backend mismatch. Include tests against the deployed adapter combination and inspect what leaves the process for reranking or telemetry. Boundary tests take setup but prevent high-impact failures. Duplicating every implementation branch with brittle tests is less valuable than exercising the invariants users depend on. Write five adversarial scenarios with expected denial and one authorized control case for each. Specify whether denial must occur before any external provider receives document text."},{"id":111,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"How are Recall@K, Precision@K, and MRR calculated?","text":"How are Recall@K, Precision@K, and MRR calculated? Recall@K is the fraction of labeled relevant items found in the first K results. Precision@K is the fraction of those K positions that are relevant under the chosen denominator convention. Mean Reciprocal Rank averages one divided by the rank of the first relevant hit, using zero when none is found. An evaluation runner evaluates an injected retrieval function against labeled chunk IDs and records latency and errors. Always inspect denominator and short-result handling in the implementation before comparing scores with another tool. Labels can be incomplete, and chunk IDs can change after reindexing. A correct passage may be marked wrong if labels are tied to obsolete physical chunks. Retrieval metrics are cheap and diagnostic but do not evaluate final answer faithfulness. Use them alongside source-span or document labels and answer-level evaluation. For relevant IDs A and B and ranking C,A,D,B, calculate metrics at K=3. Then compare with the harness's exact conventions."},{"id":112,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"How should I create a useful evaluation dataset?","text":"How should I create a useful evaluation dataset? Include realistic questions, expected sources or spans, answerability, permissions, and failure cases. Cover exact identifiers, paraphrases, ambiguous requests, stale knowledge rules, multi-document synthesis, multilingual inputs, and unanswerable questions. Keep a held-out set separate from tuning examples. The evaluation modules accept datasets and injected retrieval or answer functions, so the same cases can compare chunkers, profiles, strategies, and host generation knowledge rules. Record the corpus revision and configuration alongside each run. Synthetic questions can mirror the source wording too closely and overstate quality. Data leakage from tuning into evaluation also produces misleading improvements. Human-labeled cases cost effort but reveal product relevance. Synthetic expansion is useful when reviewed and balanced against real user questions. Author 30 questions with source-level gold evidence and five deliberate non-answerable cases. Have another reviewer check labels without seeing your retrieval output."},{"id":113,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"Why is retrieval-only evaluation useful before generation exists?","text":"Why is retrieval-only evaluation useful before generation exists? It isolates whether the system can locate supporting material. If relevant passages never reach the candidate or evidence set, a better generator cannot reliably repair that failure. Retrieval-only runs are also cheaper and more deterministic than end-to-end model evaluation. The runner supports distinct evaluation modes and accepts host callbacks. the retrieval service can be evaluated as a subsystem without pretending it ships a complete production chat application. Strong retrieval metrics can hide poor evidence budgeting, unsupported claims, and an unhelpful UI. They are a stage gate, not the final product score. Early isolation speeds diagnosis and reduces API cost, while end-to-end testing is still needed to assess user outcomes. Use both at different stages of development. Establish a retrieval baseline before adding a generator. Record cases where the correct source is retrieved but later excluded from the evidence budget."},{"id":114,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"How do heuristic answer scoring and LLM-as-judge differ?","text":"How do heuristic answer scoring and LLM-as-judge differ? Heuristic scoring uses deterministic text-overlap signals and is cheap to run offline. LLM-as-judge asks a configured model to assess qualities such as faithfulness, relevance, completeness, and conciseness. Neither is an infallible measure of truth. The corresponding service module implements both strategies with standardized score containers. Heuristics provide a stable baseline; judges can assess richer language relationships but add provider cost and evaluation-model variability. Overlap can reward copying a wrong answer, while judges can exhibit bias, prompt sensitivity, and correlated mistakes. A judge may also be influenced by adversarial content in the answer or evidence. Use deterministic metrics for broad regression feedback and calibrated human review for critical cases. Model judging can expand coverage if its agreement with humans is measured. Score a faithful paraphrase, a copied incorrect answer, and a fluent unsupported answer using both approaches. Explain disagreements rather than averaging them away."},{"id":115,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"What does a high-value test strategy look like for this reference design?","text":"What does a high-value test strategy look like for this reference design? Test behavioral invariants at seams: no mutation after invalid preparation, scope constraints on both stages, explicit backend failure, stale-worker rejection, revision-correct lookup, and safe repeated job processing. Use focused fakes for deterministic races and real adapter tests for actual storage semantics. The test suite has targeted modules for ingestion locking, embedding execution, retrieval capabilities, and sync coordination. These boundaries are more consequential than mirroring every helper's internal implementation. Passing fake-based tests cannot prove production rollback, SQL locking, or vendor filter behavior. Conversely, an enormous end-to-end suite can be slow and poor at locating failures. A layered mix gives fast feedback and credible integration evidence. Choose the smallest test that can actually falsify the relevant guarantee. For one transaction guarantee and one retrieval filter guarantee, state which test needs a real database and which can use a controlled fake."},{"id":116,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"What is the difference between liveness, readiness, and degraded health?","text":"What is the difference between liveness, readiness, and degraded health? Liveness asks whether a process can run. Readiness asks whether it can serve its intended work. Degraded health means some useful behavior remains but a dependency or freshness condition is impaired. One healthy HTTP process does not prove its index is current or its embedding provider works. A health collector collects configuration, connectivity, counts, sync, job, and evaluation information. a health report exposes distinctions that a host can map to probes and user-facing status. An expensive consistency scan in every readiness probe can overload the database. A permissive count returning zero after an error can also mask a dependency failure if interpreted carelessly. Deeper checks improve diagnosis and cost more I/O. Separate cheap frequent probes from scheduled expensive consistency checks. Define probe behavior when the process is alive, the vector store is down, and the index is stale but readable. Explain whether traffic should continue in each case."},{"id":117,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"What operational signals should be monitored?","text":"What operational signals should be monitored? Track ingestion lag, scan authority failures, embedding retries and queue pressure, active-generation age, job backlog and oldest age, search latency, empty and failed retrieval rates, evidence truncation, and quality regressions. Each signal should connect to a user-visible consequence. Events and health checks expose stage and lifecycle information. Optional OpenTelemetry integration can connect this information to a host's tracing system without making it a mandatory runtime dependency. Large numbers of document IDs or tenant IDs as metric labels create cardinality and privacy problems. Raw query logging can expose sensitive user intent. Aggregated metrics are scalable but less detailed; controlled traces aid diagnosis. Correlate with sanitized operation identifiers and use retention knowledge rules appropriate to the data. Define an alert for rising stale-index age and another for rising backend-failure rate. Explain why their remediation differs even if both reduce answer quality."},{"id":118,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"How should a system meet an end-to-end latency target?","text":"How should a system meet an end-to-end latency target? Allocate a request deadline across stages, measure tail latency, and propagate cancellation and remaining time. Per-call timeouts and retry ceilings are ingredients, not an end-to-end deadline. Query variants, sequential stages, reranking, and generation can each consume the budget. A bounded embedding executor and query transformation expose local bounds; telemetry gives stage durations. The host must compose them into a product-level deadline and decide which optional stages can be skipped safely. Summing independent p95 measurements does not yield an exact request p95, and a timeout can leave remote work running. Retry storms can worsen the same overload they attempt to recover from. Tight deadlines improve responsiveness while reducing recovery and ranking opportunities. A slower asynchronous ingestion path can tolerate different retry policy from an interactive search. Trace a request with one retry and two query variants. Compare actual elapsed time with your budget and identify which optional stage should be shed first."},{"id":119,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"How do you roll out a retrieval change safely?","text":"How do you roll out a retrieval change safely? Pin the corpus and configuration, evaluate the candidate offline, compare shadow traffic where permitted, deploy to a limited audience, and watch quality plus operational signals. Preserve a rollback path to the prior profile, generation, and strategy configuration. Explicit snapshots, profiles, typed requests, and replaceable strategies make changes observable. They do not automatically implement feature flags, traffic splitting, or deployment rollback; the host supplies those controls. A model change can alter score distributions and invalidate thresholds, caches, and labels. A schema change can also break an older application version during rollback. Parallel operation costs resources but exposes regressions before broad impact. A direct cutover is simpler and appropriate only when risk and recovery are well understood. Write a release plan for enabling reranking with acceptance thresholds, cost limits, denial tests, and a one-step configuration rollback."},{"id":120,"slug":"evaluation","chapter":"Evaluation, testing, and operations","title":"How should you investigate quality drift over time?","text":"How should you investigate quality drift over time? Separate source drift, query-distribution drift, model or provider changes, extraction changes, index freshness, and application-policy changes. Re-run a stable benchmark and inspect newly failing real-world cases. A single aggregate score rarely identifies the cause. Profiles and ingestion snapshots record important configuration identity, while telemetry and evaluation capture operational and quality observations. A host should retain run metadata tying them together. Silent provider updates or hidden preprocessing changes may not be visible unless a model revision and adapter configuration are recorded. Privacy limits also constrain how production questions can be retained for analysis. Stable benchmarks preserve comparability but can become unrepresentative. Add reviewed new cases while keeping a fixed core set for historical comparison. Simulate an unchanged corpus with a changed query mix, then a changed extractor with the old query mix. Show how you would distinguish the resulting metric changes."},{"id":121,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"How do AI, ML, DL, NLP, and GenAI relate?","text":"How do AI, ML, DL, NLP, and GenAI relate? Artificial Intelligence is the broad field of building systems that perform intelligent tasks. Machine Learning learns behavior from data. Deep Learning uses layered neural networks. Natural Language Processing concerns language tasks. Generative AI produces new outputs such as text. A RAG application combines these ideas with conventional data and backend engineering. The retrieval service can call learned embedding and reranking providers, but much of its code is deterministic orchestration, validation, indexing, and coordination. Understanding that division prevents attributing every system behavior to a neural model. Acronyms can hide missing skills. Knowing how to call a model API is different from understanding representation learning, and understanding attention is different from securing a multi-tenant API. An AI full-stack engineer needs practical breadth and selective depth. Research-level model training is not required for every product, but model limitations must inform system design. Label each reference component as deterministic infrastructure, learned-model adapter, or application boundary. Explain why several components require more than one learning discipline."},{"id":122,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"What does attention compute, and why does context length matter?","text":"What does attention compute, and why does context length matter? Scaled dot-product attention forms weights from queries and keys, then combines value vectors: softmax(QK-transpose divided by square-root d) times V. This lets token representations depend on other tokens. Standard dense attention has pairwise interactions whose work and memory grow quadratically with sequence length, though implementations vary. A retrieval application supplies selected context rather than every document. the retrieval service's evidence budget controls that supplied material; it does not implement Transformer attention or determine a provider's internal serving architecture. A model accepting a long context does not guarantee equal use of every passage. More evidence can increase cost and distraction, so context limits and useful context are different concepts. Larger context can preserve more information while reducing the pressure to retrieve precisely. Retrieval remains useful for selection, access control, attribution, and cost even with long windows. Draw token-to-token attention for four tokens, then estimate how pair counts change when sequence length doubles."},{"id":123,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"How are encoder embeddings, decoder generation, bi-encoders, and cross-encoders different?","text":"How are encoder embeddings, decoder generation, bi-encoders, and cross-encoders different? An embedding encoder maps text into a reusable representation. A decoder language model generates tokens conditioned on prior context. A bi-encoder embeds query and document separately for efficient comparison. A cross-encoder jointly processes a query-document pair for more detailed scoring. The retrieval service's embedding interface fits reusable vector production, while the Reranker protocol permits more expensive pairwise relevance scoring. The application generator remains outside the ordinary search pipeline. A provider's model name or API endpoint does not prove which architecture or training objective it uses. Follow its documented task contract and validate retrieval quality rather than assuming all embeddings or rerankers are interchangeable. Separate encoding enables indexing documents once and searching quickly. Joint scoring spends more work per pair and often belongs after candidate retrieval. Estimate how many model computations are needed to retrieve from 100,000 documents using precomputed bi-encoder vectors versus scoring every pair with a cross-encoder."},{"id":124,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"What is contrastive learning, and why does it matter for retrieval?","text":"What is contrastive learning, and why does it matter for retrieval? Contrastive learning trains representations so relevant pairs score above irrelevant pairs. A common objective compares a query's positive passage with negatives using a softmax-based loss. Negative selection, label quality, and the task distribution shape what similarity means. The retrieval service consumes pretrained embedding functions rather than training them. Understanding their objective helps explain why a general embedding may confuse domain-specific exceptions or why a specialized model improves some queries and harms others. False negatives can teach a model to separate two genuinely relevant passages. Training leakage can also inflate benchmark scores, and same-dimension models remain incompatible despite similar training goals. Domain adaptation can improve ranking but needs representative data, compute, evaluation, and a new index generation. Better query and chunk design may solve the problem more cheaply. Create one query, one positive passage, one easy negative, and one hard negative. Explain what an accidental false negative would teach."},{"id":125,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"What are pretraining, SFT, and instruction tuning?","text":"What are pretraining, SFT, and instruction tuning? Pretraining learns broad statistical structure from large datasets. Supervised Fine-Tuning trains on task examples or desired responses. Instruction tuning is supervised adaptation aimed at following instructions across tasks. These change model parameters; indexing new documents changes external knowledge storage instead. The reference design does not include a model-training pipeline. Its datasets and answer evaluators support application evaluation, not an automatic SFT trainer. A host can use evaluation failures to decide whether behavior adaptation is warranted. Fine-tuning on private facts can create memorization and retention problems without solving freshness or citation. Poor examples can also teach confident unsupported answering. Behavior tuning may improve format and task consistency, while retrieval is usually more direct for changing private documents. Combining them requires separate evaluation of behavior and knowledge access. Divide twenty failures into missing evidence, bad ranking, bad prompt policy, and persistent behavioral problems. Choose which, if any, justify SFT."},{"id":126,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"What are LoRA, PEFT, and quantization?","text":"What are LoRA, PEFT, and quantization? Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning adapts a subset or compact parameterization instead of updating all weights. LoRA is one PEFT method that adds low-rank updates to frozen weights. Quantization represents weights or activations with reduced numerical precision; it addresses memory and compute rather than directly supplying new knowledge. These methods belong to a different responsibility from retrieval orchestration. They belong to the model layer a host may select, while embedding-profile changes still require compatible indexing and evaluation. Reduced precision or adaptation can change quality, and an efficient technique is not automatically safe for every model or hardware target. Avoid promising universal speedups or equal accuracy. These methods can reduce adaptation or serving resources while adding tooling and compatibility considerations. Provider-hosted models transfer much of that operational work elsewhere. Compare full fine-tuning, LoRA adaptation, and quantized inference in a table of what changes, what stays frozen, and which evaluation is needed."},{"id":127,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"How do RLHF and DPO differ from RAG?","text":"How do RLHF and DPO differ from RAG? Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback uses human preference information to shape model behavior, often through a learned reward and policy optimization. Direct Preference Optimization trains from preferred and rejected response pairs without the same explicit reinforcement-learning loop. RAG supplies external evidence at inference time. These methods operate at different layers and can coexist. This reference design provides retrieval and evaluation components, not RLHF or DPO training. Its answer-quality failures may motivate better behavior data, but indexing is not preference optimization. Preferences can reward pleasing or verbose answers instead of faithful ones. A preference-trained model can still hallucinate, and a retrieved passage can still be wrong. Alignment training addresses behavioral tendencies and requires datasets and training infrastructure. Retrieval addresses access to evidence and requires corpus infrastructure. Neither replaces the other's evaluation. Write a preferred and rejected answer pair where the preference is justified by citation faithfulness. Explain what that pair teaches and what source freshness it cannot teach permanently."},{"id":128,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"What are temperature, top-p, tokens, and structured output?","text":"What are temperature, top-p, tokens, and structured output? Tokens are the model's discrete input and output units. Temperature changes the sharpness of sampling probabilities; top-p restricts sampling to a cumulative probability mass. Structured output constrains response form, such as a schema. These are generation controls, not retrieval relevance measures. A host chooses generation parameters and serialization outside the library's search path. Evidence budgets reserve output capacity, but the actual generator must honor the chosen output contract and handle parse or validation failures. Low temperature does not guarantee truth or reproducibility across providers. Valid JSON can still contain unsupported claims or unauthorized source references. Tighter structure improves integration and can constrain expressive answers. More stochastic generation can help creative tasks while making faithful factual workflows harder to evaluate. Define an answer schema with status, text, and citation references. Validate citation membership against the actual EvidenceBundle rather than only validating JSON syntax."},{"id":129,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"What are HyDE, agentic RAG, GraphRAG, and multimodal RAG?","text":"What are HyDE, agentic RAG, GraphRAG, and multimodal RAG? HyDE retrieves using an embedding of a generated hypothetical answer or document. Agentic RAG lets a controller choose repeated retrieval or tool actions. GraphRAG adds graph relationships to retrieval or synthesis. Multimodal RAG includes non-text modalities. These are families of techniques, not automatically enabled project features. Query-transform, strategy, tool-bridge, and extractor seams can support experiments, but a host must implement the actual controller, graph, modality representation, and evaluation. A source connector alone does not create multimodal reasoning. Each added generative or traversal step expands cost and failure modes. Hypothetical content can introduce invented facts; agents can loop; graph edges can be stale; modality extraction can lose evidence fidelity. Advanced methods can solve demonstrated complex cases while making the baseline harder to understand and operate. Add them incrementally with bounded execution. Pick one baseline failure and design the smallest extension that addresses it. Define a cost ceiling and a benchmark that could prove the extension unnecessary."},{"id":130,"slug":"models","chapter":"Deep learning and generative AI","title":"How should an AI full-stack engineer learn deep learning alongside this reference design?","text":"How should an AI full-stack engineer learn deep learning alongside this reference design? Learn vectors and similarity first, then tokenization, neural training objectives, attention, model adaptation, and inference. Tie each topic to an observable product failure: incompatible vectors, poor paraphrase recall, context overflow, unsupported generation, or latency under load. The code offers a concrete laboratory for interfaces around learned systems while keeping infrastructure inspectable. Training a small encoder or language model is a separate learning exercise that deepens understanding without requiring a rewrite of the retrieval subsystem. Studying only theory misses deployment and security; studying only APIs leaves model behavior mysterious. A portfolio should demonstrate both a measured model comparison and a robust application boundary. Breadth requires sustained practice. Sequence small, testable projects rather than attempting to master distributed storage and large-model training simultaneously. Follow the roadmap labs, then train a tiny contrastive encoder on a reviewed toy dataset. Compare it with the hashing baseline and a pretrained model using the same held-out queries."},{"id":131,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"What application components are still needed around the retrieval service?","text":"What application components are still needed around the retrieval service? A complete product needs identity, authorized API routes, a document-management workflow, durable adapters, background workers, generation policy, a user interface, monitoring, deployment, and evaluation. The reference design supplies important subsystem pieces and examples, not all of these production services. Build around the evidence contract: the API obtains authorized evidence, then a host generator produces an answer, while the UI can open source spans and show status. This keeps source verification useful even without generation. Assuming the library owns users or migrations can leave critical responsibilities unimplemented. A polished chat screen does not compensate for missing tenant isolation or recovery behavior. Explicit host ownership requires more work but fits an existing backend. A monolithic demo can be quicker and should be labeled according to its actual guarantees. Draw your target application with browser, API, workers, identity provider, source system, vector store, snapshot store, and model provider. Mark every trust boundary."},{"id":132,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"How would you design the search and chat API contracts?","text":"How would you design the search and chat API contracts? Expose structured request and response objects with query, permitted collection choice, bounded retrieval options, operation identity, status, evidence, and citations. Resolve user identity and allowed scope on the server. Keep internal database names and arbitrary backend filters out of the public API unless deliberately supported. RetrievalRequest and EvidenceBundle provide useful internal shapes, but the host should create a stable transport contract rather than expose all library internals. Errors should distinguish validation, denied access, backend outage, and successful empty search. Letting a browser select raw table names or trusted client IDs bypasses the composition boundary. Returning provider exceptions directly may leak secrets or infrastructure details. A narrow API is safer and easier to evolve; an expert administration API may expose more controls under stronger permissions. Write example request and response JSON for success, denied scope, and backend failure. Explain which fields the server derives instead of trusting the client."},{"id":133,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"How should streaming work without misleading the user?","text":"How should streaming work without misleading the user? Complete authorization and evidence selection before presenting factual generated service requests as supported. Stream generation events with an operation ID and explicit stages, then preserve a final status and citation mapping. The exact HTTP transport is a host design choice, not a provided chat server feature. Evidence assembly produces a stable bundle before a generator consumes it. The UI can show searching, assembling, generating, complete, cancelled, or failed states without pretending that partial text is a verified final answer. A network disconnect can leave server work running, and partial output may contain incomplete citation syntax. Propagate cancellation where possible and never silently mark a truncated stream complete. Early streaming improves perceived responsiveness while complicating error recovery and state handling. A non-streaming first version is easier to validate. Design events for evidence_ready, answer_delta, complete, and failure. Simulate a disconnect after two deltas and specify what remains visible and retryable."},{"id":134,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"What should a useful RAG interface show besides the answer text?","text":"What should a useful RAG interface show besides the answer text? Show source titles and bounded excerpts, revision or freshness when relevant, citation navigation, loading and failure states, and a clear way to refine an ambiguous question. Make evidence accessible by keyboard and avoid presenting similarity as factual confidence. The bundle already contains attribution and status data that a frontend can render. revision-aware source lookup provides a verification path when the user opens a citation; that path must retain the same identity and scope policy. Rendering document text as raw HTML can create cross-site scripting. Use safe text rendering or a deliberately sanitized markup pipeline, and treat citation URLs as untrusted input. More evidence improves transparency but can overwhelm users. Start with concise source summaries and progressive disclosure of full spans. Build a search-only UI before chat. Include empty, stale, failed, and insufficient-budget examples, then test it entirely with keyboard navigation."},{"id":135,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"How should frontend state be separated from backend truth?","text":"How should frontend state be separated from backend truth? Keep local interaction state such as expanded sources or draft input separate from server state such as operation status, evidence, and active document versions. Key cached results by the relevant identity, scope, query, and configuration or revision where necessary. The retrieval service returns structured results, leaving the browser framework and caching library to the host. A frontend must preserve status distinctions and invalidate data when permissions or active generations change. Reusing a browser cache across sign-out or tenant switching can expose another user's evidence. Optimistically marking ingestion complete before activation can also misrepresent searchability. Explicit invalidation and cache partitioning add complexity but preserve correct user expectations. A simple no-cache first implementation can be preferable to unsafe reuse. Switch tenants while a request is in flight. Ensure its late response cannot populate the new tenant's visible state or source drawer."},{"id":136,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"How should document upload and indexing progress be designed?","text":"How should document upload and indexing progress be designed? Treat upload acceptance, source storage, extraction, embedding, activation, and searchability as distinct states. Persist the accepted source and enqueue background work according to the host's transaction policy. Return an operation identifier rather than blocking the request until every provider call completes. Outbox and drainer interfaces decouple request latency from processing. Sync reports and events provide lifecycle information that a host can translate into progress, while active visibility determines when content can actually be searched. A successful upload can later fail extraction or embedding. The UI must not claim indexed status solely because bytes were received, and retry actions must avoid duplicate logical documents. Background workflows need polling or event delivery and durable state. Synchronous ingestion is simpler for small local experiments but less resilient under provider latency. Define a document state machine from uploaded to searchable, with retry and failed states. Associate each transition with the component that can authoritatively report it."},{"id":137,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"What does production deployment require beyond installing optional extras?","text":"What does production deployment require beyond installing optional extras? Provision durable stores and schemas, configure credentials and network access, run workers, set identity and permissions, select compatible profiles, validate startup, and establish backups, monitoring, and rollback. An optional dependency installs client code; it does not provision or secure its service. The dependency manifest separates backend extras from dependency-free core, while health checks and integration examples help validate wiring. Actual database indexes and extension configuration remain deployment responsibilities. Multiple replicas using in-memory manifests or rate limits do not share authoritative state. Secrets in configuration fingerprints or public diagnostics can also create exposure. Managed services reduce some operations work and add cost or provider dependence. Self-hosting gives control and requires deeper operational expertise. Produce a deployment inventory with ownership, persistence, backup, restart behavior, and secret source for every component. Include what happens when one replica restarts."},{"id":138,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"How would you plan capacity and handle overload?","text":"How would you plan capacity and handle overload? Model request arrival, per-stage service time, concurrency, queue capacity, database pools, and provider quotas. Apply admission control before expensive work, bound jobs, and shed optional processing when appropriate. Interactive search and batch ingestion should not starve each other accidentally. The executor semaphore, transport limiter, job store, and health signals are local building blocks. The host must coordinate quotas across replicas and enforce end-to-end budgets. Increasing worker count can increase throttling rather than throughput. An unbounded backlog hides overload until memory, storage, or freshness collapses. Separate worker pools and queues improve isolation but add infrastructure. A shared small deployment is economical until contention becomes measurable. Estimate sustainable throughput from provider concurrency and observed call duration, then load-test around that estimate. Report queue age as well as requests per second."},{"id":139,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"What portfolio project would demonstrate AI full-stack competence?","text":"What portfolio project would demonstrate AI full-stack competence? Build a multi-tenant knowledge assistant with a search-first interface, revision-aware citations, background ingestion, a grounded answer policy, and a reproducible evaluation report. Show a failure and its diagnosis, not only a polished happy-path recording. This combines frontend state, API design, identity, async jobs, data modeling, retrieval, generation, evaluation, and operations. the retrieval service provides the subsystem foundation while your work proves the application integration. A public demo can accidentally expose private sample documents or provider credentials. Use a reviewed fixture corpus and document implemented guarantees rather than aspirational architecture. A narrow reliable project is more convincing than many unfinished advanced features. Add reranking or multi-query only when your benchmark shows why they help. Deliver a README with architecture, a threat-boundary map, benchmark results, three recovery demonstrations, and a five-minute walkthrough that opens a cited source revision."},{"id":140,"slug":"fullstack","chapter":"From subsystem to AI full-stack product","title":"How should I answer a senior system-design interview using this reference design?","text":"How should I answer a senior system-design interview using this reference design? Start with requirements and constraints, then describe the offline and online flows, data identities, permission boundary, consistency model, failure recovery, quality evaluation, and capacity plan. Defend choices with case-based mechanisms and acknowledge which guarantees depend on host adapters. The reference design's strongest lessons are explicit boundaries: prepare versus commit, candidates versus final evidence, logical visibility versus physical cleanup, and retrieval quality versus answer faithfulness. These distinctions scale beyond one library. Memorized pattern names and invented timing claims collapse under follow-up questions. Be ready to explain a crash interleaving, an authorization failure, and a measured quality trade-off in concrete terms. A good design is conditional on requirements. State when a keyword-only system, one transactional database, or a simpler synchronous workflow would be the better choice. Give a 45-minute mock interview: five minutes requirements, ten architecture, ten consistency and security, ten evaluation and scale, and ten trade-offs and alternatives."},{"id":141,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"What does a full support application add beyond a retrieval subsystem?","text":"What does a full support application add beyond a retrieval subsystem? A complete application adds authenticated APIs, customer-owned service requests, versioned knowledge, an agent runtime, confirmation workflows, persistent conversation history, background jobs, evaluation, and observability. Retrieval remains one service within that product. Bind authenticated APIs, domain repositories, retrieval, model execution, and a client through typed contracts. Each layer should expose failures without pretending another layer has completed its work. Do not infer that every newer the retrieval service feature is integrated. The knowledge tool concatenates retrieved context directly; it does not automatically use the library's full an evidence assembler and revision-lookup contract. A narrow host binding is easier to ship, while richer evidence contracts add explicit statuses and provenance. Adoption requires wiring and migration rather than only upgrading a dependency. Trace a question from the browser through authentication, tool selection, scoped retrieval, evidence assembly, generation, and source opening."},{"id":142,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"How should the orchestration framework be distinguished from model and retrieval adapters?","text":"How should the orchestration framework be distinguished from model and retrieval adapters? The orchestration runtime controls the agent loop. A model adapter normalizes provider access, a retrieval service supplies evidence, and a tracing backend records execution. A product may use ADK, LangChain, or another runtime without changing these responsibilities. Draw separate boxes for the runtime, model adapter, retrieval service, business services, and telemetry. Evaluate a replacement at its contract boundary rather than inferring responsibilities from package names. Framework confusion leads to examples that cannot run against the reference design's dependencies. The later LangChain, LangGraph, and A2A chapters explicitly describe alternatives or additions rather than pretending those frameworks are already installed. Staying with one orchestrator reduces state-model overlap. Adding another can solve a measured workflow need but introduces serialization, tracing, error, and deployment boundaries. Make a dependency-layer table with web framework, agent runtime, model adapter, retrieval library, persistence layer, and tracing service. Name their concrete constructors."},{"id":143,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"How does an eligibility question differ from a request-status question?","text":"How does an eligibility question differ from a request-status question? Coverage requires retrieving knowledge rules and applying a reviewed procedure to the user's facts. Claim status is a lookup of structured application state under the authenticated owner's scope. An answer about a policy is evidence-based interpretation; an answer about an existing request must reflect the actual recorded claim. Route an exact request identifier to an authorized structured lookup. Route a knowledge question to applicable evidence. Evaluate both the intent decision and the resulting resource scope. Semantic similarity cannot establish that an arbitrary request ID belongs to the user or that a versioned rule applies to their contract. Tool-side identity checks remain necessary even when prompting selects the right tool. Separate tools make intent and authority clearer but require route-selection evaluation. One universal search tool is simpler and can blur exact facts with approximate matches. Write expected tool sequences for an exact request ID, a remembered incident without an ID, and an eligibility question with missing facts."},{"id":144,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"What is the difference between an agent session and a conversation record?","text":"What is the difference between an agent session and a conversation record? Agent sessions hold runtime state and events used by ADK. Conversation records hold application-visible messages, ownership, archival state, and IDs used by feedback and history APIs. They overlap in content but serve different contracts and lifecycle requirements. Give runtime sessions and user-visible conversations separate identities and retention rules. Rehydrate only authorized history and reconcile message IDs with durable task and operation state. Divergence can replay a turn twice, omit tool results, or show UI messages that the agent cannot recall. Switching storage modes also needs a deliberate migration and restart story. Separate stores decouple product history from runtime implementation but require clear reconciliation and retention ownership. A single store reduces duplication and can bind the product tightly to one framework. Trace which IDs identify the user, conversation, ADK session, invocation, user message, and assistant message. Explain which remain stable after a restart."},{"id":145,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"What is the real prepare-and-confirm boundary for request creation?","text":"What is the real prepare-and-confirm boundary for request creation? Preparation validates fields and stores a pending submission with a confirmation token and expiry. Confirmation is an authenticated API operation that locks the pending submission, creates the service request and associated records, marks completion, and commits. Agent prose does not itself complete the financial workflow. Prepare validated intent, present exact parameters for review, then verify approval inside a short transaction. Persist the authoritative result before claiming the action completed. The prompt's phrase that a tool call is the confirmation action can be misunderstood. The actual database submission still requires the separate route. A textual yes from the model is not proof that the API operation succeeded. Two steps add user interaction and pending-state management while reducing accidental writes. A one-step workflow is faster but gives less opportunity to review material fields. Follow one token from preparation to a locked confirmation transaction. Identify which operation first makes the service request durable."},{"id":146,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"Why separate policy procedures from knowledge facts?","text":"Why separate policy procedures from knowledge facts? A procedure describes how to investigate a question or route an unclear request. A knowledge source supplies actual limits, deductibles, exclusions, and conditions. The procedure should not become an alternative authority for contractual facts merely because it is easier to retrieve. Store playbooks as workflow guidance and knowledge articles as factual authority. Version both and evaluate whether a current procedure can still select an inapplicable source. A stale procedure can direct an incomplete investigation, while a current procedure can still be combined with the wrong rule revision. Both source identities need to be visible in evaluation and operational diagnosis. Separating reasoning guidance from factual material supports independent review but adds retrieval calls and version combinations. Hardcoding all instructions in one prompt is easier to start and harder to govern. Label every sentence in the eligibility-review playbook as a workflow rule or factual claim. Move any unsupported numeric policy claim back to the authoritative source layer."},{"id":147,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"Where does eventual consistency appear to a customer?","text":"Where does eventual consistency appear to a customer? A request can be durably submitted before its embedding job completes, so exact structured lookup and semantic search may temporarily disagree. Notification delivery can also lag the request transaction. These are different read and delivery projections of one business operation. Commit the request and its downstream jobs together. Serve exact status from authoritative records while exposing indexing or notification lag as separate projection states. A search miss immediately after filing must not be reported as proof that no claim exists. An authoritative exact lookup can confirm persistence while the index catches up. Asynchronous processing keeps submission independent of provider latency but introduces lag and recovery work. Synchronous indexing is simpler conceptually and couples business availability to model services. Pause the embedding worker, file a service request, and compare exact lookup with semantic search. Define the appropriate user-visible status for each result."},{"id":148,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"What are the main trust boundaries in this application?","text":"What are the main trust boundaries in this application? Boundaries include browser-to-API identity, model-to-tool authority, source-to-prompt content, application-to-database privileges, application-to-model disclosure, and application-to-observability export. Each boundary has a different actor and failure mode. Annotate every edge with identity, allowed data, and permitted effects. Model output, retrieved text, remote metadata, and observability exports cross different trust boundaries. A protected chat endpoint is insufficient if a tool trusts model-supplied owner IDs or a trace exports private claim text. Read and write paths require separate evidence of enforcement. Explicit boundaries add policy and testing effort but make failures diagnosable. Combining all access behind a powerful agent credential simplifies integration and magnifies mistakes. Draw the support runtime diagram and annotate every edge with identity, permitted data, and whether the receiver can initiate authoritative writes."},{"id":149,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"Which application guarantees must be established end to end?","text":"Which application guarantees must be established end to end? Implemented mechanisms include owner-scoped case retrieval, a locked confirmation row, caller-owned submission transactions, typed streaming events, optional shared idempotency, and trace instrumentation. Their deployment guarantees depend on real database behavior, chosen storage settings, provider configuration, and complete use of the intended paths. Define tool availability, ownership checks, approval validation, persistence, and stream outcomes as independently testable contracts. A framework swap should preserve or explicitly revise those contracts. Reading a comment about deployment defaults or a role model as proof of configured behavior creates false confidence. Follow defaults and call sites rather than naming available classes. Conservative defaults support local development but may require production gates. Rejecting startup on missing critical state is stricter than silently falling back to local state. Create a deployment evidence checklist showing the selected session, idempotency, rate-limit, and tracing backends without printing secrets."},{"id":150,"slug":"application-architecture","chapter":"A reference AI application end to end","title":"How should you trace a request across the complete application?","text":"How should you trace a request across the complete application? Start at user intent and verified identity, follow tool selection and scoped services, inspect evidence and model output, then confirm persisted results and client state. Trace the actual path, including failures and recovery. Turn architectural guarantees into acceptance tests. A diagram describes intended responsibilities; concurrent workers, deployment settings, provider errors, and bypass paths determine whether the implementation actually meets them. A diagram can describe an ideal design while a bypass path violates it. Verify each critical boundary through observable behavior rather than relying only on intended structure. Cross-project tracing takes more effort than a single overview but teaches integration judgment, the central skill in full-stack AI engineering. Trace three synthetic tasks: a factual answer, an approved write, and a provider failure. Record the invariant checked at every boundary."},{"id":151,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"Why store support service requests in relational tables instead of only vector documents?","text":"Why store support service requests in relational tables instead of only vector documents? Service requests have exact ownership, monetary amounts, lifecycle states, references, and transaction boundaries. A vector index optimizes similarity retrieval and does not replace those relational invariants. The same request can have an authoritative row and a derived search representation. Keep authoritative ownership, state transitions, and relationships in constrained transactional records. Use vector indexes as derived search projections that can be rebuilt, not as the only source of business truth. Treating the vector record as the sole source of truth makes exact authorization, status transitions, and recovery dependent on a derived index. Search ranking cannot decide whether a service request exists or who owns it. Relational plus search modeling creates projection maintenance work but supports exact operations and flexible discovery. A text-only prototype is smaller and insufficient for authoritative workflows. Mark every Claim column as identity, business fact, authority constraint, lifecycle metadata, or derived search data. Explain which can be rebuilt."},{"id":152,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"Why have both a UUID primary key and a human-readable request ID?","text":"Why have both a UUID primary key and a human-readable request ID? A UUID provides an internal identifier suitable for references; a separate request ID provides a support-friendly external label. They have different display and collision properties. Neither identifier should be treated as an authorization secret. Choose immutable internal identifiers and stable public references deliberately. Scope uniqueness to the right tenant or operation and reject collisions through database constraints. Shortened random identifiers can collide. A production collision-handling path should distinguish a retryable uniqueness conflict from unrelated database errors, and users must still be scoped even when an ID is known. Friendly IDs improve support and communication while adding an extra index and identity mapping. Full opaque IDs are simpler but awkward to read aloud. Explain how an identifier collision differs from an idempotent replay. Design a bounded generation retry that does not duplicate a pending submission."},{"id":153,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"Why use Numeric and Decimal for request amounts?","text":"Why use Numeric and Decimal for request amounts? Decimal arithmetic and a fixed-scale Numeric column represent monetary values more predictably than binary floating point. Currency precision, rounding, allowed scale, and upper bounds are part of the domain contract rather than formatting details. Use decimal representations and explicit currency rules. Validate scale and range before persistence, and preserve decimal strings across API serialization rather than round-tripping through binary floats. A Decimal column cannot retroactively undo precision lost earlier in JSON parsing, float conversion, or arithmetic. Validation and serialization must preserve the intended amount end to end. Decimal values need explicit JSON handling and careful provider-tool schemas. Float is convenient for generic numeric tools but weakens exact-money guarantees. Trace an amount with cents from browser JSON through Pydantic, pending JSONB, confirmation, and SQL storage. Propose a Decimal-preserving signature and matching tests."},{"id":154,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"How do foreign keys, unique constraints, and check constraints complement application validation?","text":"How do foreign keys, unique constraints, and check constraints complement application validation? Application validation gives useful error messages; database constraints protect against every writer sharing the database. Foreign keys preserve references, unique constraints enforce identity rules, and check constraints reject invalid row-level values even when a route is bypassed. Specify foreign keys, nullability, uniqueness, and state checks in migrations. Application validation improves errors; database constraints protect against concurrent callers and alternative write paths. Assuming all invariants are enforced because ORM types exist leaves gaps for scripts, workers, and future routes. Constraints also need deliberate delete and migration behavior. Strong constraints simplify consistency reasoning but can complicate staged migrations and bulk imports. Relaxing them requires another proven enforcement mechanism. Compare the Claim, PendingRequest, an indexing job, and a request event models with their migrations. List invariants enforced only in Python and those enforced by PostgreSQL."},{"id":155,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"What belongs in normalized columns versus JSONB?","text":"What belongs in normalized columns versus JSONB? Put fields used for relationships, strict constraints, frequent filtering, and stable reporting in typed columns. JSONB is useful for variable event payloads or a prepared-form snapshot. Its flexibility does not remove the need for schema versions, validation, or selective indexing. Normalize identity, ownership, status, amounts, timestamps, and relationships that need constraints. Reserve JSONB for evolving payloads whose validation and query needs are explicitly managed. Unversioned JSON can become unreadable after schema changes. Copying an entire stale state blob can also overwrite concurrent updates, while arbitrary JSON may retain more private data than needed. JSONB speeds evolving payloads and weakens some relational guarantees. Normalize stable, high-value fields instead of treating the database as a dictionary with no contracts. Design a version field and migration reader for a prepared submission whose incident_date field was added later. Explain the fallback for older records."},{"id":156,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"Why does the owner-and-created-at composite index matter?","text":"Why does the owner-and-created-at composite index matter? A request list filters by owner and orders by creation time. An index beginning with owner_id and then created_at can support that access pattern more directly than two unrelated indexes. Index design should follow real predicates, ordering, and pagination. Start index design from actual predicates and ordering. Inspect query plans and representative data distributions, then balance faster reads against write cost and maintenance. An index declaration does not prove an efficient plan at production data sizes. Additional sort keys, selective predicates, and row estimates can change the plan; unused indexes still cost writes and storage. More indexes accelerate selected reads while slowing mutation and increasing maintenance. Validate with representative EXPLAIN plans rather than indexing every column reflexively. Compare the owner-scoped listing plan with and without the composite index in a disposable database. Include a tie-breaking ID if you design keyset pagination."},{"id":157,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"How do knowledge versions differ from active knowledge chunks?","text":"How do knowledge versions differ from active knowledge chunks? A knowledge version records the source and indexing configuration over time. Policy chunks are searchable material for a version. Keeping version records does not necessarily mean every historical chunk remains retained or that a query selects the contract applicable to an incident. Track effective intervals separately from indexing time. Resolve the applicable source revision before searching its derived chunks, and retain the revision needed to verify an earlier answer. Current-source retrieval can answer the wrong historical question even when its text is accurately quoted. Historical evidence needs both retention and an explicit applicable-version filter. Retaining every version supports audits and costs storage and selection logic. Current-only indexing is simpler and must state its scope honestly. Create two rule revisions with different deductibles and one incident between their effective dates. Define the query and citation fields required to choose correctly."},{"id":158,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"Why use Alembic migrations instead of create_all for production evolution?","text":"Why use Alembic migrations instead of create_all for production evolution? Create_all creates missing schema objects represented in current metadata; it is not a reviewed sequence for changing existing columns, backfilling data, or coordinating old and new application versions. Migrations record explicit upgrade steps and dependencies. Make production schema changes explicit and reviewed. Assign migration ownership, verify upgrades against existing data, and coordinate application compatibility instead of relying on runtime table creation. Runtime-created tables can escape a migration policy, and concurrent DDL can disrupt startup. Inventory every schema owner and make a failed migration visibly block incompatible application use. Migrations add review and deployment sequencing but make history inspectable. Runtime creation is convenient for framework state and harder to govern consistently. Define which component owns each reference table and write a reviewed upgrade path for an existing populated database."},{"id":159,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"What is an expand-and-contract migration?","text":"What is an expand-and-contract migration? Add a compatible schema surface first, deploy code that can read old and new forms, backfill safely, switch writers, then remove the old form in a later release. The sequence allows rolling deployment and rollback without requiring every process to switch simultaneously. Add a compatible new structure, backfill with bounded work, deploy readers and writers deliberately, and remove obsolete structure only after verifying all consumers have migrated. A backfill can hold locks or overload the database; a destructive cleanup can make application rollback impossible. Background jobs may also contain payloads written by an older schema version. Compatibility periods increase temporary complexity and reduce deployment coupling. A single destructive migration is shorter and can require a maintenance window. Design a migration from float-shaped tool input to explicit decimal-string amounts. Include old pending submissions and jobs, not just current Claim rows."},{"id":160,"slug":"database-modeling","chapter":"Relational data modeling and migrations","title":"How should you model authoritative events versus derived projections?","text":"How should you model authoritative events versus derived projections? A request event records lifecycle history; a request status is a current-state projection. Embedding records and notifications are downstream projections. Agent session events describe runtime execution, while audit events describe selected high-value actions. These logs are related but not interchangeable event-sourcing systems. Separate authoritative lifecycle records from derived search and notification projections. Define replay and reconciliation for each projection rather than calling every append-only table an event-sourced system. Calling any append-only table event sourcing overstates replayability. A complete event-sourced design needs sufficient immutable events, deterministic projection rebuilding, versioning, and a declared source of truth. Current rows plus a transition trail provide useful history with lower complexity. Full event sourcing improves replay options and adds substantial schema and operational obligations. For each event table, state whether it can rebuild a Claim exactly. Identify missing facts before claiming replay-based recovery."},{"id":161,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"How does SELECT FOR UPDATE prevent duplicate confirmation?","text":"How does SELECT FOR UPDATE prevent duplicate confirmation? Confirmation locks the pending submission selected by token, owner, and pending status. A second transaction targeting the same row waits; after the first commits, the second must observe the updated state under the actual isolation rules. The lock protects a database transition, not the browser button. Select the pending operation by trusted owner and token, acquire the appropriate row lock, recheck expiry and state, then apply the effect and final state in one transaction. An internal commit would release the lock before the pending row changes, enabling duplicate creation. A request timeout or double click is expected behavior to test, not evidence that the client will never retry. Pessimistic locking simplifies this contested transition but makes lock duration important. Keep remote model work out of the locked transaction. Start two confirmations simultaneously in separate database sessions. Assert one logical claim and one consumed submission, then inspect the losing request's result."},{"id":162,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"What is the difference between flush, commit, and rollback?","text":"What is the difference between flush, commit, and rollback? Flush sends pending ORM changes to the transaction, allowing generated identities and database constraints to be observed. Commit makes the transaction's successful changes durable. Rollback discards its transactional changes. Flushed data is not therefore committed, and another session's visibility depends on isolation. Flush exposes transactional changes and constraint errors without committing. Keep one owner for commit and rollback so nested services cannot partially finalize a larger business operation. Catching a database exception and continuing with the same failed transaction can produce further errors. A swallowed exception is not a successful recovery; rollback or a correctly scoped savepoint is needed. Caller-owned transactions permit atomic composition but require explicit ownership. Helpers that commit themselves are easier in isolation and unsafe inside larger workflows. Inject failure after the service request flush but before the final commit. Inspect all related tables from another session after rollback."},{"id":163,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"What does PostgreSQL Read Committed mean for an application workflow?","text":"What does PostgreSQL Read Committed mean for an application workflow? Ordinary statements see committed state as of each statement's snapshot, so two reads in one transaction can observe intervening commits. A transaction boundary alone does not make a sequence of business decisions appear serial. Locking or conditional updates must protect the relevant invariant. Under Read Committed, separate statements may observe different committed snapshots. Protect a multi-statement invariant with appropriate constraints, locks, or guarded updates instead of assuming one stable view. A pattern that reads pending, calls external work, then updates completed can race under ordinary concurrency. Shared data snapshots and write authority are different properties. Stronger isolation can reduce anomalies while increasing retries and contention. It does not eliminate the need for idempotent external effects. Reproduce a changing read in two sessions, then compare a locked confirmation and a conditional UPDATE. Record actual transaction settings."},{"id":164,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"When would optimistic concurrency control be useful?","text":"When would optimistic concurrency control be useful? Optimistic control updates a row only when its version or prior state matches what the caller observed. A zero-row update signals a conflict. It suits edits where collisions are uncommon and a caller can reload rather than hold a lock during long user interaction. Include the expected version in the update predicate and require exactly one affected row. On conflict, reload state and revalidate intent before deciding whether retry is safe. Checking a version in Python and then issuing an unconditional update is not optimistic concurrency control. The comparison and mutation must be one database operation. Short conditional writes reduce waiting but require conflict handling. Row locks can be simpler for a small critical transition with many related writes. Write a versioned update for a hypothetical claim note. Make two editors submit the same expected version and verify only one succeeds."},{"id":165,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"How does job selection differ from durable job ownership?","text":"How does job selection differ from durable job ownership? A selection transaction can return candidate IDs and then release its locks. If acquisition happens later, a guarded update and its returned rows establish which worker owns each job. Alternatively, select and claim atomically in one transaction. Inspect the full lifecycle before asserting exclusive ownership. If candidate selection ends its transaction before acquisition, a later conditional update establishes ownership. Alternatively, claim atomically in one transaction; inspect the whole lifecycle before asserting exclusivity. Describing selection alone as exclusive ownership hides a race and encourages unsafe future changes. Completion and reclamation also need ownership-aware conditions to prevent obsolete workers from overwriting newer outcomes. Split selection and guarded claiming compose with the generic protocol but require more careful reasoning. One atomic claim statement can simplify the implementation at the cost of adapter-specific SQL. Interleave two workers between selection and marking. Verify the loser receives no processing payload for the contested job."},{"id":166,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"Why is one AsyncSession per concurrent task important?","text":"Why is one AsyncSession per concurrent task important? AsyncSession represents mutable transactional and ORM state. Independent concurrent operations should use independent sessions rather than sharing one session inside asyncio.gather. An async driver does not make one transaction object safe for arbitrary concurrent use. Create an independent AsyncSession per independently concurrent transaction. Bound database concurrency separately from provider calls and avoid sharing mutable ORM state across unrelated async tasks. Sharing can cause state errors, unintended transaction coupling, or unpredictable query sequencing. Creating too many sessions instead can exhaust the pool, so concurrency also needs a bound. Separate sessions allow independent I/O while consuming additional connections and potentially observing different snapshots. Sequential queries on one session are easier when one consistent transaction is required. Compare concurrent reads with separate sessions and sequential reads with one session. Measure pool checkout waits and document the required consistency model."},{"id":167,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"Can persistent ADK session state still suffer lost updates?","text":"Can persistent ADK session state still suffer lost updates? Yes. Persistence retains state but does not automatically merge concurrent updates. If two workers read one version, modify different fields, and overwrite the whole JSON object, the later write can discard the earlier change. Choose session serialization, optimistic versions, or explicit field-level merge semantics. Test two stale readers changing different fields; durable storage alone does not protect against a lost update. An append-only event record can survive while the latest state loses another turn's field. Sequence numbers also do not prove the model saw a serial conversational history. Per-session turn serialization improves consistency and reduces same-conversation throughput. Delta updates or optimistic versions offer alternatives with their own conflict policy. Load the same session twice, apply different state keys concurrently, and inspect both the event log and final state."},{"id":168,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"Why is the Redis idempotency claim a best-effort mechanism rather than exactly-once execution?","text":"Why is the Redis idempotency claim a best-effort mechanism rather than exactly-once execution? The claim has a finite TTL, waiters stop waiting after a bounded time, and Redis outages permit local execution. A slow winner can still run while another replica gives up and recomputes. The claim value is not a durable operation result or a fencing token for a business write. A short-lived Redis lease can reduce duplicate model work while expiring during a slow call. Use an owner token with checked release, and enforce business idempotency through a durable operation key in the authoritative database. An obsolete worker can delete or overwrite a newer lease if ownership is not checked. Duplicate model calls may add cost; repeated business writes require a separate durable protection. Best-effort deduplication improves responsiveness. Stronger operation state requires ownership tokens, durable result identity, and explicit timeout/retry semantics. Make a model turn outlast the service request TTL and waiter deadline. Count model executions separately from durable business effects."},{"id":169,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"How should deadlocks, lock timeouts, and serialization failures be handled?","text":"How should deadlocks, lock timeouts, and serialization failures be handled? Acquire shared resources in a consistent order, keep transactions short, bound waiting, and retry only the whole appropriate transactional unit when the database reports a retryable conflict. Reusing a partially failed session or blindly retrying external effects is unsafe. Classify database errors and retry only an entire safely repeatable transaction under a bounded deadline. Preserve the business operation key and avoid retrying partial side effects blindly. Retrying a transaction can repeat already-sent emails or model calls if they occur inside the unit. Outbox writes let the database retry without repeating external delivery synchronously. Retries improve recovery while increasing tail latency. An overloaded system needs admission control, not only more retry attempts. Create a two-row opposite-order locking exercise in a disposable database. Record the failure class and retry the complete unit with a bounded policy."},{"id":170,"slug":"database-concurrency","chapter":"Transactions, locks, and concurrency","title":"How do you test concurrency without relying on random sleeps?","text":"How do you test concurrency without relying on random sleeps? Use controlled barriers or events to pause workers at known boundaries, then force a specific interleaving. Assert durable state and ownership outcomes, not only response codes. Use a real PostgreSQL instance for lock and isolation service requests. Use barriers or explicit synchronization to control the interleaving. Assert final rows and operation outcomes after takeover, duplicate confirmation, deadlock recovery, and stale-worker completion attempts. A test that runs two coroutines on one session may test an invalid integration rather than a real race. An in-memory SQLite database cannot establish PostgreSQL row-lock semantics. Deterministic integration tests take setup but produce stronger evidence and fewer flaky timing assumptions. Broad stress tests complement them and do not replace them. Specify barriers for two confirmations, two job service requests, and two session-state updates. State the invariant each test could falsify."},{"id":171,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"What does least privilege mean for application, migration, and reporting database roles?","text":"What does least privilege mean for application, migration, and reporting database roles? Use separate credentials for runtime queries, schema migration, and analysis. The application role should have only the tables and operations its workload needs; it should not need unrestricted DDL or superuser access. Read-only reporting still needs row and data-disclosure controls. Provision separate application, migration, reporting, and worker roles. Verify actual grants and keep credentials outside model context; sharing one connection string does not establish least privilege. A single powerful credential turns a query injection, tool bug, or compromised worker into a broader database incident. A model must never receive that credential or arbitrary SQL authority. Separate roles require grants and migration coordination but reduce blast radius. One admin credential is convenient locally and inappropriate as evidence of production isolation. Design a grant matrix for API, embedding worker, notification worker, migrator, and analyst. Include which sensitive tables each must never read."},{"id":172,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"How could PostgreSQL Row-Level Security complement owner-scoped queries?","text":"How could PostgreSQL Row-Level Security complement owner-scoped queries? RLS applies database policies to rows readable or writable through a role. It can add a second barrier when an application query forgets owner filtering. Its protection depends on actual role grants, bypass behavior, identity propagation, and tested policies. Enable and test RLS under the actual non-bypass role. Bind identity transaction-locally, verify pooled connection reuse, and understand owner or administrative bypass behavior. Table owners or privileged roles can bypass knowledge rules under documented conditions. Connection reuse can leak session-level identity if it is not reset; transaction-local settings and pool tests matter. Database enforcement strengthens defense in depth but complicates jobs, migrations, tests, and administrative workflows. It does not replace server authentication. In a disposable schema, create a transaction-local owner setting and policy. Test two users through reused pooled connections and verify writes as well as reads."},{"id":173,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"Why are parameterized values insufficient for arbitrary query generation?","text":"Why are parameterized values insufficient for arbitrary query generation? Binding values prevents them from being interpreted as SQL syntax in the intended parameter position. It does not make arbitrary table names, operators, SQL fragments, or model-generated statements safe. Query structure needs a trusted construction path and an allowlisted operation surface. Parameterize values and allowlist identifiers, operators, and query shapes. Arbitrary generated SQL remains a privilege boundary even when some values use bound parameters. An LLM-generated SELECT can still disclose unauthorized rows, invoke expensive functions, or exploit permissions even if it contains no classic string-concatenation injection. Narrow domain tools limit exploratory flexibility and support predictable authorization. A natural-language analytics feature needs an independently constrained read model and query policy. Design an exact aggregate tool for a user's service requests using typed inputs. Explain why it is safer than giving the model a raw database connection."},{"id":174,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"How do database credentials and TLS fit into the trust model?","text":"How do database credentials and TLS fit into the trust model? Credentials authenticate a process to the database; TLS protects transport and can verify server identity. Neither automatically limits which rows the process may read. Rotate credentials, use appropriate secret storage, and validate certificate settings in the actual deployment. Verify server identity through the configured TLS policy, rotate credentials, and restrict network access. A reachable encrypted connection still needs resource authorization and least privilege. Printing URLs or raw driver errors can expose passwords or infrastructure details. Copying secrets into model context, traces, sample files, or shell arguments creates additional disclosure paths. Managed identities or short-lived credentials reduce secret lifetime where supported, while adding integration requirements. Static credentials are simpler and need deliberate rotation and incident response. Create a secret-free deployment evidence record showing connection identity, TLS verification mode, role grants, and rotation ownership. Do not include the secret value."},{"id":175,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"Which timeouts protect database availability?","text":"Which timeouts protect database availability? Bound pool checkout, connection establishment, statement execution, lock waits, and idle transactions according to the workload. These limits solve different problems. An HTTP timeout alone may leave server-side queries or open transactions consuming resources. Set connection, statement, lock, and transaction-duration limits according to workload. Cancel abandoned work and keep application deadlines shorter than an uncontrolled sequence of downstream waits. An expensive permitted query can be a denial-of-service vector. Very short timeouts can also cause retry amplification or repeatedly abort useful work. Tighter limits improve isolation and can reduce completion rates. Use separate knowledge rules for interactive search, migrations, and backfills instead of one global number. Propose settings for pool wait, lock wait, and statement time with a measured request budget. Simulate a blocked transaction and observe rollback and connection reuse."},{"id":176,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"What is the difference between secure deletion, logical revocation, and retention?","text":"What is the difference between secure deletion, logical revocation, and retention? Revocation stops authorized disclosure. Logical deletion changes application visibility. Physical deletion removes stored copies, subject to backups and retention. Service records, embeddings, pending payloads, conversations, traces, and notifications can each retain different copies of the same private facts. Distinguish removing search visibility from deleting retained originals, embeddings, caches, logs, and backups. Define which lifecycle obligation applies to every derived artifact. Deleting a relational row may leave vectors, trace payloads, exports, or historical messages accessible. Conversely, deleting everything indiscriminately can violate a required evidence-retention policy; the applicable policy must be established outside this engineering guide. Purpose-specific retention minimizes unnecessary copies while preserving required records. One indefinite retention setting is easy and accumulates disclosure risk. Draw a data inventory for a single claim description and specify revocation, retention, deletion, and verification owners for every copy."},{"id":177,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"Is an append-only audit table automatically tamper-proof and complete?","text":"Is an append-only audit table automatically tamper-proof and complete? No. Append-only is an application behavior unless database privileges and external retention enforce it. Completeness depends on what is recorded and what happens when recording fails. Trace data and business audit records also have different reliability requirements. Give required audit events transactional or explicitly durable delivery semantics. Append-only application behavior does not prevent privileged database changes, and a failed transaction needs rollback or a savepoint. A missing trail can matter during an investigation. Also, swallowing a failed SQL statement does not by itself restore a PostgreSQL transaction; savepoint and rollback behavior require testing. Best-effort audit favors availability. Mandatory audit or a transactional audit outbox provides stronger completeness at the cost of possible business-operation failure or more infrastructure. Inject an audit insertion failure and inspect the surrounding transaction. Define which actions must fail closed if audit cannot be guaranteed."},{"id":178,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"How should backup and point-in-time recovery be tested for an AI application?","text":"How should backup and point-in-time recovery be tested for an AI application? Restore the relational database and verify business records, ownership, migrations, outbox state, and knowledge versions. Then decide whether vector projections should be restored or rebuilt. The recovery objective concerns a coherent application state, not only a successful backup command. Restore a known fixture into an isolated environment and verify authoritative records, outbox work, derived indexes, credentials, and access policy. Measure recovery time and actual recoverable data loss. Replaying old notification or submission jobs can duplicate effects. A backup also contains private data and requires its own encryption, access, and retention policy. Rebuilding vectors reduces backup coupling and takes time and provider cost. Snapshotting every projection can accelerate recovery while making consistency coordination harder. Write a restore drill with a known claim, pending job, rule revision, and revoked user. Verify exact reads, search visibility, and safe job resumption."},{"id":179,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"Why separate operational metrics from database content exports?","text":"Why separate operational metrics from database content exports? Counts, durations, and sanitized error categories support capacity and health without automatically exporting claim text, customer identifiers, or confirmation tokens. Content exports need an explicit purpose and access policy rather than being a side effect of debugging. Export allowlisted operational metadata instead of whole records. Separate observability access from business-data access and review whether identifiers themselves reveal sensitive relationships. A credential-safe SQL logger can still expose PII through parameter values. Traces may be readable by a broader audience than the application database. Minimal telemetry reduces diagnosis detail; controlled replay fixtures and opt-in redacted samples can provide useful debugging with less disclosure. Trace one claim description and token through logs, tool spans, model callbacks, and telemetry endpoints. Record exactly what leaves the process."},{"id":180,"slug":"database-security","chapter":"Securing database operations","title":"How should database security be verified before adding agent autonomy?","text":"How should database security be verified before adding agent autonomy? Prove owner filtering, least-privilege credentials, constrained tools, input validation, timeout behavior, audit policy, and recovery using the actual storage setup. A more autonomous planner increases the number and variety of operations, so it should inherit a secure capability surface rather than broadening it. Test with the real application role and cross-tenant fixtures. Exercise denied reads and writes, pooled reuse, arbitrary-input rejection, backup access, and every non-agent administrative path. A framework demo may call internal functions directly and skip route dependencies. The same tool needs an identity context or service principal appropriate to its execution path. A capability-oriented service layer takes design work and supports safe reuse across orchestration frameworks. Direct ORM access inside every agent is fast to prototype and hard to govern. Build a deny-first test matrix for interactive users, background workers, and remote agents. Include actions each principal may read, prepare, confirm, or never perform."},{"id":181,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"How are JWTs validated and converted into database ownership?","text":"How are JWTs validated and converted into database ownership? Validate the token under an explicit issuer, audience, algorithm, and lifetime profile, resolve the subject to a trusted application principal, and authorize access to each resource. A model-generated owner identifier cannot replace authenticated identity. Validate signature, allowed algorithm, issuer, audience, expiry, and required claims under the token profile. Resolve the trusted principal and enforce resource ownership in services, not model arguments. A valid token may remain usable after access should be revoked unless the product checks revocation or session versions. Choose lifetime and refresh behavior from the threat model. Stateless token checks scale well, while immediate revocation needs additional state or shorter-lived credentials. Database user lookup already provides one current-state validation point. Test expired, malformed, deleted-user, wrong-signature, and valid tokens. Verify that a known request ID still requires owner authorization."},{"id":182,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"Why are HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite, CORS, and CSRF different controls?","text":"Why are HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite, CORS, and CSRF different controls? HttpOnly limits script access to cookies, Secure limits cookie transport to HTTPS, and SameSite constrains some cross-site sending. CORS controls browser access across origins. CSRF protection addresses unwanted state-changing requests made with ambient credentials. None is interchangeable with user authorization. Set cookie and origin policies explicitly for the deployment. Review state-changing routes for CSRF and XSS exposure; CORS, SameSite, HttpOnly, and Secure address different browser behaviors. These settings do not establish a complete CSRF review of all mutations and deployment origins. XSS can still issue authorized actions even when it cannot read an HttpOnly cookie. Cookie authentication simplifies browser sessions but requires deliberate origin and state-change policy. Bearer clients have different storage and transport concerns. Build a matrix for sign-in, confirmation, cancellation, and logout across trusted and untrusted origins. State which server-side checks enforce each result."},{"id":183,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"How does ContextVar prevent cross-request identity leakage?","text":"How does ContextVar prevent cross-request identity leakage? ContextVar associates a value with an asynchronous execution context rather than one mutable process-global variable. Concurrent requests can therefore carry different identities through await boundaries. It is a context propagation mechanism, not an authorization decision or durable identity store. Bind task-local identity at the authenticated boundary and reset it in a finally block. A background task must receive a deliberate identity rather than rely on accidental inherited context. Capturing context for a task that outlives a request can retain stale identity. Failing to reset temporary bindings can also confuse tests or reused execution contexts. Server code must define where identity begins and ends. Implicit context reduces parameter threading and can hide dependencies. Explicit identity parameters improve local clarity and require more signatures. Run two concurrent tool calls under separate context bindings. Test missing identity and an outliving background task; verify both fail or receive an explicit service identity as intended."},{"id":184,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"Why must case ownership be checked in tools as well as routes?","text":"Why must case ownership be checked in tools as well as routes? Agent tools may execute through a runner, test harness, or future remote protocol without passing through a particular HTTP route. A protected route cannot be the only place a domain operation learns whose data it may access. Authorize the resource inside the service called by every adapter. A protected chat endpoint cannot make a later model-selected identifier trustworthy. A model-supplied user_id is untrusted even if it is a valid UUID. A future LangChain adapter must not expose that internal parameter as a tool argument the model can freely choose. Repeated enforcement at domain boundaries adds code and protects alternate callers. Duplicated policy implementations can drift, so centralize shared service authorization where possible. Invoke the service request-search tool directly without an HTTP request and with the wrong user context. Verify that knowing another user's request ID does not reveal it."},{"id":185,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"What is the distinction between RBAC and resource ownership here?","text":"What is the distinction between RBAC and resource ownership here? Role-Based Access Control determines whether a principal may perform a category of action; ownership determines which records that principal may affect. A support operator role does not automatically justify unrestricted access to every customer's service requests. Use roles for permitted categories of action and ownership for access to a particular resource. Check both when a privileged operation also targets tenant-scoped data. Replacing the allowlist with a broad role check without resource policy can overgrant authority. Tests that widen the allowlist for fixtures do not prove production administration is configured. Combining role and attribute checks is more expressive and more complex. A deny-by-default seam is safer than silently pretending a missing administrative policy exists. Define reader, customer, handler, and administrator actions with both role and record-scope rules. Map each rule to its actual enforcement point."},{"id":186,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"Why does password hashing require more than naming Argon2?","text":"Why does password hashing require more than naming Argon2? A password hash uses intentionally costly computation and salt to resist offline guessing. Operational parameters, input limits, verification errors, rehash policy, and login rate limiting matter too. A hash algorithm name alone does not establish timing-safe user enumeration resistance. Use a valid dummy hash with a reviewed work factor for unknown accounts. Compare timing distributions under load and keep authentication errors consistent without suppressing operational diagnostics. Malformed dummy verification can return faster than a real check. Unbounded password input or excessive concurrent hashing can also consume resources before the agent is involved. Higher hashing cost protects stolen hashes and increases authentication resource use. Choose measured parameters and a valid equivalent dummy path. Benchmark known-user wrong-password and unknown-user checks with reviewed test data. Verify hash validity and compare distributions without asserting perfect constant time."},{"id":187,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"How should confirmation tokens be treated as capabilities?","text":"How should confirmation tokens be treated as capabilities? A confirmation token identifies a specific prepared action and should be bound to the user, payload, pending state, and expiry. Possession alone should not bypass authentication. Material edits should produce a new reviewed operation and invalidate or supersede the old one server-side. Bind confirmation to owner, exact parameters, revision, expiry, and pending state. Supersede earlier approvals explicitly after material changes and reject reused completed tokens. A model's statement cannot revoke a still-pending token. Some preparation logs also include tokens, increasing the audience that may see them even though owner binding remains required. Server-enforced supersession adds lifecycle bookkeeping and prevents stale confirmation. Keeping multiple live preparations is simpler but needs an honest user-visible model. Prepare an amount, correct it, then try both tokens. Document current behavior and specify the desired supersession invariant."},{"id":188,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"Why must rate limiting, request-size limits, and tool budgets coexist?","text":"Why must rate limiting, request-size limits, and tool budgets coexist? Request rate bounds arrival frequency, size limits bound per-request input, and tool or token budgets bound work initiated by one accepted request. An agent can turn one small request into many expensive provider calls, so requests per minute alone do not control cost. Apply limits at request, run, model, and tool boundaries. Account for nested calls and retries so one accepted HTTP request cannot expand into unbounded downstream work. Fail-open Redis behavior preserves availability and can weaken fleet-wide limits during an outage. Model-generated loops may also stay within HTTP request limits while exhausting provider budgets. Layered budgets increase configuration and improve predictable resource use. Tight limits can interrupt legitimate multi-intent conversations and need explicit completion states. Estimate worst-case cost from maximum turns, tool calls, retries, candidates, and output tokens. Test overload without using real private service requests."},{"id":189,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"How can error responses leak information even when access is denied?","text":"How can error responses leak information even when access is denied? Different messages, status codes, timings, trace payloads, and raw exception strings can reveal record existence or internal details. A denial response should reveal only what the caller is entitled to learn, while internal diagnostics remain appropriately protected. Return appropriately indistinguishable not-found or inaccessible responses when disclosure policy requires it. Keep private diagnostic detail in protected logs rather than client exception text. A model may repeat an internal error returned by a tool. Sanitizing only the final HTTP exception leaves tool results and streaming error events as additional channels. Generic external messages reduce detail for users and support; structured internal error categories preserve diagnosis without exposing private values. Compare responses for unknown and foreign-owned request IDs, invalid tokens, provider failures, and database errors. Inspect both the streamed events and final response."},{"id":190,"slug":"api-security","chapter":"API identity and application security","title":"What does an effective threat-model review produce?","text":"What does an effective threat-model review produce? It produces a concrete asset and actor map, trust boundaries, abuse cases, enforcement points, and tests. For this product, include private claim text, confirmation tokens, knowledge versions, prompt data, provider keys, session history, and traces. List assets, actors, entry points, trust boundaries, and forbidden effects. Convert identified threats into owners, controls, and observable acceptance tests rather than a generic security checklist. A checklist detached from actual call sites can certify controls that are merely configured or documented. Threat modeling should include alternate paths such as evaluation, administration, scripts, and workers. A focused model tied to major workflows is maintainable. An exhaustive speculative catalog can consume time without improving concrete denial behavior. Pick cross-user disclosure, duplicate submission, and malicious-source instructions. For each, identify the earliest reliable enforcement point and a test proving denial."},{"id":191,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"What makes this an agent rather than a fixed retrieval pipeline?","text":"What makes this an agent rather than a fixed retrieval pipeline? A model can select tools, inspect results, and decide whether to continue before answering. A fixed pipeline executes a predetermined path for a known request shape. The example agent has bounded domain capabilities but flexible intent routing and multi-tool turns. An agent chooses its next permitted action from observations; a fixed pipeline follows application-defined stages. Use model discretion only where it improves the task under measured constraints. Flexible selection introduces wrong-tool, repeated-tool, and premature-answer failures beyond retrieval quality. Tool availability should reflect server authorization, not only instructions saying which tools are appropriate. Agents handle conversational ambiguity and multiple intents while requiring trajectory evaluation and execution limits. A deterministic route remains preferable for exact confirmation and lifecycle mutation. Compare a fixed policy-search endpoint with an ADK turn asking both a coverage and request-status question. Identify the additional decisions the agent makes."},{"id":192,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"How does the tool registry become a model-facing contract?","text":"How does the tool registry become a model-facing contract? The registry returns callable handlers. Their signatures and docstrings inform tool declarations; registry metadata and human descriptions are not automatically security enforcement or the exact text the model sees. Define small tools with precise descriptions and typed inputs. Bind trusted identity outside model arguments and preserve business validation inside the service behind the tool. Adding requires_auth metadata does not prove authentication runs. Wrappers that alter signatures can also accidentally expose internal configuration or remove required field information. Function-derived tools are convenient and reduce duplicated schemas. They require disciplined annotations, docstrings, wrapper preservation, and server validation. Inspect the generated declaration of prepare_request and compare it with a tool specification. Verify that owner identity is absent from model-controlled arguments."},{"id":193,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"Why preserve function signatures when instrumenting tools?","text":"Why preserve function signatures when instrumenting tools? Agent runtimes introspect callable signatures and annotations to construct schemas. A tracing wrapper can add internal parameters or change annotation resolution, causing the model to see a contract the underlying function does not support. Instrumentation wrappers should preserve callable metadata used for schema generation. Verify the resulting advertised tool contract and avoid double wrapping or duplicate spans. A wrapper that preserves results but changes the model declaration can still break behavior. Likewise, exception-only success recording can classify a returned error payload as a successful call. Transparent instrumentation needs careful compatibility tests but keeps business tools vendor-neutral. Hand-instrumenting every function is simpler locally and prone to inconsistent coverage. Compare inspect.signature and generated schema before and after instrumentation. Test both a raised exception and a returned success=False payload."},{"id":194,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"What does LiteLLM abstract, and what remains provider-specific?","text":"What does LiteLLM abstract, and what remains provider-specific? LiteLLM offers a common access surface for different model providers. Provider capabilities still vary in tool calling, structured output, token accounting, streaming, pricing, context windows, and error behavior. A common adapter does not make all models behaviorally interchangeable. Treat the adapter as a compatibility layer rather than proof of identical provider behavior. Record the actual model and test tool use, structured output, cancellation, and usage reporting. A provider switch can change tool selection or output schemas without a Python type error. The example temperature override uses truthiness, so an explicit zero does not follow the same branch as a positive configured value. Routing flexibility reduces provider coupling while expanding the compatibility matrix. A single well-tested provider can be a sound early production choice. Compare two providers on the same safe tool trajectories and output contracts. Test temperature zero separately from an omitted value."},{"id":195,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"How should agent memory be bounded and rehydrated?","text":"How should agent memory be bounded and rehydrated? Retain enough recent user, assistant, and tool context to resolve references, while bounding replay and clearly separating temporary invocation state from persistent session state. Long history is not automatically useful context and may contain obsolete instructions or private data. Select relevant recent history and validated working state under a budget. Rehydrate from authorized conversation records and retain durable operation identities across compaction. A lossy summary can forget corrected facts, retain an obsolete confirmation token, or misrepresent a previous tool failure. Message order and tool-result pairing need explicit tests. Compaction reduces tokens and latency while losing detail. Retrieving structured prior facts can be more reliable for critical values than trusting an unconstrained summary. Create a conversation that corrects an amount, cancels a preparation, and returns later. Verify memory does not revive the old action."},{"id":196,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"Does a database-backed session service guarantee cross-replica continuity?","text":"Does a database-backed session service guarantee cross-replica continuity? It provides shared stored events and state when configured and healthy. Continuity also requires stable user/session resolution, compatible serialization, correct ordering, migration ownership, and a concurrency policy for overlapping turns. Durable storage alone does not serialize reasoning. Choose a shared durable session store when cross-replica continuity is required. Test concurrent updates, message order, ownership, restart recovery, and migration rather than assuming persistence solves all continuity. Silent fallback can make one replica forget or diverge from another while health appears generally available. Runtime-created session tables also sit outside the ordinary Alembic ownership model. Fallback preserves some availability and weakens continuity. Production may need fail-closed readiness when shared conversation state is a required guarantee. Start two replicas with explicit database sessions, then simulate storage initialization failure. Inspect the effective backend and continuity behavior."},{"id":197,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"Why distinguish a returned tool error from a raised exception?","text":"Why distinguish a returned tool error from a raised exception? A tool can complete its Python call while returning found=False, success=False, or an error field. Those are domain outcomes, not necessarily exceptions. The agent, stream mapper, telemetry, and evaluation need a shared interpretation of those payloads. Record transport success, function completion, and business outcome separately. A normal returned value can describe rejection or unavailability and must not become a successful task metric. Reporting every returned dictionary as success inflates reliability metrics. Treating every no-result as an error also misleads the user and can trigger unnecessary retries. Structured result types require more explicit schemas and simplify downstream interpretation. Free-form dictionaries are flexible and can accumulate incompatible conventions. Define expected stream and metric outcomes for found=False, empty search, validation_errors, backend error, and a raised exception."},{"id":198,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"Why must a human handoff be more than a sentence in a prompt?","text":"Why must a human handoff be more than a sentence in a prompt? A handoff needs an actual destination, case or ticket identity, permitted context transfer, status, and recovery if delivery fails. Saying that a specialist is being connected does not establish that any operational transfer occurred. Create a real handoff record through a queue or case service. Report assignment state honestly and preserve a fallback when escalation infrastructure is unavailable. Users may believe a dispute or urgent request is being handled when the system only generated text. Evaluation should distinguish an escalation recommendation from a completed transfer. A real handoff integration adds external dependencies and privacy choices. An honest support-contact response is simpler and preferable to an unsupported promise. Simulate a dispute and inspect all tool events. Specify a proposed escalation tool with success, pending, failed, and authorized-payload contracts."},{"id":199,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"How should agent execution be bounded?","text":"How should agent execution be bounded? Bound model calls, tool calls, wall time, tokens, retries, and repeated identical actions. Use deterministic server checks for irreversible operations. A prompt asking the model not to repeat a tool is helpful guidance but not a hard execution ceiling. Use a shared call, cost, and deadline budget for the whole task. Bound retries and repeated equivalent actions, and propagate cancellation through providers and tools. A looping agent can accumulate cost or keep a stream open while request-rate limits remain satisfied. A process-level cancellation may not stop already-started provider or worker operations. Tight limits improve predictability and can interrupt complex legitimate requests. Explicit partial-completion and escalation states preserve useful progress. Use a fake model repeatedly requesting the same tool. Verify the host stops within a declared time and count budget and returns a truthful incomplete status."},{"id":200,"slug":"adk-agents","chapter":"ADK agents, tools, and memory","title":"How can deterministic tools and flexible reasoning coexist safely?","text":"How can deterministic tools and flexible reasoning coexist safely? Let the model interpret requests and propose a narrow operation; let deterministic validation, authorization, and transaction code decide whether and how that operation takes effect. The model's narrative should describe the authoritative result rather than manufacture it. Keep flexible intent resolution outside deterministic authorization and transaction logic. The same narrow business service should enforce its rules regardless of the orchestration framework. A future tool that directly approves service requests or writes arbitrary status values would bypass the example authority model. Pattern consistency matters more than the orchestrator's brand. Narrow tools reduce open-ended autonomy but make actions auditable and testable. High-autonomy systems need equally strong capability and approval boundaries. Design a proposed handler-assistance tool that recommends a decision without committing one. State who may approve it and which exact record becomes authoritative."},{"id":201,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"What would LangChain replace or add in this application?","text":"What would LangChain replace or add in this application? LangChain can supply model, message, tool, middleware, structured-output, and agent-building abstractions. Adopting its agent factory would replace part of the example ADK orchestration, not PostgreSQL, the retrieval service, domain validation, or the confirmation transaction. Wrap existing services through compatible tool interfaces and retain business ownership and transactions. Compare the runtime with the baseline using identical application tasks and measured integration cost. Installing a framework without deciding who owns sessions, tool errors, and streaming can create two overlapping runtimes. A dependency change alone does not migrate behavioral contracts. LangChain offers a broad integration surface; staying with ADK avoids migration cost. Choose based on a demonstrated need and an equivalent behavioral benchmark. Map each current ADK constructor argument to the proposed LangChain composition. Identify the contracts that should remain unchanged."},{"id":202,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"What are messages, chat models, and invocation methods?","text":"What are messages, chat models, and invocation methods? Messages represent roles, text, tool calls, and tool results. A chat model consumes a message sequence and produces model output. Invocation, streaming, and batch methods are execution interfaces, not different authorization knowledge rules or independent guarantees of model quality. Inject a compatible model interface and record provider-specific capabilities. A common constructor does not guarantee identical tool, streaming, or structured-output behavior. Dropping a tool result, role boundary, or corrected user value can change behavior. A model supporting text may not support the tool or structured-output features the application requires. Standard interfaces ease experimentation while provider capabilities still require testing. A single-provider direct adapter can be smaller for a narrow workload. Translate a user question, tool call, tool result, and assistant answer into an explicit message sequence and verify the pairing identifiers."},{"id":203,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"How can an existing domain service become a LangChain tool?","text":"How can an existing domain service become a LangChain tool? Wrap the existing async domain function with a narrow typed tool whose arguments contain only user-searchable input. Resolve trusted identity in server context and retain the domain function's validation and owner filters. The wrapper should not expose internal session or owner parameters to the model. Expose a narrow model-facing function that calls an authorized service with trusted runtime context. Keep internal owner and tenant arguments out of user-editable tool schemas. A convenient wrapper accepting user_id from the model defeats the example scoping boundary. Context setup and reset must be defined for HTTP, evaluation, and background execution. Thin adapters reuse tested business logic and add a small compatibility surface. Rewriting tools inside each framework risks policy drift. Inspect the proposed read-only policy wrapper in the worked examples. Add a service requests wrapper that obtains identity from a trusted service context."},{"id":204,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"How do runnable composition and an agent loop differ?","text":"How do runnable composition and an agent loop differ? A composed chain follows a known sequence such as retrieve, assemble, and answer. An agent loop lets a model choose tools and continuation. Both can use the same model and retriever interfaces, but they create different testing and cost requirements. Use fixed composition when stage order is known; use an agent loop when tool selection depends on observations. Evaluate the extra flexibility against latency and failure complexity. Calling a fixed chain an agent can hide the absence of planning; calling an agent a simple chain can hide repeated calls and nondeterministic routing. Name the actual control flow. Fixed composition is easier to bound and diagnose; flexible routing handles ambiguity. Start from the least flexible flow that meets the requirement. Build a deterministic retrieval-and-answer baseline and compare it with agent orchestration on single-intent and multi-intent cases."},{"id":205,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"What does structured output validate, and what does it not validate?","text":"What does structured output validate, and what does it not validate? A structured schema constrains the form and types of a model result, such as an answer with citation IDs and a status. It does not prove the citations exist, the amounts match policy text, or the caller may execute a proposed action. Choose a compatible structured-output strategy, parse the result, and run domain validation. Schema compliance does not establish factual correctness or action authority. A valid JSON decision saying approved is still not an authorized claim transition. Schema conformance can otherwise create a false impression of factual or business correctness. Typed outputs simplify UI integration and require explicit parse, retry, and semantic-validation branches. Free-form prose is flexible and harder to validate reliably. Define a recommendation schema with status, cited evidence IDs, missing facts, and explanation. Reject unknown citations before presentation."},{"id":206,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"What belongs in middleware instead of a business tool?","text":"What belongs in middleware instead of a business tool? Cross-cutting knowledge rules such as call budgets, model selection, standardized error mapping, and context preparation can sit around execution. Business tools should still own their domain authorization, validation, and transaction behavior. Use middleware for cross-cutting policy, budget, logging, and context transformations. Keep business validation in application services so non-agent callers enforce the same invariants. A middleware-only authorization check can be bypassed by direct service calls. Overly broad retry middleware can also repeat side effects whose business idempotency is not established. Central hooks reduce repetition while increasing execution-order and wrapper-compatibility complexity. Keep critical invariants enforceable independently of orchestration hooks. Classify budget checking, owner filtering, signature preservation, and pending-token consumption by the layer that must enforce each one."},{"id":207,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"How should short-term memory map to support conversations?","text":"How should short-term memory map to support conversations? Short-term memory stores a thread's evolving conversational context. It needs a stable thread identity and a persistence policy. Long-term customer facts, authoritative service requests, and pending confirmation state should remain in domain storage rather than only in a message buffer. Map conversation and thread ownership explicitly, define retention and compaction, and test concurrent updates. Framework memory is neither a business ledger nor a universal persistence contract. Reusing thread IDs across users or forgetting ownership checks can disclose history. Memory summaries can also retain obsolete tokens or knowledge facts after a correction. Persistent memory improves continuity and increases retention obligations. Stateless turns simplify privacy and require more explicit context from the user. Design a user-and-conversation thread key and a migration test for archived history, corrected facts, and sign-out followed by another user."},{"id":208,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"How should LangChain streams be adapted to the existing UI contract?","text":"How should LangChain streams be adapted to the existing UI contract? Translate framework-specific chunks into the application's typed events rather than exposing raw runtime objects. Preserve text deltas, tool activity, errors, persisted message IDs, and a truthful terminal status. Translate framework events into a stable user-safe schema. Preserve task identity, event order, cancellation, errors, and persistence-before-completion semantics across adapters. A tool error or cancelled stream can otherwise be mislabeled complete. Forwarding arbitrary runtime state may leak internal or private fields. A stable application event layer costs adapter code and decouples UI releases from framework changes. Raw framework streams are faster to demo and fragile to evolve. Feed equivalent synthetic ADK and LangChain event sequences into two adapters and compare the resulting public events."},{"id":209,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"Are framework guardrails a replacement for deterministic permissions?","text":"Are framework guardrails a replacement for deterministic permissions? No. Guardrails can classify inputs, validate outputs, or interrupt unsafe model behavior, but backend authorization must still deny prohibited reads and writes. A classifier can be wrong or unavailable, and a prompt can be ignored. Validate both proposed arguments and actual service outcomes. A tool description improves selection but cannot prevent a model from proposing unauthorized or invalid operations. A content-safety pass does not certify that a customer may access another claim or approve a payout. Combining unrelated notions of safety into one boolean hides authority errors. Guardrails add useful signals and latency. Deploy them as complementary checks with explicit error behavior, not as the sole permission system. Make a simulated model issue an unauthorized operation after a guardrail reports safe text. Verify the service still denies it."},{"id":210,"slug":"langchain","chapter":"LangChain as an integration alternative","title":"How would you decide whether a runtime-to-LangChain migration is worthwhile?","text":"How would you decide whether a runtime-to-LangChain migration is worthwhile? Compare required integrations, operational complexity, framework support, team familiarity, migration risk, and measured behavior. Require parity on tool routing, scope enforcement, confirmation, streaming, memory, evaluation, and tracing before changing the production runtime. Compare routing, scope enforcement, approval, memory, streaming, recovery, and trace privacy on the same fixtures. Preserve product contracts or explicitly redesign them with migration and rollback evidence. Benchmarking only a successful coverage response misses multi-turn correction, tool failure, and duplicate writes. New abstractions can also change trace payload disclosure. A better-fit ecosystem can justify the cost; keeping the example stack may be more valuable when problems are domain contracts rather than orchestration limitations. Write an architecture decision record with one concrete unmet need, a parity checklist, a limited experiment, and a rollback condition."},{"id":211,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"What problem would LangGraph solve that a basic agent loop does not?","text":"What problem would LangGraph solve that a basic agent loop does not? LangGraph expresses a workflow as state, nodes, and transitions, with explicit branching and persistence options. It is useful when retries, human review, resumability, and bounded stages should be visible in the orchestration design rather than left entirely to a model loop. Represent explicit state transitions when the workflow needs branching, checkpoints, review, or recovery. Keep a simple agent loop when those requirements do not justify a graph. A graph diagram can suggest stronger guarantees than its nodes implement. Domain writes still need their own transaction and permission boundaries. Explicit control improves inspectability while adding state schema and orchestration code. A simple tool-calling loop can remain sufficient for narrow conversational tasks. Draw a proposed eligibility-review graph with answerable, missing-facts, unavailable-backend, and escalation branches before writing nodes."},{"id":212,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"How do StateGraph state, nodes, and conditional edges work together?","text":"How do StateGraph state, nodes, and conditional edges work together? State is the workflow's shared data contract; nodes compute updates; edges choose what executes next. A node can be deterministic code or a model call. Conditional routing should use validated state rather than treating arbitrary generated text as an unrestricted next-node name. Define typed state with clear ownership, transient fields, durable references, and terminal outcomes. Store large artifacts separately and keep versioned IDs inside graph state. Overloaded state can retain private text, credentials, or stale authorization. Input schema shape alone does not decide what a stream or trace may disclose. Explicit state improves testing and requires careful versioning. A dictionary with unrestricted keys is flexible and harder to migrate or secure. Separate public output fields, internal evidence references, and trusted identity in the proposed graph contract."},{"id":213,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"Why do reducers matter when graph nodes run in parallel?","text":"Why do reducers matter when graph nodes run in parallel? A reducer defines how multiple updates to a state field combine. Replacing a value, appending messages, and merging keyed results are different semantics. Parallel branches need a declared merge rule rather than assuming the last writer is correct. Give parallel updates explicit merge semantics through compatible reducers. Decide whether lists append, records replace, or conflicts fail; persistence alone does not define a safe merge. An append reducer can duplicate retries, and a merge can combine evidence from incompatible revisions or scopes. A reducer is not a distributed database transaction. Explicit merge rules support parallel work and require identity, ordering, and replay discipline. Sequential nodes avoid some conflicts at the cost of latency. Design a reducer for evidence keyed by source, revision, and chunk ID. Show how repeated delivery avoids duplicating the same item."},{"id":214,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"What is a checkpoint, and how is it different from a business commit?","text":"What is a checkpoint, and how is it different from a business commit? A checkpoint stores workflow state so execution can resume or be inspected. A business commit makes an authoritative business transition durable. A checkpoint can refer to a completed operation, but saving it does not atomically commit another service's database transaction. Treat checkpoints as workflow progress records. Reconcile them with authoritative operation status after crashes, because checkpoint persistence and a separate business transaction may not commit together. On resume, a node may retry an already-completed side effect. An in-memory saver demonstrates state handling only within its lifetime and is not a durable production guarantee. Durable checkpoints support recovery and require storage, retention, access policy, and schema evolution. Pure in-memory graphs are easier for local experiments. Draw a crash after business commit but before checkpoint save. Specify how an operation ID lets the resumed node discover the existing result."},{"id":215,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"How does a human-review interrupt differ from authorization?","text":"How does a human-review interrupt differ from authorization? An interrupt pauses workflow progress and exposes a question or proposed action for review. Authorization determines whether the identity resuming it is permitted to approve that exact action. A resume payload saying approved is untrusted unless the server validates its actor and scope. Pause execution for input or review, then validate the resumed principal and approval against current business state. A resume command is control flow, not authorization. Guessable or cross-user thread access can resume another person's workflow. Material edits after review can invalidate what was approved even if the resume token is technically valid. Human pauses reduce accidental actions while adding persistence and expiry management. For low-risk reads they may be unnecessary. Bind a proposed review to user, payload hash, operation ID, and expiry. Test an unauthorized resume and a changed amount."},{"id":216,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"Why must code before an interrupt or retry be safe to execute again?","text":"Why must code before an interrupt or retry be safe to execute again? Resuming or recovering a workflow may re-enter node code. Work performed before the pause can therefore repeat. Deterministic computation is usually safe; sending a notification or creating a service request needs a durable idempotent operation boundary. Put nondeterministic and side-effecting work behind replay-safe boundaries. Use durable operation keys and test the crash window after an effect but before checkpoint completion. Repeating a preparation can leave multiple live tokens; repeating an external notification can confuse the user. A cached Python return value is not enough across process crashes. Smaller nodes with explicit side-effect tasks improve replay reasoning and add orchestration detail. One large node is shorter and harder to recover safely. Put a harmless counter and a mock external action before a teaching interrupt. Observe replay, then move the action behind an idempotent service contract."},{"id":217,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"How should graph retries and timeouts be layered?","text":"How should graph retries and timeouts be layered? Retry only failures likely to recover, with bounded attempts and an overall deadline. A node retry, provider retry, database retry, and client retry can multiply each other. Track the full operation budget and avoid retrying invalid authorization or schema errors. Layer node retries, provider retries, and task deadlines deliberately. Count all attempts in one budget and classify business rejection separately from transient infrastructure failure. Nested retries amplify load and can execute write nodes repeatedly. A model timeout also does not prove the provider or remote agent stopped processing. Local resilience helps transient failures, while a simpler centralized budget is easier to reason about. Use stage-specific policy with one overall ceiling. Calculate worst-case attempts for two client retries, three node attempts, and four provider attempts. Replace that accidental multiplier with an explicit budget."},{"id":218,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"Can graph private state be streamed safely by default?","text":"Can graph private state be streamed safely by default? Do not assume a private schema is a redaction policy. A framework may stream internal state or custom events depending on mode. Define an application event projection that allowlists public fields and removes private evidence, identity, and credentials. Project only approved fields into public events. State needed for internal coordination can include private evidence, tool arguments, or identifiers that should not be streamed. Forwarding raw values or updates can expose claim details or internal analysis. A UI that hides a field visually has still received it over the network. Allowlisted projections take adapter work and preserve privacy across framework updates. Raw graph streaming is convenient for development diagnostics only under controlled access. Insert a synthetic private field into graph state and inspect every chosen stream mode. Verify the public SSE adapter never emits it."},{"id":219,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"How should graph state and checkpoints evolve across deployments?","text":"How should graph state and checkpoints evolve across deployments? Version state schemas and maintain readers or migrations for active checkpoints. A suspended service workflow may resume after code changes, so renamed fields, changed node semantics, and new authorization requirements need compatibility handling. Version checkpoint schemas and migrate or isolate old threads deliberately. Test resume with old artifacts after deployment, and prevent incompatible readers from silently interpreting changed state fields. A resumed old workflow can invoke a new write path with stale assumptions. Authorization should be re-evaluated at action time rather than frozen indefinitely in a checkpoint. Compatibility readers increase maintenance and preserve resumability. Expiring old workflows simplifies migration but must communicate how users restart safely. Design a v1-to-v2 checkpoint migration that adds rule revision and payload hash before confirmation. Specify what happens if the old evidence no longer exists."},{"id":220,"slug":"langgraph","chapter":"LangGraph stateful workflows","title":"When is LangGraph a better choice than ADK or a plain function?","text":"When is LangGraph a better choice than ADK or a plain function? Choose it when explicit state transitions, branching, durable pause/resume, or inspectable parallel work solve a real product requirement. Keep a plain function for deterministic small operations and consider retaining ADK when flexible tool routing already meets the need. Evaluate transitions, terminal outcomes, approvals, replay, and cost under realistic failures. A passing graph visualization or one happy-path invocation does not establish business reliability. Introducing a graph solely to draw a more impressive architecture can add persistence and deployment obligations without improving outcomes. More orchestration layers can also fragment traces and ownership. Explicit workflow control is valuable when recovery and review dominate. Simpler code is preferable when the path is short and has no durable pauses. Write a decision record for one proposed graph workflow and one operation you deliberately keep as a normal function. Defend both choices."},{"id":221,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"What is A2A, and when should a backend expose a remote agent?","text":"What is A2A, and when should a backend expose a remote agent? Agent2Agent describes interaction between independently hosted agents, including discovery, messages, tasks, and outputs. Use it when a remote specialist has a genuine service boundary rather than wrapping an ordinary deterministic lookup in another agent. A remote agent integration needs explicit client and server adapters, discovery, identity, task state, and a sharing policy. An agent SDK dependency does not create an operational endpoint. Calling two local functions agents is not proof of protocol interoperability. A diagram arrow labeled A2A must correspond to an implemented and version-tested transport contract. A2A can support organizational and runtime boundaries while adding network, identity, compatibility, and operational complexity. A local domain service is simpler when independent deployment is unnecessary. Identify one support capability that genuinely needs an independently operated agent and one that should remain an ordinary local tool."},{"id":222,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"How do A2A, MCP, and ordinary APIs differ?","text":"How do A2A, MCP, and ordinary APIs differ? A2A concerns agent-to-agent interactions and task-oriented collaboration. MCP commonly exposes tools and resources to model-enabled clients. A normal API can provide deterministic domain operations without claiming to be an autonomous peer. These interfaces can coexist but do not grant authority by themselves. Use MCP for AI-facing capability access, ordinary APIs for deterministic service operations, and A2A where remote agent task ownership is useful. Define who owns every durable effect. Treating a peer agent as a trusted internal function can bypass user scope or disclose entire conversations. Capability discovery is not consent to every requested action. Protocol choice affects interoperability and lifecycle semantics. Use the narrowest interface that meets the integration need rather than wrapping every endpoint in an agent protocol. Map policy search, request confirmation, long-running specialist review, and metrics export to appropriate interfaces and explain the decision."},{"id":223,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"What is an Agent Card, and why is it not an authorization token?","text":"What is an Agent Card, and why is it not an authorization token? An Agent Card describes an agent's identity, capabilities, skills, endpoint, and security requirements. Discovery helps a client select a compatible service. Credentials and authorization are separately validated when invoking protected operations. Treat published capability metadata as untrusted service input. Review the endpoint and supported contracts before sending scoped customer context or delegating a task. A malicious or stale card can point at an unintended endpoint or overstate capabilities. Do not send credentials or private claim data to arbitrary discovered destinations without trust and network policy. Dynamic discovery improves flexibility and adds authenticity, caching, and allowlist requirements. Explicitly configured peers are simpler for a small controlled deployment. Draft a secret-free capability description for a review-only specialist. List information that belongs in deployment configuration rather than its public card."},{"id":224,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"How do messages, tasks, and artifacts differ in A2A?","text":"How do messages, tasks, and artifacts differ in A2A? A message is an exchange of content, a task represents tracked work with lifecycle state, and an artifact is an output produced by that work. A reply being delivered does not necessarily mean the task completed or its output is authorized for all users. Map remote lifecycle states into a versioned internal task model. Preserve remote identifiers, required user input, cancellation state, and authoritative completion references. Treating progress text as a final approved decision can trigger premature downstream action. Artifact references also need ownership, integrity, revision, and retrieval limits. Explicit tasks support long-running work and require durable tracking. One synchronous response is easier when the operation is genuinely short. Design an internal task record for a review request with owner, remote ID, status, result reference, deadline, and idempotency key."},{"id":225,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"How should remote-agent authentication and user delegation work?","text":"How should remote-agent authentication and user delegation work? Authenticate the service peer and separately authorize the delegated action for the originating user or case. A service identity should receive only the evidence and actions needed for its task. Do not forward a browser cookie indiscriminately across organizational boundaries. Authenticate the remote service and delegate only the minimum user authority required. Verify token audience and avoid sending a broad application credential as a convenience. A powerful remote agent can become a confused deputy, using its own privileges for a caller who lacks them. Agent-card metadata does not enforce resource authorization. Narrow delegated capabilities reduce disclosure and require identity integration. Broad shared credentials simplify demos and make accountability difficult. Define which identity approves a review, which identity reads evidence, and which identity may confirm a service request. Keep final write authority local unless explicitly delegated."},{"id":226,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"How do remote retries avoid duplicate work?","text":"How do remote retries avoid duplicate work? Give one logical request a stable operation identity, persist its relationship to the remote task, and query or reconcile before creating another task after an ambiguous timeout. Transport request IDs and business idempotency keys may serve different purposes. Propagate deadlines and durable operation identity across retries. After an uncertain response, query task status rather than automatically restarting a side-effecting remote task. A timeout can mean the remote service accepted the task but the acknowledgment was lost. Blind retries can create duplicate cases, charges, or conflicting recommendations. Reconciliation adds storage and latency but avoids assuming failure from missing acknowledgment. Fire-and-forget messaging is smaller and unsuitable for untracked consequential work. Simulate remote acceptance followed by a dropped response. Show how the client discovers or reuses the original task without submitting twice."},{"id":227,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"What does cancellation mean for a remote agent task?","text":"What does cancellation mean for a remote agent task? Cancellation is a request to stop eligible work, not proof that every side effect has been undone. A task may have completed or crossed an irreversible boundary before the cancellation arrives. The host must interpret the remote result and reconcile its own state. Specify what cancellation can stop and what already completed. A remote cancelled state does not reverse an external effect, so expose authoritative status and compensation where appropriate. Closing the browser stream does not establish task cancellation. A user may otherwise believe a case was abandoned while remote work continues or a notification is delivered. Explicit cancellation acknowledgment improves user expectations and adds race handling. Letting tasks finish is operationally simpler for read-only work if clearly communicated. Draw cancellation before acceptance, during review, and after completion. Define the displayed state and permissible follow-up action for each race."},{"id":228,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"How can A2A artifacts become a prompt-injection or disclosure channel?","text":"How can A2A artifacts become a prompt-injection or disclosure channel? A remote result can contain untrusted text, URLs, attachments, or instructions. Treat it as data from a service with a specific trust level, validate its schema, and allowlist any subsequent action. Remote output must not inherit system-instruction authority. Constrain outputs by schema, scope, size, provenance, and retention. Validate artifacts before using them as facts or displaying them through a privileged renderer. An artifact can ask the model to retrieve another customer's claim or fetch an internal URL. Authentication of the sender does not make every returned instruction safe or every referenced document authorized. Rich artifacts support useful collaboration and need validation, size limits, safe fetching, and provenance. Minimal structured references are easier to govern than entire conversations and attachments. Return a mock artifact containing a cross-tenant reference and an instruction to bypass confirmation. Verify neither is executed."},{"id":229,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"How should one trace span cross an A2A boundary?","text":"How should one trace span cross an A2A boundary? Propagate an approved trace context and stable operation identifiers while keeping credentials and private content separate. Each service records its own spans and outcome; the host correlates the remote task with the originating operation. Pass safe trace context under a reviewed policy and retain task correlation across services. Distributed spans aid diagnosis but do not establish who authorized an operation. A trace ID is not authentication, and untrusted inbound trace metadata needs validation. Copying full claim text into span attributes can leak it to both organizations' telemetry systems. Distributed traces speed diagnosis but require shared conventions and retention policy. Correlation by sanitized operation IDs is a simpler fallback. Design a trace tree for one remote review with a retry and later artifact delivery. Show which spans represent waiting versus remote execution."},{"id":230,"slug":"a2a","chapter":"A2A and distributed agent collaboration","title":"When is a multi-agent architecture justified for service work?","text":"When is a multi-agent architecture justified for service work? Use independently scoped agents when capabilities have distinct ownership, data access, deployment, or specialist review responsibilities. A local planner calling five functions does not need networked peers simply to look sophisticated. Use multiple agents when ownership, capabilities, trust, or deployment boundaries justify them. Compare with one local agent and deterministic services before accepting the coordination overhead. More peers increase routing errors, coordination cost, inconsistent evidence, and cascading failures. Agreement between agents is not independent verification if they share the same wrong source or model. Specialization and organizational boundaries can justify distributed collaboration; a single orchestrator with narrow tools is cheaper and easier to test. Write an A2A architecture decision record with a task lifecycle, delegation policy, data-minimization plan, failure budget, and a simpler alternative."},{"id":231,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"Why is answer evaluation insufficient for an agent?","text":"Why is answer evaluation insufficient for an agent? An answer can sound correct after using the wrong tool, revealing unauthorized information, repeating an expensive action, or failing to commit the intended operation. Agent evaluation needs task success, tool choice and arguments, permissions, side effects, recovery, and final communication. Define task outcomes and forbidden effects before choosing metrics. Evaluate tools and durable state as well as final text, then use traces to identify the failing layer. A model judge can reward a statement that a service request was submitted even when no claim exists. Conversely, a safely rejected operation may sound less helpful while being the correct outcome. Multi-layer evaluation needs more fixtures and gives actionable diagnosis. One aggregate answer score is easier to display and weaker as a release gate. Create three cases with identical final text but different underlying tool and database histories. Define which pass and why."},{"id":232,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How do reference trajectories and invariant-based trajectories differ?","text":"How do reference trajectories and invariant-based trajectories differ? A reference trajectory compares observed calls with an expected sequence. An invariant checks necessary properties such as owner scoping, no duplicate confirmation, and policy retrieval before an eligibility determination. Different valid tool sequences may satisfy the same task. Use exact reference sequences only for required order. Prefer invariants or partial orders when several legitimate trajectories can complete the task without violating scope or approval. Exact sequence tests can fail harmless reordering; very loose rubrics can miss a missing critical step. Tool names alone also ignore whether arguments or results were correct. Reference paths are easy to inspect and brittle under valid variation. Invariants need careful definition and better express safety and business requirements. Evaluate a mixed coverage/status request with two valid call orders, then reject both if the service request lookup lacks the correct owner context."},{"id":233,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"What do retrieval-only, context, and end-to-end evaluations measure?","text":"What do retrieval-only, context, and end-to-end evaluations measure? Retrieval-only evaluation measures candidate quality. Context evaluation examines the evidence assembled for generation. End-to-end evaluation checks the actual answer, agent actions, and relevant durable outcome. Each localizes a different failure class. Record whether an output came from retrieval, context assembly, a baseline, or the live agent. Compare the same path across releases and label synthetic substitutes explicitly. Comparing proxy scores with live-agent scores without checking labels can create a false regression or improvement. Availability fallbacks should never silently change the meaning of the reported metric. Offline proxies are cheap and useful for early diagnosis. Actual agent evaluation costs provider calls and measures a different layer. Run or inspect configurations for all modes and assert the reported answer_source. Separate proxy and live results in your report."},{"id":234,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How should a multi-turn request-preparation test be structured?","text":"How should a multi-turn request-preparation test be structured? Model the user's progressive facts, corrections, missing fields, preparation, and explicit confirmation boundary. Assert the prepared payload and server effects at each step, not only whether the assistant eventually produces a token. Create multi-turn fixtures with missing fields, corrections, stale approvals, cancellation, and repeated submission. Assert both the user interaction and the final business state. A user simulator may accidentally supply hints or accept a fabricated success. Test fixtures should inspect the database and use deterministic assertions for required fields and token state. Multi-turn tests take more execution and reveal memory and correction failures absent from isolated prompts. Keep a small high-value regression set alongside larger sampled evaluations. Create a scenario that changes the amount after preparation and attempts the old token. Report both current behavior and the desired contract."},{"id":235,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How should refusal and clarification be evaluated separately?","text":"How should refusal and clarification be evaluated separately? Refusal is appropriate when the task is unsupported or evidence cannot justify an answer. Clarification is appropriate when obtaining missing facts could make the task answerable. Escalation is another outcome requiring truthful operational status. Distinguish unavailable evidence, unsupported intent, missing user information, and denied authority. Evaluate whether the response chooses the correct refusal, clarification, or retry behavior. Rewarding all refusals as safe can produce an unusable assistant. Rewarding direct answers to ambiguous eligibility questions can produce unsupported decisions. Rich outcome labels cost annotation effort and better match product expectations. A binary correct/incorrect label is simpler and loses diagnostic detail. Label cases as answer, clarify, refuse, retry, or escalate. Require evidence for every final coverage claim and an actual handoff result before claiming transfer."},{"id":236,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How can evaluation verify that writes happened exactly as intended?","text":"How can evaluation verify that writes happened exactly as intended? Assert durable rows, ownership, consumed-token state, lifecycle events, and outbox records after the operation. Repeat and race requests to verify logical uniqueness. Evaluate external deliveries separately from database acceptance. Run against disposable services with an effect ledger. Count writes, approvals, owners, and operation keys; a convincing success message cannot replace database assertions. A mocked submit function can return success without exercising SQL locks or commits. A judge of answer text cannot establish atomicity or idempotency. Real integration tests are slower and necessary for authoritative effects. Cheap fake-model tests remain useful for routing and error mapping around them. Build a failure-injection table for every write in confirmation. For each failure, assert the visible claim, submission, event, and job state."},{"id":237,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How should a judge be calibrated and protected from the answer being judged?","text":"How should a judge be calibrated and protected from the answer being judged? Use a precise rubric, trusted references, known positive and negative cases, and human review to measure agreement. Treat candidate answer and retrieved text as untrusted data in the judge prompt. Record judge model, prompt, and sampling settings. Calibrate judges against human-reviewed labels, keep answer text untrusted, and version the rubric and model. Track disagreements and test adversarial scoring instructions. A candidate can contain instructions to the judge, and a judge can favor style over factual support. A second model is not independent truth when it shares the same missing evidence. Model judging scales nuanced review and costs money and uncertainty. Deterministic checks should handle exact citations, amounts, permissions, and state transitions where possible. Add a fluent unsupported answer and a judge-injection answer to a calibration set. Compare model verdicts with explicit human labels."},{"id":238,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How do uncertainty intervals and category slices improve evaluation?","text":"How do uncertainty intervals and category slices improve evaluation? A score from a small sample is an estimate, not a universal property. Report sample counts, uncertainty, and important categories such as exclusions, limits, corrections, unanswerable questions, and authorization failures. Inspect severity as well as frequency. Report sample counts, confidence or uncertainty intervals, and meaningful task slices. Avoid claiming a small aggregate score proves equivalent quality across languages or difficult cases. Repeatedly tuning on the same visible cases overfits the benchmark. Averaging a cross-user disclosure with several harmless formatting errors gives a misleading quality summary. Category reporting creates more numbers and reveals actionable regressions. Small categories need cautious interpretation rather than overconfident ranking. Split a benchmark by answerability and category, publish counts, and flag critical failures as release blockers instead of averaging them away."},{"id":239,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"How should evaluation control cost and side effects?","text":"How should evaluation control cost and side effects? Separate deterministic unit tests, offline retrieval checks, fake-model trajectories, and opt-in live-agent runs. Use synthetic identities and a sandbox, forbid real irreversible actions, and bound tokens, calls, and total spend. Record which execution mode actually ran. Use synthetic identities, isolated data, and sandbox endpoints. Bound judge calls and agent retries, and never let an evaluation trigger real payments or customer notifications. A live agent can prepare records or call real services if its test capabilities are not constrained. Test traces may also export fixtures containing private data. Offline checks are fast and less realistic; live runs expose provider behavior and require stronger controls. Use both without conflating their scores. Define a CI budget and a nightly live-evaluation budget with safe tool replacements and explicit answer-source assertions."},{"id":240,"slug":"agent-evaluation","chapter":"Evaluating agents and application outcomes","title":"What should an agent release gate require?","text":"What should an agent release gate require? Require critical authorization and write invariants, non-regression on held-out task outcomes, acceptable latency and cost, and a rollback path. Keep quality thresholds distinct from informational efficiency metrics. A release gate should reflect product harm and operational capacity. Predeclare release gates for critical invariants, task quality, latency, and cost. Keep immutable run manifests, reviewed failures, and a rollback artifact for the selected release. A changing judge or proxy fallback can move metrics without a real behavior change. A passing average must never excuse a critical cross-user disclosure or duplicate write. Strict gates slow some releases and prevent expensive regressions. Narrow deterministic checks should fail quickly before costly live evaluation. Write a gate with hard denials, category quality thresholds, p95 latency, cost per successful task, and explicit metadata compatibility checks."},{"id":241,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"How do you choose an evaluation library without turning the stack into a collection of dashboards?","text":"How do you choose an evaluation library without turning the stack into a collection of dashboards? Start from the question to measure: retrieval relevance, grounded answers, tool trajectories, task completion, adversarial behavior, or production drift. Choose one durable experiment record and connect additional metrics only where they add diagnostic value. Choose tools by the question they can measure, required input data, workflow fit, privacy, and cost. A growing collection of dashboards is not an evaluation strategy. Different tools may use the same metric name for different inputs, judges, and aggregation rules. Comparing their numeric outputs without recording definitions is invalid. One harness is easier to operate; specialized libraries can deepen particular checks. Prefer a small adapter around a shared dataset and result schema. Create a selection matrix for ADK evaluation, LangSmith, Ragas, DeepEval, TruLens, Phoenix, and Promptfoo. State one justified use and one limitation for each."},{"id":242,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"What does ADK evaluation add for this example ADK agent?","text":"What does ADK evaluation add for this example ADK agent? ADK evaluation can test tool-use trajectories and final responses using reference or rubric-based criteria. Its fit is strongest when you want evaluation close to the runtime's sessions and events, including reviewed multi-turn cases. Use compatible ADK evaluation interfaces for agent-specific criteria where they fit the runtime. Verify which criteria actually ran and retain application-level transaction and authorization tests separately. Exact trajectory matching can reject valid alternative orders, and judge-based criteria add model dependencies and cost. Some criteria require additional services; the dependency's broad lower bound does not guarantee every current documentation feature exists locally. Runtime-native evaluation reduces event translation and may couple fixtures to the framework. A framework-neutral contract suite survives migration more easily. Define one exact confirmation-preparation trajectory and one rubric for flexible coverage review. Pin versions before running the documented criterion configuration."},{"id":243,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"What is LangSmith evaluation useful for if the agent uses ADK?","text":"What is LangSmith evaluation useful for if the agent uses ADK? LangSmith supports datasets, experiments, evaluators, and review workflows that need not require LangChain as the agent runtime. The application can submit its own target function and measured outputs while retaining ADK execution. Use dataset, experiment, and trace workflows independently of a particular orchestrator where supported. Integrate through a narrow adapter and review what payloads leave the application. Uploading examples, outputs, or traces can disclose claim data. Experiment identity must distinguish model output from an offline context proxy and preserve prompt, corpus, and evaluator revisions. A shared experiment service improves collaboration and adds data-governance and service-dependency considerations. Local JSON reports are easier to keep isolated and less convenient for team review. Design a target function returning answer, evidence IDs, tool trace, and answer_source for one synthetic case. Specify which fields may be exported."},{"id":244,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"How does Ragas help evaluate retrieval-augmented answers?","text":"How does Ragas help evaluate retrieval-augmented answers? Ragas provides metrics for relationships among questions, retrieved contexts, answers, and references. Faithfulness asks whether answer service requests are supported by context; other metrics address relevance and retrieval behavior. Each metric has required inputs and may use a model judge. Match each Ragas metric to its required context, answer, reference, and judge inputs. Pin the implementation and do not assume every metric is reference-free or comparable across versions. Faithfulness to the wrong rule revision can still score well. Missing references, synthetic contexts, and judge changes can alter meaning or cost. Specialized RAG diagnostics help isolate failure layers and do not establish authorization, exact-money calculations, or correct claim writes. Keep those deterministic tests separate. Compare an answer faithful to stale evidence with an answer correct for the active policy. Explain why both evidence applicability and faithfulness must be evaluated."},{"id":245,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"How does DeepEval fit into a pytest-oriented workflow?","text":"How does DeepEval fit into a pytest-oriented workflow? DeepEval provides test-case and metric abstractions for language-model systems, including faithfulness evaluation over retrieval context. It can make selected behavioral quality checks part of an existing test workflow while still depending on judge configuration for relevant metrics. Represent a task with the required test-case fields and choose a DeepEval metric suited to the rubric. Record judge configuration and retain deterministic business-invariant checks alongside it. A unit-test appearance can conceal network calls, nondeterminism, and cost. A passing metric does not prove server-side authorization or transaction atomicity. Developer-friendly tests improve feedback locality; broad judge-heavy CI can become slow and flaky. Run exact contract tests frequently and sampled model judging under a controlled budget. Add one faithful and one unsupported answer to an optional evaluation suite, pin the judge configuration, and record raw verdicts alongside thresholds."},{"id":246,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"What does the TruLens RAG triad measure?","text":"What does the TruLens RAG triad measure? The RAG triad distinguishes context relevance, groundedness of the response in context, and answer relevance to the question. Separating these relationships helps identify whether retrieval or generation deserves investigation. Treat context relevance, groundedness, and answer relevance as distinct questions. Define feedback inputs and calibrate scores before using any RAG triad as a release decision. Good triad scores do not establish that the policy applies to this user or that a submitted claim exists. Domain and permission checks remain outside those semantic relationships. Structured feedback improves diagnosis while adding instrumentation and evaluator configuration. Use it when the team will act on the distinctions rather than only collect more scores. Create three failures: irrelevant context, unsupported answer, and grounded but off-topic answer. Map each to the relationship that should fail."},{"id":247,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"How can Phoenix combine tracing and evaluation?","text":"How can Phoenix combine tracing and evaluation? Phoenix supports inspecting model-system traces and evaluating behavior using recorded inputs and outputs. It can help connect a quality failure to a particular retrieval, tool, or model span. It is a proposed observability/evaluation alternative here, not an existing backend integration. Use safe trace recording to connect failures with evaluation feedback. Review deployment, exporters, retention, and dataset identity rather than adopting a platform for its interface alone. Automatic instrumentation may capture sensitive arguments or create duplicate spans alongside LangSmith. Local hosting alone does not guarantee safe access control or retention. Correlated traces and evaluation improve diagnosis but require export policy, span conventions, and service operation. One well-configured backend is often enough. Define the minimum trace fields needed to explain a wrong policy answer, then compare that allowlist with an automatic export's actual payload."},{"id":248,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"Where does Promptfoo fit compared with a Python evaluation harness?","text":"Where does Promptfoo fit compared with a Python evaluation harness? Promptfoo provides configuration-driven evaluations and assertions over model or application outputs, useful for comparing prompts, providers, and adversarial cases. A Python harness is often more natural for inspecting internal service and database state. Use configuration-driven providers and assertions for repeatable comparisons. Separate deterministic schema or policy checks from model-judged language criteria and control live side effects. A contains assertion can pass on quoted or negated text, and a model-graded assertion still depends on judge quality. End-to-end outputs alone cannot reveal every unsafe intermediate tool call. Declarative matrices make broad experiments convenient; custom Python gives finer control over stateful workflows and failure injection. Define deterministic assertions for an empty result and missing confirmation, then add a reviewed rubric for coverage faithfulness. Explain which checks need backend instrumentation."},{"id":249,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"How do you make evaluation results comparable across libraries?","text":"How do you make evaluation results comparable across libraries? Preserve a shared case identity, corpus revision, policy applicability, model and prompt identity, exact evidence, trajectory, answer_source, judge configuration, and failure policy. Record raw results before converting them to a common report. Align task fixtures, inputs, rubric, judge, sampling, thresholds, and versions before comparing scores. Identical metric names can represent different scales and decision rules. Averaging one library's probability-like score with another's binary verdict produces an uninterpretable number. Silent skipping of failed cases can also inflate averages. Rich records cost storage and support reproducibility. A compact dashboard is useful only when links expose the underlying definitions and examples. Run the same two synthetic cases through two adapters and compare case coverage, inputs, judge settings, and failure handling before comparing scores."},{"id":250,"slug":"evaluation-libraries","chapter":"Agent and RAG evaluation libraries","title":"What evaluation stack is a sensible starting point for this reference design?","text":"What evaluation stack is a sensible starting point for this reference design? Retain deterministic pytest contracts for security and writes, use the existing the retrieval service harness for retrieval diagnosis, and add a small opt-in agent trajectory suite. Choose one experiment and tracing workflow, then add one specialized evaluator only for a demonstrated gap. Start with service invariants, a small agent trajectory suite, and one coherent experiment workflow. Add a new evaluator only for a specific unanswered measurement need. Tool proliferation fragments datasets, duplicates provider spend, and increases private-data copies. A simpler stack with reviewed gold cases can produce more reliable engineering decisions. Incremental adoption delays some advanced features and keeps failure attribution manageable. Expand when the team can name the question the example tools cannot answer. Write a one-page evaluation architecture with test tiers, dataset owner, judge budget, privacy policy, and release blockers. Limit the first implementation to three layers."},{"id":251,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"How do logs, metrics, traces, audit records, and evaluations differ?","text":"How do logs, metrics, traces, audit records, and evaluations differ? Logs describe events, metrics aggregate measurements, traces connect causal execution spans, audit records document selected authoritative actions, and evaluations assess behavior against expectations. They can correlate through operation IDs but are not substitutes for one another. Use metrics for trends, logs for events, traces for causal execution, audit for required accountability, and evaluation for quality. Give each a retention and failure policy suited to its purpose. A successful trace export does not prove a business committed, and an audit row does not explain a model's poor retrieval. Confusing these roles produces incomplete incident diagnosis. Multiple signals add operational work and answer different questions. Start with a small coherent schema rather than unrelated dashboards. For a failed confirmation after a successful model turn, state which signal proves the transaction result and which explains the preceding tool choice."},{"id":252,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"What should a useful agent trace tree contain?","text":"What should a useful agent trace tree contain? One request or turn root should connect relevant model, tool, retrieval, and persistence activity with durations and sanitized outcomes. Include operation and version metadata needed to reproduce the failure, while excluding secrets and unnecessary private content. Create a task or request root and connect model, retrieval, tool, and persistence spans. Include safe operation and release references without storing unnecessary user content. Orphan model spans can hide which user-visible turn incurred cost. Copying entire state into attributes creates a privacy problem and a noisy trace. Rich trace structure improves diagnosis but adds instrumentation complexity. Keep high-value boundaries stable and avoid tracing every tiny helper. Draw the trace tree for coverage review with two tool calls, retrieval, one retry, and final persistence. Mark parallel versus sequential time honestly."},{"id":253,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"Why does the runtime-to-model tracing bridge need explicit context stitching?","text":"Why does the runtime-to-model tracing bridge need explicit context stitching? Different instrumentation layers may not share the same active trace context. A parent turn recorded by one SDK and a completion recorded by another callback can otherwise appear as unrelated runs. Correlation metadata bridges that boundary. Propagate trace context through supported async and adapter hooks. Verify parentage and avoid brittle monkey patches unless version-specific tests justify the integration. Patching a framework seam is sensitive to dependency changes. A successful import does not prove nesting remains correct after an upgrade, and duplicate callbacks can double recorded usage. A localized bridge preserves business-code independence and needs targeted compatibility tests. Provider-native tracing can be simpler but may not connect all application activity. Assert one root turn with nested tool and model spans before and after repeated startup. Verify parent IDs using synthetic data."},{"id":254,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"How do you distinguish tool exceptions from business failures in telemetry?","text":"How do you distinguish tool exceptions from business failures in telemetry? Record transport/runtime success separately from domain outcome. A function that returns error='backend unavailable' has executed without raising but did not satisfy the user task. An empty successful search is another distinct outcome. Record function exceptions and returned business failures separately. A successful function invocation may still mean not found, denied, unavailable, or needs confirmation. A dashboard based only on exceptions can report excellent tool reliability while users receive failed searches or validations. Mixing not-found with outages also obscures incident severity. Typed outcome categories require consistent tool schemas and provide actionable alerts. Raw exception counts are simple and incomplete. Add outcome dimensions for completed, empty, validation_rejected, unauthorized, backend_failed, and cancelled. Test each without logging private arguments."},{"id":255,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"How should token and cost telemetry handle unknown prices?","text":"How should token and cost telemetry handle unknown prices? Record observed usage, estimated cost, pricing source, and whether pricing is known. Unknown should not be silently interpreted as free. Compare cost per successful task and per stage rather than only per model request. Resolve usage and pricing independently, retaining unknown and estimated values explicitly. Compare total cost per completed task, including retries and failed runs, with a known pricing source. A zero-cost metric can hide a new provider or unsupported model price. Retries, embedding, and reranking may also be omitted from a narrow generation-only dashboard. Detailed accounting adds metadata and improves budget control. Provider invoices remain a reconciliation source rather than assuming local estimates are exact bills. Send a synthetic unknown-model usage record and verify it is reported as unpriced, not confidently free. Attribute all calls in one successful task."},{"id":256,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"Why are in-process metrics different from fleet-wide monitoring?","text":"Why are in-process metrics different from fleet-wide monitoring? Process-local counters and bounded event buffers describe one process since its start. They do not automatically aggregate across replicas, survive restarts, or provide a durable long-term time series. Fleet monitoring requires collection and identity rules. Aggregate across replicas through an external collection system and stable metric definitions. Process-local counters cannot establish a deployment-wide rate or survive restart by themselves. Summing request snapshots repeatedly can double count, and restarting a process can look like a sudden drop in demand. High-cardinality labels such as raw request IDs can overwhelm a metrics backend. Local metrics are lightweight and useful for debugging; shared systems add infrastructure and coherent operational visibility. Run two replicas and restart one. Specify how counters, gauges, and histograms should be exported and queried without inventing fleet totals."},{"id":257,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"How can trace collection disclose private customer data?","text":"How can trace collection disclose private customer data? Tool arguments, results, prompts, completions, exception strings, and URLs can contain customer or claim information. Tracing a trusted tool does not make its payload safe to export. Data minimization must apply before or at the instrumentation/export boundary. Apply an allowlist before export, redact necessary payloads, and control access and retention. Review embeddings, transcripts, documents, and identifiers as potential disclosure sources. Trace readers or vendors may gain access broader than the product's owner-scoped API permits. Confirmation tokens and source text should not appear by accident in support exports. Content-rich traces help reproduce model failures and increase privacy obligations. Prefer sanitized fixtures and reviewed sampled content when necessary. Inventory actual exported payloads for one synthetic request preparation. Define an allowlist and prove a fake token and private description are absent."},{"id":258,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"Should tracing failures be allowed to fail business operations?","text":"Should tracing failures be allowed to fail business operations? Optional telemetry often uses best-effort failure containment to preserve availability. Mandatory business audit may require a different policy. Decide per signal rather than declaring that every kind of observation may be dropped harmlessly. Keep optional telemetry failure from blocking business work where the product permits it. Required audit may need transactional delivery or a controlled fail-closed policy instead. Silent instrumentation failure can leave an incident undiagnosable. Conversely, blocking every request on a slow telemetry vendor can create an avoidable outage. Fail-open telemetry improves availability and needs health signals for lost coverage. Transactional audit or a durable audit outbox increases assurance and operational coupling. Simulate unavailable tracing and unavailable audit storage separately. Define the expected request, readiness, and alert behavior for each."},{"id":259,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"What SLOs and alerts are useful for a support assistant?","text":"What SLOs and alerts are useful for a support assistant? Monitor successful authorized task completion, stream completion, backend failures, indexing lag, oldest pending job age, provider throttling, and cost per completed task. Use freshness and correctness signals alongside request latency. Define SLOs around useful outcomes, response delay, evidence freshness, and recovery. Alert on sustained user impact and preserve a path from aggregate metrics to safe trace identifiers. Alerting only on average HTTP latency misses stuck jobs and quality failures. Sampling can also miss rare severe authorization incidents, which need deterministic denial monitoring and tests. More nuanced SLOs require definitions and ownership. A small set linked to concrete user impact is more maintainable than dozens of disconnected thresholds. Define a stream-success SLO and an indexing-lag SLO, including numerator, denominator, window, and corrective action."},{"id":260,"slug":"agent-observability","chapter":"Agent observability and trace privacy","title":"How should observability produce better evaluation cases?","text":"How should observability produce better evaluation cases? Triage a failure trace, minimize and sanitize it, establish the correct outcome with a reviewer, and add a versioned regression case. Keep raw production data access restricted and preserve which failure the new case is intended to catch. Turn reviewed incidents into redacted reproducible fixtures. Keep task category, configuration lineage, observed failure, and expected invariant while avoiding unnecessary customer-data retention. Automatically adding model answers as gold labels reinforces errors. Copying production conversations into broad datasets can violate data-minimization policy. Reviewed case creation costs human time and improves relevance. Fully synthetic tests scale cheaply and may miss real ambiguity. Turn one synthetic wrong-policy trace into a case with expected revision, required tool steps, allowed final outcomes, and a clear privacy review."},{"id":261,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"How does explaining eligibility differ from authorizing a business outcome?","text":"How does explaining eligibility differ from authorizing a business outcome? An assistant can explain documented eligibility rules and identify missing facts. A binding business outcome requires the authorized process, applicable evidence, and an authoritative recorded decision. Clearly communicate which responsibility the product is performing. Separate explanation from authority. An assistant can summarize applicable rules; a deterministic service and authorized actor own the actual business transition and approval. A fluent answer can sound like approval despite missing personal applicability evidence. Distinguish general rules, verified customer-specific facts, and actual recorded decisions. Careful conditional explanations may feel less decisive and preserve the actual authority boundary. Automated adjudication is a separate product requiring substantially stronger domain and governance evidence. Rewrite three coverage answers to state the supporting clause, material conditions, unresolved facts, and whether an authorized decision has actually occurred."},{"id":262,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"How should event date, submission date, and rule effective date be handled?","text":"How should event date, submission date, and rule effective date be handled? Event date concerns when something happened, submission date records when a request was filed, and rule effective dates define version applicability. These are separate facts; do not infer one from another merely to fill a required field. Store event time, submission time, and rule effective intervals separately. Select the applicable revision explicitly and evaluate timezone and boundary cases rather than guessing a missing date. Applying today's policy to an earlier incident can produce an inaccurate answer. Time zones and inclusive/exclusive boundary rules also matter for exact applicability. Explicit temporal modeling adds query and validation work. A current-only general FAQ is simpler if the UI honestly limits its scope. Create incidents before, at, and after a policy-version boundary. Specify the applicable interval rule and the evidence required for each answer."},{"id":263,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"Why must exclusions and conditions be retrieved alongside a positive coverage clause?","text":"Why must exclusions and conditions be retrieved alongside a positive coverage clause? A positive clause can be limited by exclusions, definitions, sublimits, deductibles, and factual conditions elsewhere. Retrieving one semantically similar paragraph is not necessarily sufficient to explain applicability. Retrieve linked exceptions and required conditions alongside a positive clause. Group evidence by applicable revision before answering and abstain when a material qualification is unavailable. A faithful summary of an isolated clause can still mislead by omitting a controlling exception. More top-k hits may not solve the problem if the corpus lacks structured relationships. Targeted follow-up retrieval or structured links improve completeness and add calls and complexity. Large indiscriminate context increases noise and cost. Build a case where the positive clause ranks highly but its exclusion is elsewhere. Evaluate whether a follow-up query or section expansion retrieves the missing condition."},{"id":264,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"Why is a section citation weaker than a revision-aware evidence reference?","text":"Why is a section citation weaker than a revision-aware evidence reference? A section label identifies a human-readable location, but may not uniquely identify source, revision, or exact supporting span. A reproducible reference needs enough identity to retrieve what the user originally saw. Carry source ID, revision, span, and applicability metadata through assembly and citation. Recheck access when opening a historical source instead of treating an identifier as a permission token. A later policy edit can make an old answer unverifiable, and section-only deduplication can collapse distinct sources. A fallback source filename is not evidence of the original document's identity. Rich citations add metadata and snapshot retention while enabling verification. Simple labels work for a small sample corpus and should not be overstated. Create two sources with an identically named section and different terms. Design a stable citation key and bounded source-opening API."},{"id":265,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"How should OCR, tables, photographs, and attachments extend the support pipeline?","text":"How should OCR, tables, photographs, and attachments extend the support pipeline? Treat each modality as a separate extraction and evidence problem. OCR converts visible text, tables need row-column relationships, and photographs may require a vision model or human review. A file upload alone does not create reliable multimodal reasoning. Preserve original artifacts, OCR text, layout, and region provenance. Validate fields and tables before indexing, and treat extracted instructions as untrusted source content. OCR errors in amounts, dates, or negations can change meaning. Attachments can also contain malware, private third-party data, prompt injection, and oversized payloads. Richer inputs improve user access and require scanning, extraction confidence, provenance, storage policy, and review states. Text-only support is narrower and easier to validate. Design an attachment intake state machine with quarantine, extraction, review, indexing, and rejection. Keep source bytes separate from generated interpretations."},{"id":266,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"Why should fraud or risk signals not be treated as automatic truth?","text":"Why should fraud or risk signals not be treated as automatic truth? A statistical risk score is evidence for investigation, not proof that a service requestant is dishonest or that a service request should be denied. Training data, false positives, protected proxies, and changing behavior all affect interpretation. Treat risk predictions as uncertain inputs with reviewed thresholds and an appeal path. Evaluate false positives and subgroup effects before connecting a signal to consequential decisions. Presenting an opaque score as a decision can create harmful unsupported accusations and unreliable automation. It also introduces sensitive-data and model-governance obligations beyond this guide's engineering examples. Assisted triage can prioritize workload while requiring calibration, review, and appeal paths. Simple rule-based routing may be more transparent for a small system. Design a review-only output with supporting observable signals, uncertainty, missing information, and prohibited automatic actions. Do not invent a fraud threshold."},{"id":267,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"How should deterministic calculations complement generated explanations?","text":"How should deterministic calculations complement generated explanations? Use reviewed deterministic code for exact arithmetic under explicitly selected policy rules, then let generation explain the result and assumptions. A model should not invent a deductible, round a monetary value arbitrarily, or apply an unverified formula. Use deterministic arithmetic with explicit units, currencies, dates, and reviewed formulas. Let the model explain a verified result rather than inventing or silently altering the calculation. Correct arithmetic with the wrong rule or knowledge version is still wrong. Caps, sublimits, depreciation, multiple currencies, and exceptions require domain-approved logic. Deterministic calculators improve reproducibility and need versioned business specifications. Free-form arithmetic is convenient and unsuitable as authoritative financial logic. Write a hypothetical deductible calculation with explicit inputs and rounding, then state which real knowledge facts must be verified before using it."},{"id":268,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"What should a real human-escalation record contain?","text":"What should a real human-escalation record contain? Store the originating operation, authorized user or case, reason, minimal evidence, destination, delivery state, and an acknowledgement or failure. Distinguish requested, accepted, in progress, and completed rather than claiming connection from generated prose alone. Create a durable handoff record with owner, urgency, evidence, completed actions, and unresolved questions. Report actual acceptance state instead of promising human contact without an integration. Exporting a full conversation can disclose unnecessary personal data, and a failed handoff can leave a user waiting without an actual owner. Structured escalation adds integration work and truthful operational status. A clear support-contact instruction is a legitimate simpler fallback. Specify a proposed handoff service and acceptance tests for delivery failure, duplicate requests, revoked access, and a later human response."},{"id":269,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"How should fairness and accessibility be evaluated in customer support?","text":"How should fairness and accessibility be evaluated in customer support? Evaluate whether the assistant handles equivalent requests consistently across language, writing style, disability-related interaction needs, and differing levels of domain knowledge. Focus on support outcomes and access, not unsupported automated judgments about protected traits. Evaluate realistic language, literacy, disability, and channel variations under reviewed criteria. Report subgroup results and preserve a usable non-voice or human-assisted alternative. Simplified wording can accidentally omit a condition, while multilingual retrieval can fail under ASCII lexical fallback. Voice or OCR inputs introduce another representation gap. Broader evaluation and interface support cost effort and improve real usability. Clearly bounded supported languages are preferable to an untested universal claim. Create semantically equivalent questions in plain language, formal wording, and a supported second language. Compare evidence, clarification, and escalation outcomes."},{"id":270,"slug":"evidence-applicability","chapter":"Evidence applicability and decision boundaries","title":"How should the application explain uncertainty without hiding system failure?","text":"How should the application explain uncertainty without hiding system failure? Explain whether facts are missing, evidence is absent, the source is inapplicable, or a backend is unavailable. Give the strongest supported statement while preserving a distinct failure status. Uncertainty should guide the next action rather than be a vague disclaimer appended to everything. Return typed missing-input, insufficient-evidence, conflict, and unavailable states. Explain the next useful step without presenting an infrastructure failure as proof that no relevant record exists. An outage can be mistaken for proof that the policy says nothing. Reassuring language may also imply a specialist handoff that never happened. Explicit statuses require API and UI changes but make retries and support decisions honest. Text-only fallback is simple and ambiguous. Design separate responses for missing incident facts, no matching clause, source outage, stale policy, and completed escalation. Map each to a testable backend status."},{"id":271,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"What is the public SSE contract, and why should the browser not parse raw ADK internals?","text":"What is the public SSE contract, and why should the browser not parse raw ADK internals? The API maps runtime events into text_delta, tool_call, tool_result, tool_error, error, and response_complete messages. A stable application contract lets the UI evolve independently of ADK and supports a future orchestrator adapter. Map provider or framework events into a versioned public contract. Persist the answer before exposing its final message ID and keep internal tool arguments out of the public stream. Forwarding raw runtime objects can leak fields and break the UI after dependency changes. A stream ending at the network layer is not necessarily a successful response_complete event. An adapter adds code and preserves product stability. Direct runtime streaming is quicker for a demo and creates tighter coupling. Capture a synthetic successful and failed stream. Verify event discriminators, JSON validity, and exactly one meaningful terminal outcome for the actual route path."},{"id":272,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"How should a browser parse streaming chunks correctly?","text":"How should a browser parse streaming chunks correctly? Buffer bytes across network reads, decode incrementally, split complete SSE frames, parse their JSON, and retain any unfinished suffix. A network chunk may contain half a JSON object or several events; it is not an event boundary. Decode UTF-8 incrementally and accumulate SSE fields through the blank-line delimiter. Validate the completed event payload separately and test every relevant network fragmentation boundary. Naive JSON.parse on each read loses events or fails on multibyte characters. Error recovery must also distinguish a malformed event from a clean terminal completion. A small parser handles a narrow contract; a mature SSE client can cover more protocol details. Test the actual API framing and reconnection requirements. Feed the same stream split at every possible byte boundary, including inside Unicode text. Verify identical parsed events."},{"id":273,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"Why do persisted message IDs matter for feedback?","text":"Why do persisted message IDs matter for feedback? Feedback should reference an actual assistant message owned by the user, not a temporary browser-generated ID. The backend can then authorize the record and connect a rating to its conversation, evidence, model configuration, and trace. Bind feedback to an owner-scoped persisted answer or operation. A client-supplied message ID must not allow another user to label or retrieve private conversation data. Rating an optimistic local ID can fail or attach feedback to the wrong turn. A persistence failure may leave partial text visible but no authoritative message to rate. Waiting for server identity adds a final synchronization step and preserves referential integrity. Client-only messages are easy to render and weak as durable references. Simulate a stream that generates text but fails persistence. Define whether feedback is disabled and how retry behaves without duplicating the displayed answer."},{"id":274,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"What should the UI state machine distinguish during a turn?","text":"What should the UI state machine distinguish during a turn? Distinguish draft, accepted, searching or tooling, generating, completed, failed, cancelled, and disconnected. Track evidence and persistence status separately where needed. A spinner stopping is not proof that the model completed successfully. Use explicit waiting, running, streaming, review, complete, incomplete, failed, and cancelled states. Transitions should follow typed outcomes rather than the presence of some generated text. Treating every closed connection as completion can show truncated service requests as final. Late responses from a prior conversation or signed-out user can also contaminate current state. Explicit states add frontend logic and improve recovery. A single loading boolean is compact and cannot represent the important distinctions. Create a reducer for stream events and test disconnect before completion, tool failure, persistence failure, cancellation, and a late event after tenant or conversation change."},{"id":275,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"How should request confirmation be presented and protected in the UI?","text":"How should request confirmation be presented and protected in the UI? Display the exact prepared fields, identify that no claim is yet submitted, and send the token through the authenticated confirmation route only after the user's action. Disable duplicate controls for usability while retaining server-side race protection. Present exact parameters, operation identity, expiry, and revision. Disable misleading repeated actions in the UI, but rely on server-side approval validation and transactional idempotency for correctness. Corrections can leave old pending tokens valid unless the server supersedes them. The UI must not promise revocation that only exists in prompt text. A review screen adds friction and supports consent to material fields. The authoritative check must remain on the server because browsers can retry or be bypassed. Test double click, refresh, expired token, cancellation race, and correction after preparation. Compare displayed state with the actual submission record."},{"id":276,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"How should browser caching respond to sign-out, role changes, and archived conversations?","text":"How should browser caching respond to sign-out, role changes, and archived conversations? Partition cached results by trusted user and relevant resource identity, cancel or ignore obsolete in-flight responses, and clear private state at sign-out. Revalidate permissions for source opening and mutation even if a cached view once allowed access. Invalidate or partition client caches by authenticated identity and permission revision. Cancel streams and clear private view state on sign-out, while retaining server-side ownership checks on every fetch. Reusing query keys across users can show private content without making a new unauthorized request. Hidden or archived messages can also remain in model context unless their lifecycle is mapped deliberately. Aggressive invalidation costs network requests and preserves clear session boundaries. Long-lived caches improve responsiveness and need stronger partitioning and freshness rules. Switch users during a slow stream and verify no old message, source, or confirmation token appears in the new user's interface."},{"id":277,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"How should citations and tool activity be rendered accessibly and safely?","text":"How should citations and tool activity be rendered accessibly and safely? Render untrusted text safely, expose expandable source details by keyboard, use meaningful labels, and avoid announcing every token to assistive technology. Show user-relevant activity without exposing internal prompts, private tool arguments, or raw exception data. Render model text as text or a sanitized approved subset. Validate links and avoid treating returned HTML or tool output as trusted application markup. Raw HTML or unsafe URLs can create XSS, and a busy live region can make streaming unusable. A similarity distance should not be displayed as percent certainty. Progressive disclosure reduces overload but must keep verification discoverable. Detailed internal traces belong in authorized operator tools, not the customer screen. Review a synthetic answer using only the keyboard and a screen reader. Include malicious markup and long source titles in the fixture."},{"id":278,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"How should client retries preserve operation identity?","text":"How should client retries preserve operation identity? Reuse one idempotency key for retries of the same logical request, but create a new operation for a genuinely changed request. Record whether the server accepted or completed it. Claim confirmation should rely on its own token and durable state, not a generic chat response cache. Preserve a durable operation key across retries and distinguish request deduplication from business idempotency. After an uncertain outcome, query status rather than restarting a write blindly. Reusing a key with a changed body should fail rather than replay another answer. Retrying a long-running request after a best-effort Redis wait can still duplicate model calls. Explicit operation identity requires lifecycle handling and supports predictable retries. Automatically retrying all failures is easy and can repeat expensive work. Test same key/same body, same key/changed body, new key/same body, and an ambiguous timeout. Document behavior for chat versus confirmation."},{"id":279,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"How should frontend, API, and agent contracts be tested together?","text":"How should frontend, API, and agent contracts be tested together? Use a small set of full user journeys plus focused contract tests for events, ownership, persistence IDs, and confirmation effects. Fake providers make UI tests deterministic; separate live-provider tests assess actual model behavior. Expose a stable API error and outcome schema across frontend and backend. Version incompatible changes and test clients against missing, delayed, duplicate, and unknown event types. A mocked stream that always ends successfully cannot reveal cancellation or malformed-event behavior. Live model tests alone are too variable for every UI assertion. Layered tests provide fast diagnosis and credible integration coverage. Excessive end-to-end duplication raises cost without necessarily testing new behavior. Define five journeys: cited answer, corrected preparation, denied foreign claim, failed stream, and post-restart history. State the provider and database realism each needs."},{"id":280,"slug":"product-delivery","chapter":"Full-stack streaming and user workflows","title":"What should the completed portfolio demonstration show?","text":"What should the completed portfolio demonstration show? Show one coherent user journey across authentication, evidence retrieval, streaming, source verification, request preparation, confirmation, background indexing, and feedback. Then demonstrate a denial and a recovery case with supporting traces and tests. Demonstrate identity, evidence, approved effects, cancellation, restart recovery, and quality measurement in one coherent journey. Use synthetic data and show a failure as well as success. A polished recording can hide unimplemented handoff, current-only policy scope, or in-memory deployment state. State those limitations and show the effective configuration. A narrow reliable application is more persuasive than many unverified agent features. Add remote agents or model adaptation only when the measured problem justifies them. Prepare a ten-minute demo with architecture, one successful task, one adversarial denial, one crash recovery, and a held-out quality/cost report."},{"id":281,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"What must change when scaling from one process to multiple replicas?","text":"What must change when scaling from one process to multiple replicas? Shared sessions, idempotency, rate limits, jobs, migrations, and telemetry need explicit cross-process semantics. Process-local dictionaries, locks, and counters remain local even when replicas run identical code. Move authoritative tasks, sessions, rate accounting, and operation identity into deliberately shared stores where required. Test concurrent replicas instead of extrapolating from one process. Adding replicas can multiply quotas, repeat model calls, and split conversational state. A load balancer does not make local state shared. Shared services improve continuity and introduce network dependencies. One process with honest limitations can be a simpler initial deployment. Test retries routed to another replica, session continuation, worker claiming, and global quota behavior during Redis failure."},{"id":282,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"Should migrations run on every application startup?","text":"Should migrations run on every application startup? Startup migration is convenient for small deployments but couples readiness to schema changes and can make replicas compete to migrate. A controlled migration job followed by compatible application rollout is an alternative. Give one deployment step ownership of reviewed migrations and deploy compatible application versions. Define upgrade failure and rollback behavior before enabling multiple startup processes. Concurrent DDL, long locks, and destructive changes can affect all replicas. A schema change can also prevent rollback to the prior image. Self-initializing services reduce manual steps; dedicated migration stages improve sequencing. Either requires tested failure behavior and appropriate database privileges. Design a rolling deployment with one migrator, compatible schema, application rollout, verification, and delayed cleanup."},{"id":283,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"Why does startup ingestion need separate operational treatment?","text":"Why does startup ingestion need separate operational treatment? Ingestion performs provider calls and writes unrelated to accepting a web request. Running it during every replica startup can duplicate preparation, delay readiness, and couple scaling to source and model availability. Run ingestion as a versioned background workflow with freshness state, deduplication, deadlines, and retry policy. Avoid repeating expensive source preparation on each replica startup. A service can start successfully with missing knowledge. Database connectivity does not prove usable policy evidence exists. Startup ingestion suits a sample corpus; a scheduled or operator-triggered worker gives clearer ownership, progress, and recovery. Start two replicas with an unchanged source, then with an unavailable provider. Report effective readiness, freshness, and duplicate preparation."},{"id":284,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"What does the minimal health endpoint prove?","text":"What does the minimal health endpoint prove? A bounded database probe establishes only that a basic connection and query worked at that moment. It does not prove model availability, current evidence, healthy workers, correct permissions, or functioning event delivery. Separate process liveness, dependency readiness, index freshness, and task-quality signals. Bound probes and avoid costly model calls on every health check. Calling this fully healthy can conceal an assistant unable to answer or index. Putting expensive evaluations in every probe can instead overload the service. Cheap probes suit frequent checks. Readiness and scheduled consistency checks should cover critical product dependencies separately. Define probe outcomes for database failure, provider failure, stale corpus, and a stopped embedding worker."},{"id":285,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"How should connection pools and worker concurrency be sized together?","text":"How should connection pools and worker concurrency be sized together? Count API requests, background jobs, agent-session writes, migrations, and probes that need connections. Bound concurrency and pool waits so one workload cannot consume all capacity. Async I/O does not remove database connection limits. Multiply pool limits by processes and replicas, then reserve database headroom for workers and operations. Bound simultaneous queries separately from model or HTTP concurrency. Increasing replicas can exceed the total connection budget. Provider waits inside open transactions amplify occupancy even when CPU usage is low. Larger pools reduce local waiting until database saturation; smaller pools protect the database and need admission control. Calculate connections across replicas and workers, reserve operational headroom, and measure pool checkout latency under load."},{"id":286,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"How should container and dependency hardening be evaluated?","text":"How should container and dependency hardening be evaluated? Review non-root execution, image contents, dependency resolution, vulnerability handling, secrets, and runtime privileges. A production filename or non-root USER is one control rather than complete supply-chain assurance. Review base images, non-root execution, dependency resolution, runtime packages, and artifact provenance. Exact direct pins alone do not establish a reproducible environment or safe deployment. Without constrained resolution and compatibility checks, upgrades can break ADK events, tracing patches, or provider behavior. Build tools and unnecessary files also add maintenance surface. Strict locking improves repeatability and requires planned updates. Broad ranges support experimentation and weaken deployment reproducibility. Compare resolved dependencies from two clean builds and propose a tested update policy without changing the support repository."},{"id":287,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"How should worker shutdown and stale-job recovery be verified?","text":"How should worker shutdown and stale-job recovery be verified? Stop accepting new work, allow bounded completion, release resources, and use durable job state for later recovery. A finally block helps graceful shutdown but cannot be guaranteed after abrupt process death. Stop accepting new work, allow or cancel active operations under a deadline, persist state, and recover stale leases safely. Verify shutdown through controlled worker interruption. Reclaiming too early overlaps workers; never reclaiming strands jobs. A crash after external delivery but before completion marking can repeat delivery. Graceful draining improves continuity and slows replacement. Abrupt replacement is faster and demands stronger recovery invariants. Terminate a worker after claim, after embedding, and after delivery. Verify the next worker converges without duplicate logical effects."},{"id":288,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"How should an external provider outage affect the product?","text":"How should an external provider outage affect the product? Distinguish unavailable generation, unavailable embeddings, failed retrieval, and degraded tracing. Preserve committed business state and expose retryable status. Do not fabricate policy answers or report unperformed actions as successful. Classify affected capabilities and provide explicit degraded outcomes. Apply bounded retries and circuit or admission policies without generating unsupported answers when evidence or tools are unavailable. Nested retries can exhaust pools and budgets. Switching providers can change tool and privacy behavior and must be an explicit evaluated policy. Structured status lookup can preserve useful service while generation is unavailable. Full-availability service requests are inappropriate when essential evidence is missing. Define an outage matrix for PostgreSQL, Redis, embedding, generation, and tracing, with permitted operations and recovery behavior."},{"id":289,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"What is a meaningful disaster-recovery drill?","text":"What is a meaningful disaster-recovery drill? Restore authoritative data, recover or rebuild derived indexes, reconcile jobs, validate ownership, and demonstrate post-restart application behavior. Measure actual recovery time and data loss against declared objectives. Restore a known fixture and reconcile authoritative records, outbox state, indexes, and access policy. Measure actual recovery duration and data-loss exposure rather than relying on backup existence. Old jobs may replay and stale vectors may remain visible. Backups themselves also need secure access, retention, and credential-restoration procedures. Frequent drills cost effort and reveal dependencies that documents miss. Backup configuration alone is not proof of recoverability. Restore a fixture with a service request, pending job, rule revision, and archived conversation. Produce a reconciliation report."},{"id":290,"slug":"deployment-reliability","chapter":"Deployment, recovery, and production readiness","title":"What evidence supports a production-readiness claim?","text":"What evidence supports a production-readiness claim? Show effective configuration, tested authorization and transaction boundaries, migration ownership, restore evidence, queue and latency measurements, quality limits, privacy policy, and incident ownership. State the workload and scope under which the service request holds. Collect deployment-specific evidence for invariants, capacity, recovery, security, and quality. Document limits honestly and retain rollback; a container build or green process probe is only one check. A polished architecture or passing unit suite can conceal untested runtime behavior. Secure, scalable, and exactly once are service requests requiring specific evidence. Readiness work is slower than a demo and establishes a defensible operational contract. A prototype may ship earlier with narrower commitments. Assemble a release packet with concurrency results, quality report, dependency inventory, privacy review, restore drill, and rollback plan."},{"id":291,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"What mathematical foundations should an AI full-stack engineer learn first?","text":"What mathematical foundations should an AI full-stack engineer learn first? Learn vectors, norms, dot products, probability, conditional probability, cross-entropy, optimization, and basic statistics. Connect them to retrieval geometry, training, calibration, and evaluation rather than memorizing formulas without a system consequence. Practice vector arithmetic, dot products, probability, gradients, and optimization with small executable examples. Connect each operation to embeddings, attention, training objectives, and inference instead of memorizing terms alone. A number can look authoritative while measuring the wrong event. Similarity is not truth probability, and judge agreement is not calibrated decision confidence. API skills enable quick prototypes; foundations improve diagnosis. Alternate practical exercises with theory. Compute normalized similarity by hand and estimate uncertainty in a small benchmark. Explain what each number establishes."},{"id":292,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"How do calibration and selective answering differ from ranking?","text":"How do calibration and selective answering differ from ranking? Ranking orders candidates; calibration relates predicted confidence to observed correctness; selective answering chooses when to abstain. Define correctness and harm before turning a score into a decision threshold. Define correctness and error cost, calibrate scores on reviewed outcomes, and report accuracy alongside answer coverage. A high ranking score is not automatically a confidence probability. A threshold tuned only on answerable questions can accept unsupported claims. Rare severe errors require separate treatment from average accuracy. More abstention can improve reliability among answered cases while reducing coverage. Evaluate the risk-coverage relationship with reviewed labels. Plot answered fraction against error rate across candidate thresholds. Separate outages, missing facts, and inapplicable knowledge rules."},{"id":293,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"What are hard negatives and why can domain retrieval need them?","text":"What are hard negatives and why can domain retrieval need them? Hard negatives appear relevant but fail a material condition, such as gradual leakage versus a sudden burst. They test whether representation and reranking distinguish the condition the user's question depends on. Use reviewed non-relevant examples that resemble the query to teach finer distinctions. Keep false negatives out of training and compare retrieval improvements on held-out domain tasks. Binary labels can incorrectly mark a controlling exclusion irrelevant. Distinguish supporting, contradicting, conditional, and unrelated evidence when the task requires it. Rich labels cost review effort and reveal meaningful limitations. Easy random negatives produce cheaper and often flattering scores. Build a query with a positive clause, exclusion, unrelated clause, and ambiguous condition. Evaluate candidate recall and evidence completeness separately."},{"id":294,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"When should you adapt an embedding or reranker instead of changing the prompt?","text":"When should you adapt an embedding or reranker instead of changing the prompt? Adapt representation when evidence consistently fails candidate retrieval or ranking after extraction, chunking, and labeling are correct. Change answer policy when useful evidence arrives but generation ignores it or overservice requests. Improve representation or ranking when the correct evidence consistently fails candidate recall or relevance ordering. Tune prompts when evidence is already correct and generation misuses it; evaluate each failure separately. Tiny-domain tuning can overfit visible questions and worsen rare exclusions or other languages. Training leakage can conceal the regression. Adaptation may improve difficult matching and adds training and migration cost. Structured metadata or deterministic routing can be cheaper. Classify twenty failures by the first stage losing correctness and justify adaptation only after simpler repairs fail."},{"id":295,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"What do distillation and synthetic data add to a learning plan?","text":"What do distillation and synthetic data add to a learning plan? Distillation trains a smaller system from a stronger teacher's signals. Synthetic data expands examples, paraphrases, or adversarial cases. Both require independent validation because confident teacher errors can be copied at scale. Distillation transfers behavior from a stronger teacher under a defined objective. Synthetic data can broaden fixtures or training examples, but needs validation, diversity checks, provenance, and separation from held-out evaluation. Generated questions can mirror source wording and overstate generalization. Generated answers should not become gold labels without review. Synthetic expansion reduces annotation effort and can reduce diversity or amplify bias. Keep a reviewed core as the anchor. Generate toy paraphrases, review them, and compare held-out quality before and after using the accepted data."},{"id":296,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"How do quantization, batching, and caching affect serving differently?","text":"How do quantization, batching, and caching affect serving differently? Quantization changes numerical precision, batching groups execution, and caching reuses prior results under a valid identity. They optimize different costs and can change quality, waiting latency, privacy, and freshness differently. Measure quantization, batching, caching, and serving concurrency on representative hardware and tasks. Report quality, throughput, tail latency, and memory together. Cached answers may be stale after policy or permission changes. Aggressive batching can delay interactive requests, and quantization may change task quality. Optimizations reduce resources and add operational complexity. Measure successful task cost and tail latency rather than throughput alone. List what each optimization changes or reuses, its invalidation identity, and the quality tests it requires."},{"id":297,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"Why separate model quality from inference-system reliability?","text":"Why separate model quality from inference-system reliability? A capable model can fail through timeouts, rate limits, broken schemas, streaming errors, or unsupported settings. A reliable serving path can consistently deliver poor reasoning. Measure execution availability and behavioral quality separately. Separate inference, tool, retrieval, queue, and network costs. Provider sampling settings and context size affect both quality and operational behavior, so version them with experiments. Replacing a model to fix a parser or transaction bug wastes effort and can introduce incompatibilities. More retries cannot repair consistently unsupported reasoning. Layered measurement requires instrumentation and identifies effective changes. One end-to-end score captures impact but does not locate cause. Create incidents in model, retrieval, tool, transport, database, and frontend categories. State which signal identifies each."},{"id":298,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"How should experimentation record lineage and reproducibility?","text":"How should experimentation record lineage and reproducibility? Record code and dependency revisions, corpus, source versions, chunking, profiles, prompts, generation settings, judge configuration, and execution mode. Keep case identity stable without assuming temporary chunk UUIDs never change. Store model, prompt, context, data, code, and environment identifiers with measured outputs. Distinguish configured, observed, estimated, and unknown values in reports. Equal model names may hide provider changes. Broad dependency ranges or a context-proxy fallback can change metrics without an obvious application diff. Rich lineage costs metadata and supports meaningful comparison. A screenshot of one average is easier to share and cannot reproduce a result. Write a machine-readable experiment manifest distinguishing configured, observed, estimated, and unknown values."},{"id":299,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"What advanced topics should follow the core journey?","text":"What advanced topics should follow the core journey? Study temporal retrieval, reranker training, calibration, multimodal extraction, secure workflow persistence, distributed agent tasks, serving economics, privacy-aware evaluation, and failure-oriented operations. Add each through a concrete benchmark or product need. Choose advanced topics from measured weaknesses: multilingual retrieval, model adaptation, efficient serving, multimodal grounding, privacy, fairness, or distributed agent recovery. Run controlled experiments rather than adding every available framework. Collecting techniques without mastering ownership, transactions, and evidence creates complexity with weak guarantees. Reading an API tutorial is not operational experience. Deep understanding of complete workflows builds judgment; broad exploration is useful when tied to falsifiable experiments. Pick one advanced track and define prerequisites, a toy experiment, a held-out metric, a failure test, and a stopping condition."},{"id":300,"slug":"model-engineering","chapter":"Model engineering and the advanced learning path","title":"What demonstrates completion of the full AI full-stack learning journey?","text":"What demonstrates completion of the full AI full-stack learning journey? Build and explain the path from source bytes to authorized evidence, conversational intent to validated tools, reviewed action to atomic commit, and measured behavior to deployment and recovery. Defend alternatives with observations rather than slogans. Show an end-to-end application, evaluated outcomes, failure recovery, and defended decisions. Completion is demonstrated engineering ability rather than finishing a list of technology names. Familiarity from reading can exceed practical skill. Production service requests need real integration, security, load, and recovery evidence beyond static review. A sustained portfolio is slower than memorization and stronger evidence of competence. Finish one reliable product before adding orchestration layers. Deliver the capstone with a working assistant, held-out report, write tests, trace-privacy review, restore drill, and three defended architectural alternatives."},{"id":301,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"How is context engineering different from writing a prompt?","text":"How is context engineering different from writing a prompt? Context engineering manages the whole information presented at a decision: instructions, user intent, retrieved evidence, memory, tool schemas, tool results, and output constraints. A prompt is one part of that environment. Build a context manifest before each model call. Record which source supplied each block, its trust level, purpose, expiry, and token cost. This makes a missing fact distinguishable from a poor instruction. Mixing user documents with privileged instructions creates an injection path. Keep explicit boundaries and authorize actions outside the model. More context can improve coverage while increasing cost, distraction, and disclosure. Design a manifest for a help-desk turn and justify every block."},{"id":302,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"What belongs in a context contract?","text":"What belongs in a context contract? Define required inputs, optional evidence, trusted identities, freshness requirements, allowed tools, output schema, and an abstention policy. The contract describes the information needed to complete one bounded task. Validate the contract before inference: a missing customer identifier should trigger an authenticated lookup or clarification rather than a guessed record. Keep the server identity out of editable user fields. A syntactically complete context may still contain stale or contradictory facts. Attach version and applicability metadata. Strict prerequisites reduce unsupported answers but add clarification turns. Write contracts for factual search, refund preparation, and approved refund execution."},{"id":303,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"How should instructions, evidence, and tool output be separated?","text":"How should instructions, evidence, and tool output be separated? Give each source an explicit role and purpose. Instructions describe behavior; evidence supports factual statements; tool output reports an operation result. A retrieved paragraph never gains authority to change permissions. Use structured envelopes with origin and trust labels. Present source text as quoted data and tell the model how to use it, while enforcing tool restrictions in backend policy. Delimiters help readability but do not form a security boundary. Malicious evidence can still influence model choices. Rich envelopes improve traceability and consume tokens. Inject an instruction into a retrieved article and verify that no unauthorized tool becomes callable."},{"id":304,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"Why does context need provenance and freshness?","text":"Why does context need provenance and freshness? A fact is useful only when its source, applicable time, and revision are known. A current-looking article may describe a retired product or another customer's entitlement. Carry document ID, revision, retrieval timestamp, effective interval, and access scope through evidence assembly. Recheck authorization when a user opens the cited source. A summary that omits the effective date can turn accurate historical evidence into an incorrect present-tense answer. Fine-grained provenance increases storage and citation complexity. Compare answers for an event occurring before and after a knowledge-rule change."},{"id":305,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"What is working memory versus long-term memory?","text":"What is working memory versus long-term memory? Working memory supports the active task: selected facts, unfinished steps, and recent tool outcomes. Long-term memory persists reviewed preferences or facts across sessions with explicit retention and correction rules. Write durable memory only after validation. Store its origin, owner, confidence, expiry, and deletion policy; retrieve only entries relevant to the current task. A mistaken inference can persist and repeatedly bias future answers. Never store speculative sensitive traits as established facts. More memory reduces repeated questions but adds privacy and consistency obligations. Design a user-visible correction and deletion workflow for a remembered preference."},{"id":306,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"How should tool schemas enter context?","text":"How should tool schemas enter context? Present only tools relevant to the task and allowed for the authenticated principal. Tool names and descriptions should explain purpose, required inputs, side effects, and failure states. Filter the server registry before model exposure. A model may select among permitted actions, but the backend validates its arguments and permissions again at execution. Dynamically discovered tool descriptions can contain hostile instructions. Treat remote metadata as untrusted. A small catalog improves selection efficiency while excessive filtering can hide a needed capability. Reduce a 100-tool catalog to five tools for one support journey and evaluate missed actions."},{"id":307,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"How do you handle contradictory evidence?","text":"How do you handle contradictory evidence? Identify the conflict instead of averaging incompatible facts. Prefer the correct authority and revision only when an explicit product rule establishes precedence; otherwise ask for review or present uncertainty. Group passages by subject and applicable interval, compare relevant statements, and surface conflicting source identifiers in the answer contract. Similarity rank does not establish authority. A highly ranked outdated article can be less applicable than a lower ranked official rule. Conflict detection costs processing and may reduce answer coverage. Build a three-document fixture with one outdated and two conflicting current statements."},{"id":308,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"What should an agent retain after a tool call?","text":"What should an agent retain after a tool call? Retain the operation identifier, outcome, essential evidence, next permitted actions, and any durable state change. Large raw payloads can move to a scoped artifact store referenced by ID. Summarize tool results into a typed observation without dropping error category or confirmation status. The next model call must know whether an action completed. Replacing a failed operation with a vague success-like summary can cause false completion claims. Compact observations save tokens but can hide details needed for diagnosis. Specify a result envelope for a failed payment and a successfully prepared payment."},{"id":309,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"How is context quality evaluated?","text":"How is context quality evaluated? Measure whether necessary facts are present, unauthorized material is absent, sources are applicable, and the model can complete the intended task within budget. Evaluate the assembled context separately from the final answer. Use known-answer fixtures with evidence labels and adversarial distractors. Compare answer quality while varying one assembly policy at a time. A context policy can look effective on easy examples while failing on permissions or long conversations. More realistic fixtures are expensive to curate and reveal failures earlier. Create ten missing-evidence cases and ten irrelevant-context cases before changing your assembler."},{"id":310,"slug":"context-engineering","chapter":"Context engineering","title":"When should an agent ask a question rather than retrieve more context?","text":"When should an agent ask a question rather than retrieve more context? Ask when the missing information belongs to the user or cannot be safely inferred, such as which account they mean or what action they intend. Retrieve when an authorized system can supply the missing fact. Classify the gap as intent, identity, evidence, or system state. Route to clarification, authentication, retrieval, or an operation lookup accordingly. Repeated retrieval cannot resolve ambiguous user intent and may disclose unrelated data. Clarification slows completion while reducing costly incorrect actions. For five ambiguous requests, identify the smallest question that changes the next valid action."},{"id":311,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"How do you calculate a safe context budget?","text":"How do you calculate a safe context budget? Reserve space for instructions, tool schemas, conversation, retrieved evidence, and expected output before adding optional material. Use the provider's tokenizer or conservative model-specific accounting. Compute remaining input allowance from the model limit and output reservation, then apply per-section caps. Multimodal inputs and hidden protocol overhead may require separate estimates. A character-based guess can exceed the limit or remove important content unpredictably. Large reservations reduce available evidence but improve completion reliability. Calculate budgets for two models with different context windows and output limits."},{"id":312,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"How should evidence be selected under a token budget?","text":"How should evidence be selected under a token budget? Select passages for relevance, novelty, applicability, and source coverage rather than filling the window with the first ranked hits. A second near-duplicate passage often adds less value than a missing condition. Deduplicate by document span, group related chunks, and reserve room for contradictory or qualifying passages. Track why a candidate was included or dropped. Greedy selection can omit a long decisive clause. Evaluate answerable cases that require multiple passages. Sophisticated selection adds latency; simple baselines remain useful. Compare top-k selection with diversity-aware selection on a fixture containing repeated FAQs."},{"id":313,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"What is the difference between truncation and compression?","text":"What is the difference between truncation and compression? Truncation discards material according to a rule; compression rewrites or extracts a smaller representation. Compression can preserve more useful information, but generated summaries can introduce errors. Prefer extractive spans for authoritative numbers and conditions. If a model summarizes, retain the raw source and evaluate factual preservation before using the summary. A summary may drop a negation, exception, or unit while remaining fluent. Compression uses extra inference and can be reused across turns. Compress a rule with an exception and verify every condition against the original."},{"id":314,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"How should conversation compaction work?","text":"How should conversation compaction work? Preserve the task goal, confirmed facts, unresolved questions, operation IDs, authorization boundaries, and recent state changes. Keep recent turns verbatim when their exact wording affects intent. Generate a structured checkpoint, validate required fields, then continue with the checkpoint plus recent messages. Durable actions must be read from backend state. A summary is not a substitute for an audit log or a completed transaction record. Frequent compaction saves context but introduces recurring summarization error. Resume a long conversation from a checkpoint and check whether any completed action is repeated."},{"id":315,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"What is lost-in-the-middle behavior?","text":"What is lost-in-the-middle behavior? Models may use evidence differently depending on where it appears in a long context. The existence and size of the effect depend on the model, task, and data. Evaluate important evidence placed at the beginning, middle, and end, with realistic distractors. Adjust context structure only after observing the failure. Treating a published positional effect as a universal ordering rule can worsen a different workload. Reordering is cheap but cannot recover missing evidence. Rotate one decisive passage through a long fixture and measure supported-answer rate."},{"id":316,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"How do prompt caching and semantic caching differ?","text":"How do prompt caching and semantic caching differ? Prompt caching reuses computation for supported repeated input prefixes. Semantic caching reuses an earlier application answer for a sufficiently similar request under a defined validity policy. Keep cache keys tied to tenant, policy, model configuration, evidence version, and required freshness where relevant. Inspect provider caching rules separately from application response reuse. A semantically similar question from another user can require a different answer or authorization scope. Reuse saves latency and cost while increasing invalidation complexity. Design cache keys and invalidation tests for two users with different entitlements."},{"id":317,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"How should memory retrieval avoid overwhelming the prompt?","text":"How should memory retrieval avoid overwhelming the prompt? Retrieve a small set of task-relevant memory entries using ownership, recency, applicability, and confidence constraints. Long-term memory should remain selective. Separate user-declared preferences from inferred observations and verified system facts. Rank within the authorized scope, then apply a token budget and expiry checks. Stale inferred preferences can override a new explicit user request if presented as privileged context. Narrow retrieval reduces noise but may repeat questions when recall misses a useful fact. Introduce an expired preference and verify that the current user instruction wins."},{"id":318,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"When should you compress tool results instead of retrieving fewer records?","text":"When should you compress tool results instead of retrieving fewer records? Compress when the selected records are necessary but their representation is verbose. Retrieve fewer records when the extras do not contribute to the task. These solve different causes of context growth. Keep authoritative identifiers and outcome fields, remove repeated schema descriptions, and store detailed payloads behind access-controlled artifact references. Dropping row-level provenance can make aggregates impossible to verify. Compact schemas improve efficiency while requiring schema version discipline. Replace a verbose 50-record tool response with an aggregate and scoped drill-down IDs."},{"id":319,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"What is token efficiency, and how do you avoid optimizing the wrong metric?","text":"What is token efficiency, and how do you avoid optimizing the wrong metric? Token efficiency is useful work per cost under a quality and safety constraint. Reducing input tokens while increasing failed turns is not a product improvement. Compare total task cost, completion rate, supported-answer rate, p95 latency, and repeat calls. Count summarization and cache misses in the same experiment. A cheaper model call can produce more retries and a more expensive complete journey. Multi-metric evaluation complicates ranking alternatives but reflects real users. Run a paired comparison where both context policies face the same task fixtures."},{"id":320,"slug":"context-optimization","chapter":"Context optimization and memory budgets","title":"How should context policies be rolled out?","text":"How should context policies be rolled out? Version the assembly policy, evaluate it offline, compare it on a limited traffic slice, and retain rollback. Include permission and long-conversation cases in release gates. Record the selected policy version in traces and experiment outputs. Change one dominant factor at a time so regressions can be attributed. A silent tokenizer or model revision change can invalidate an old budget assumption. Controlled rollout delays broad savings while reducing unknown failure exposure. Write a rollout plan with a regression threshold and an explicit rollback trigger."},{"id":321,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"What makes a useful system instruction?","text":"What makes a useful system instruction? A useful instruction defines the assistant's purpose, allowed capabilities, evidence requirements, interaction style, and escalation behavior. It should make decision rules observable rather than merely assigning a persona. Separate stable product policy from task-specific instructions and user-provided data. Version the instruction alongside tool schemas and evaluation fixtures. Long conflicting instructions can obscure the rules that matter most. Concise rules are easier to maintain while examples may be needed for ambiguous behavior. Rewrite a vague helpful-assistant prompt into five testable behavior requirements."},{"id":322,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"How do role, task, context, and output specification work together?","text":"How do role, task, context, and output specification work together? The role supplies the operating perspective, the task defines the work, context supplies relevant facts, and the output contract defines the required result. None should imply capabilities the backend lacks. Use explicit sections and a small validated schema for structured answers. Include what to do when required facts are missing. A detailed role description can encourage unjustified confidence without improving evidence. Structured outputs simplify integration and reduce free-form flexibility. Design an extraction prompt that returns unknown rather than inventing a missing date."},{"id":323,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"When are zero-shot and few-shot prompts appropriate?","text":"When are zero-shot and few-shot prompts appropriate? Zero-shot prompts rely on instructions alone. Few-shot prompts add examples showing the desired mapping, especially useful when labels or output conventions are ambiguous. Include diverse normal, boundary, and abstention examples. Keep test cases separate so reported performance does not merely reflect prompt memorization. Biased examples can teach spurious formatting or demographic associations. Examples improve clarity but consume context and require maintenance. Compare a classification task with no examples and four balanced examples on a held-out set."},{"id":324,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"How do negative instructions compare with positive alternatives?","text":"How do negative instructions compare with positive alternatives? A prohibition is easier to follow when paired with an allowed replacement behavior. For example, require a clarification when a required field is absent. Turn each major restriction into a condition and action: if evidence is insufficient, state the gap and ask the smallest useful question. Repeating long lists of forbidden content can distract from the actual task and still cannot enforce permissions. Explicit alternatives add length while making behavior easier to evaluate. Replace five vague do-not rules with observable conditional actions."},{"id":325,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"How should a prompt express uncertainty?","text":"How should a prompt express uncertainty? Require the assistant to distinguish supported facts, assumptions, unresolved conflicts, and missing information. Confidence should follow an evaluated policy rather than an adjective chosen by the model. Attach source-backed statements to evidence IDs and use typed unresolved reasons for downstream handling. Numerical self-reported confidence is not automatically calibrated. More abstention can reduce harmful errors while increasing follow-up effort. Create cases that require a direct answer, a qualified answer, and an abstention."},{"id":326,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"Why is output parsing a backend responsibility?","text":"Why is output parsing a backend responsibility? The model produces untrusted output even when a schema is requested. The backend must validate structure, types, allowed values, and business invariants before downstream use. Parse with a schema validator and apply separate domain checks. Reject extra properties where they create ambiguity, and never execute returned code by default. Schema-valid output can still name an unauthorized resource or unsupported amount. Strict validation causes recoverable failures instead of silently accepting malformed data. Send a schema-valid but unauthorized record ID through your proposed handler."},{"id":327,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"How should prompt templates avoid accidental instruction injection?","text":"How should prompt templates avoid accidental instruction injection? Keep trusted instructions and variable data separate. Treat documents, user messages, and tool descriptions as data that may contain adversarial instructions. Render variables through a controlled template, label sources, and enforce action permissions in backend policy regardless of model output. Escaping characters and adding delimiters do not guarantee resistance to semantic injection. Layered controls take more work than a prompt-only defense and provide stronger boundaries. Insert a malicious instruction into a document field and inspect the resulting tool permissions."},{"id":328,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"What should be versioned with a prompt?","text":"What should be versioned with a prompt? Version the instruction, examples, tool descriptions, output schema, retrieval policy, model settings, and evaluation dataset references. An instruction string alone does not identify system behavior. Store a release manifest and attach its ID to traces and evaluation runs. Use immutable artifacts for comparisons. Changing the tool schema while retaining an old prompt version makes regressions difficult to explain. Detailed manifests add bookkeeping and enable reproducibility. Create two releases that differ only in one instruction and compare matched tasks."},{"id":329,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"What is the right way to debug a poor answer?","text":"What is the right way to debug a poor answer? Inspect the task contract, evidence, tool trajectory, model output, and parser outcome in order. A bad answer may arise before the prompt ever reaches the model. Reproduce the failure with captured non-sensitive fixtures. Change the smallest identified cause and add a regression example. Editing instructions for a retrieval bug can produce a brittle prompt that hides the real problem. Focused fixes are slower initially than ad hoc tweaking and accumulate more reliable behavior. Classify five failures as missing context, wrong tool, unsupported generation, or parser rejection."},{"id":330,"slug":"prompt-foundations","chapter":"Prompt engineering foundations","title":"How do you evaluate prompt quality beyond personal preference?","text":"How do you evaluate prompt quality beyond personal preference? Define task-specific success criteria and compare prompts on the same held-out examples. Evaluate correctness, groundedness, unsafe actions, latency, and user effort separately. Blind reviewers to prompt variant, calibrate any model judge against humans, and report subgroup and failure-category results. A fluent response can win a style preference while failing a business requirement. Human review costs time but anchors automated judging. Build a rubric that scores both factual support and whether the correct next action was taken."},{"id":331,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"When should a task be decomposed into smaller prompts?","text":"When should a task be decomposed into smaller prompts? Decompose when subtasks have different evidence, validation rules, or failure recovery. Extraction followed by validation and drafting is often easier to inspect than a single opaque request. Pass typed intermediate results, not unbounded prose. Validate each boundary before invoking the next stage. Errors in an early stage can become authoritative inputs to later stages. Decomposition improves diagnosis and adds latency and orchestration cost. Split a support response into intent extraction, authorized evidence lookup, and answer generation."},{"id":332,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"What is chain-of-thought prompting useful for, and what should users see?","text":"What is chain-of-thought prompting useful for, and what should users see? Some reasoning tasks benefit from structured intermediate work, but private internal reasoning is not a reliable audit record. Ask for concise conclusions, checkable calculations, evidence, or a plan when useful. Verify the observable result using tools or deterministic checks. Use public explanations that cite the facts needed to assess the conclusion. A persuasive reasoning narrative can rationalize an incorrect result. Additional reasoning can improve some tasks while increasing cost and latency. Compare direct answers with a short checkable calculation and validate both against known outcomes."},{"id":333,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"How does self-consistency work?","text":"How does self-consistency work? Generate multiple candidate solutions and aggregate their observable results under a defined rule. Agreement can improve some tasks but does not establish truth when samples share a bias. Normalize answers before aggregation and track the extra model cost. Require external evidence for factual questions. Five matching hallucinations are still wrong. Sampling increases compute and may be unnecessary for deterministic tool-backed tasks. Compare single-sample accuracy with majority voting on a bounded reasoning dataset."},{"id":334,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"What is retrieval-grounded prompting?","text":"What is retrieval-grounded prompting? Supply task-relevant evidence and require answers to distinguish supported statements from unresolved gaps. The retrieval and citation contracts matter as much as the instruction. Include source IDs, revisions, and applicability notes, then validate citations and critical facts after generation. A model can copy an irrelevant passage accurately and still answer the wrong question. Strong grounding improves traceability while limiting unsupported coverage. Add one irrelevant but plausible passage and check whether the answer cites the right source."},{"id":335,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"What is plan-and-execute prompting?","text":"What is plan-and-execute prompting? A planner proposes steps; an executor performs permitted actions and records outcomes. The plan is a hypothesis that may need revision when tools reveal new facts. Validate steps against capability and permission constraints, set a budget, and checkpoint durable progress. Executing a model's plan without backend checks turns a reasoning error into a system action. Separate planning improves visibility but adds state and recovery complexity. Require a plan to pause before an irreversible action and resume after approval."},{"id":336,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"When is a critique-and-revise loop helpful?","text":"When is a critique-and-revise loop helpful? Use it when a critic has a specific rubric, additional evidence, or a stronger verification method. Generic requests to improve the answer often produce stylistic churn. Bound revision count and require each revision to address an identified defect. Re-evaluate against independent acceptance criteria. A critic can hallucinate errors or remove a correct qualification. Additional passes improve some outputs and consume latency and tokens. Give the critic a citation-support rubric and measure whether factual errors actually decrease."},{"id":337,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"How should constrained decoding be distinguished from semantic validation?","text":"How should constrained decoding be distinguished from semantic validation? Constrained decoding can restrict output structure or allowed tokens. Semantic validation checks whether the selected values are true, authorized, and meaningful for the application. Use both when integrating model output with software. Apply ownership checks, numeric constraints, and state-transition rules after parsing. A perfectly valid JSON object can still request an impermissible action. Strong output constraints simplify parsing and may limit model expressiveness. Produce valid JSON containing a nonexistent resource and ensure the domain layer rejects it."},{"id":338,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"What is prompt chaining versus tool use?","text":"What is prompt chaining versus tool use? Prompt chaining connects model calls through application-controlled stages. Tool use lets a model request an external capability under host control. A workflow may combine both. Keep a typed boundary between the proposed tool call and actual execution. Feed the operation outcome back as an observation. Treating tool-call text as execution proof creates false success. Chaining is predictable; tool selection is flexible and harder to evaluate exhaustively. Draw one fixed extraction chain and one agent loop for the same task."},{"id":339,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"How should prompt injection be evaluated?","text":"How should prompt injection be evaluated? Use direct and indirect attacks that attempt data exfiltration, permission escalation, instruction replacement, and false completion. Evaluate resulting actions as well as response wording. Include attacks inside retrieved documents, OCR text, remote tool metadata, and conversation history. Verify the backend rejects forbidden operations. Passing a few known jailbreak strings does not establish general resistance. Adversarial coverage costs maintenance and exposes high-impact gaps. Add one injection fixture per untrusted input channel and define an invariant for each."},{"id":340,"slug":"prompt-techniques","chapter":"Prompt techniques and structured reasoning","title":"When should you stop optimizing the prompt?","text":"When should you stop optimizing the prompt? Stop when the dominant failures require better evidence, a reliable tool, changed product scope, or model capability rather than clearer instructions. Optimize the full system against a release target. Categorize remaining failures and estimate the expected value of the next intervention. Keep a simple baseline for comparison. Prompt complexity can grow while task reliability remains flat. Backend improvements take engineering effort but often provide durable guarantees. Rank your ten largest failures by whether a prompt change could plausibly resolve them."},{"id":341,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"What is MCP and which problem does it solve?","text":"What is MCP and which problem does it solve? Model Context Protocol standardizes how compatible hosts and clients discover and interact with server capabilities. It reduces custom integration glue for tools, resources, and reusable prompts. A host manages model interaction and policy; a client communicates with a server that exposes capabilities. Consult the negotiated protocol revision rather than assuming every server supports the same features. Interoperability does not imply trust or authorization. A common protocol helps reuse while adding transport and lifecycle responsibilities. Map a document-search integration into host, client, server, and underlying service."},{"id":342,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"How do tools, resources, and prompts differ?","text":"How do tools, resources, and prompts differ? Tools perform requested operations; resources expose addressable context; prompts expose reusable interaction templates. Their selection and use are coordinated by the host. Keep side-effecting business operations in narrowly scoped tools and use resources for read-oriented artifacts with explicit authorization. A resource URI can reveal private data if access checks are omitted. Rich capability modeling improves clarity while increasing implementation work. Classify a search function, document URI, and review template using these three concepts."},{"id":343,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"What does capability negotiation mean for an MCP integration?","text":"What does capability negotiation mean for an MCP integration? Participants communicate supported features for a protocol revision so each side can use only compatible behavior. An application must handle absent capabilities explicitly. Test the actual client and server pair, pin compatible SDK versions, and expose feature-dependent paths only when supported. Assuming a newer feature exists can break an otherwise healthy integration. Conservative compatibility reduces features but improves interoperability. Define a fallback when a server cannot provide a feature your UI would prefer."},{"id":344,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"How should a tool's input and output contracts be designed?","text":"How should a tool's input and output contracts be designed? Use precise names, small input schemas, typed outcomes, and stable identifiers. Describe side effects and missing-input behavior clearly enough for both models and humans. Validate arguments server-side and return minimal structured data plus a useful explanation. Keep protocol errors distinct from normal business failures. Descriptions and annotations are metadata, not a permission system. Rich output helps agents reason while consuming context. Design a search tool that distinguishes zero matches from backend failure."},{"id":345,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"How does MCP differ from A2A and REST?","text":"How does MCP differ from A2A and REST? REST commonly exposes application resources and operations. MCP standardizes AI-facing capability access. A2A coordinates tasks between agent systems. They can wrap the same backend while serving different contracts. Choose the smallest boundary that matches the integration. A deterministic lookup rarely needs a remote agent negotiation layer. Adding protocols can obscure which service owns authorization and durable writes. Standardization helps interoperability and adds lifecycle overhead. Explain why your account-balance lookup uses a tool while a specialist investigation may use a remote task."},{"id":346,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"Which transport should an MCP server use?","text":"Which transport should an MCP server use? Local stdio suits supervised local processes; remote HTTP transport suits network services with explicit identity and deployment controls. Supported details depend on the protocol and SDK versions. Choose based on process ownership, trust boundary, network reachability, and operational needs. Implement the lifecycle required by the selected revision. Never expose a local development server broadly merely because it starts successfully. Local processes simplify access while remote services enable shared operations and require stronger controls. Compare a developer desktop connector with a shared enterprise document connector."},{"id":347,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"Why are remote tool descriptions a trust boundary?","text":"Why are remote tool descriptions a trust boundary? A server can supply text that the host places in the model's context. Hostile metadata can try to redirect behavior before any tool is called. Approve servers, scope available tools, review metadata changes, and keep secrets outside model-visible descriptions. Backend authorization must still apply on execution. A read-only annotation cannot prove that a remote implementation has no side effects. Server review slows onboarding and reduces uncontrolled capability exposure. Create a tool description containing a malicious instruction and verify host policy remains effective."},{"id":348,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"How should MCP authorization map to backend identity?","text":"How should MCP authorization map to backend identity? Authenticate the request and map permitted scopes to an application principal. The server must authorize the requested resource and operation using trusted identity. Validate token issuer, audience, expiry, and required scopes according to the selected authorization profile. Do not pass arbitrary caller tokens through to unrelated services. A valid token for one resource may be invalid for another. Fine-grained scopes require lifecycle management and reduce blast radius. Write allow and deny cases for two tenants using the same tool name."},{"id":349,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"What should cancellation and timeouts mean?","text":"What should cancellation and timeouts mean? Cancellation stops work where possible and communicates the resulting operation state. It does not reverse an already committed side effect. Propagate deadlines, retain operation identifiers, and provide a status lookup for uncertain outcomes. Follow the protocol revision's cancellation behavior. A client disconnect can leave a server operation running. Durable status adds storage while supporting safe recovery. Disconnect during a simulated write and determine whether retry would duplicate it."},{"id":350,"slug":"mcp-foundations","chapter":"MCP foundations and capability contracts","title":"What is a good first MCP project?","text":"What is a good first MCP project? Build a read-only knowledge-search server with synthetic documents, an authenticated service adapter, structured results, and clear empty versus failure outcomes. Start with one tool, test compatible clients, and add access-control and injection cases before write tools. Keep provider secrets in server configuration. A toy search demo is not proof of production readiness. Narrow scope makes the protocol learnable before distributed security complexity. Deliver a client transcript, authorization matrix, and failure-recovery demonstration."},{"id":351,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How should an MCP server be layered?","text":"How should an MCP server be layered? Keep transport handling, tool schemas, application services, and persistence adapters separate. The MCP handler should translate requests rather than embed the entire business workflow. Inject a service with a narrow interface and derive the principal from request authentication. Reuse the same application service from HTTP and MCP adapters. Duplicated business logic can enforce different permissions through different protocols. Layering adds interfaces while keeping behavior consistent. Implement one search service behind both an HTTP route and an MCP tool."},{"id":352,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How do you create a minimal Python MCP tool?","text":"How do you create a minimal Python MCP tool? Use a supported official SDK interface, define a typed function, and expose it through a server with an explicitly selected transport. Keep the first example read-only. The accompanying SDK example uses the v1 FastMCP style and synthetic data. Pin a compatible v1 SDK; evaluate migration separately for another major version. A minimal decorator example omits production authentication, quotas, and shutdown handling. SDK convenience reduces boilerplate and introduces version coupling. Run the example locally with a compatible client and inspect the advertised schema."},{"id":353,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How should tool argument validation work?","text":"How should tool argument validation work? Validate shape, length, allowed identifiers, numeric bounds, and application invariants before contacting downstream services. Derive tenant and user scope outside model arguments. Reject arbitrary SQL, paths, or URLs unless the capability is explicitly designed and constrained for them. Use allowlisted operations and parameter binding. A well-typed string can still contain a dangerous destination or a cross-tenant identifier. Narrow inputs reduce flexibility and improve control. Try oversized queries, unexpected properties, and unauthorized resource IDs."},{"id":354,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How do you secure a remote MCP deployment?","text":"How do you secure a remote MCP deployment? Apply request authentication, resource authorization, encrypted transport, origin and host validation where relevant, outbound restrictions, quotas, and secret management. Follow the selected protocol security guidance and deploy behind an explicit ingress policy. Log security outcomes without storing credentials. A reverse proxy does not automatically validate application-level permissions or block server-side request forgery. More controls add operational work and reduce exposure. Threat-model both a malicious client and a compromised downstream API."},{"id":355,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How should tool errors be modeled?","text":"How should tool errors be modeled? Separate transport or protocol failures from expected application outcomes such as not found, permission denied, unavailable, and needs confirmation. Return stable machine-readable categories. Map internal exceptions to a controlled envelope and preserve a trace or operation ID for support. Do not expose stack traces or query strings containing private data. Returning an empty list for every failure causes an agent to misreport outages as no matches. Typed outcomes require client handling and support reliable decisions. Simulate one normal empty result and one timeout and compare agent behavior."},{"id":356,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How should write tools require human confirmation?","text":"How should write tools require human confirmation? Preparation should produce a reviewable operation with exact parameters, owner, expiry, and revision. Execution should validate an approval bound to that operation. Keep approval verification and the business transaction server-side. A natural-language yes or model-provided approved flag is insufficient. Reusing approval after parameters change can authorize a different action. Two-step operations add interaction and reduce accidental changes. Change the amount after approval and verify execution rejects the stale approval."},{"id":357,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How do you control concurrency and retries for tools?","text":"How do you control concurrency and retries for tools? Apply per-tenant quotas, bounded execution concurrency, downstream deadlines, and an idempotency policy for side effects. Retries must depend on outcome and operation semantics. Keep the same durable operation key across retry attempts, and query status after an ambiguous failure. Avoid holding database locks during remote model calls. Retrying a timeout can repeat a committed write. Conservative retry limits may surface more transient failures while protecting correctness. Inject a timeout immediately after commit and recover through the operation ledger."},{"id":358,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"What should MCP observability record?","text":"What should MCP observability record? Record tool name, schema version, permitted principal category, latency, outcome, retry count, and trace linkage. Store arguments only under a reviewed privacy policy. Use an allowlist of safe attributes and attach task or operation IDs across adapters. Separate user-visible outcomes from instrumentation success. A tool returning a domain failure may still look like a successful function call. Detailed payload logging improves diagnosis and increases disclosure risk. Show metrics that distinguish validation rejection, denied access, empty search, and provider outage."},{"id":359,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How should MCP contract tests be organized?","text":"How should MCP contract tests be organized? Test schema discovery, normal execution, denied access, malformed inputs, cancellation, timeouts, and client-version compatibility. Add backend invariants separately. Use synthetic data and a sandbox service. Assert what actions occurred, not only what text the agent returned. Mock-only tests cannot establish behavior of real transport framing or token validation. End-to-end tests cost more and catch boundary failures unit tests miss. Publish a small compatibility matrix for the clients and protocol revisions you actually ran."},{"id":360,"slug":"mcp-server-engineering","chapter":"Build and secure an MCP server","title":"How should tool changes be released?","text":"How should tool changes be released? Treat schema, semantic, permission, and outcome changes as API changes. Version incompatible contracts and evaluate tool selection after description edits. Pin the deployed artifact, announce deprecations, retain rollback, and compare tool trajectories before expanding traffic. Renaming a field can break stored plans or cached tool metadata. Backward compatibility costs maintenance and prevents abrupt client failure. Design a migration from one search field to a structured query without silently changing behavior."},{"id":361,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"What is an agent skill?","text":"What is an agent skill? A skill is a reusable package of task instructions and optional resources or scripts. It tells a compatible agent how to perform a category of work using available capabilities. Discover concise metadata first, then load detailed instructions when relevant. Keep the skill's purpose, triggers, dependencies, and limits explicit. Loading a skill does not establish that its instructions are trustworthy or that its scripts are safe. Packaging expertise reduces repeated prompting while adding version and supply-chain management. Write a skill for reviewing a support response against an evidence rubric."},{"id":362,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"How do skills differ from tools and system instructions?","text":"How do skills differ from tools and system instructions? System instructions define product behavior and constraints. Skills supply task-specific methods. Tools provide executable capabilities. A skill can explain tool use without granting tool authority. Resolve instructions through the actual host's precedence rules and enforce permissions in backend execution. Different products may package skills differently. A skill that claims to override host policy creates confusion and potential unsafe behavior. Separate layers improve reuse and require clear ownership. Draw which layer can propose a refund and which layer can authorize it."},{"id":363,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"What is progressive disclosure for skills?","text":"What is progressive disclosure for skills? Load a small description for routing, the main skill instructions when needed, and supporting references only for the selected task. This avoids inserting every skill into every turn. Keep the entry document short and link narrowly scoped supporting material. Measure both skill-selection accuracy and context cost. Vague metadata can select the wrong skill or hide a useful one. Fine-grained skills save context and increase discovery complexity. Compare loading ten complete skills with metadata-first discovery on the same tasks."},{"id":364,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"How should skill layers be organized?","text":"How should skill layers be organized? Separate organization-wide rules, application policy, role-specific methods, task skills, and local working context. The host must define actual precedence and conflict behavior. Keep stable constraints outside editable task packages. Skills should reference reviewed policy rather than duplicate it inconsistently. An outdated copied policy can silently disagree with a newer application rule. Centralized policy supports consistency and may reduce team autonomy. Create a conflict fixture where a task skill requests a prohibited data export."},{"id":365,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"How should a skill invoke scripts safely?","text":"How should a skill invoke scripts safely? A skill may describe scripts, but execution requires a controlled runtime, known inputs, constrained filesystem access, and appropriate credentials. Review code and dependencies separately from prose. Prefer deterministic utilities with narrow arguments and synthetic fixtures. Run untrusted scripts in a sandbox with explicit resource and network limits. Prompt text cannot make arbitrary shell execution safe. Sandboxing limits flexibility and contains failures. Add a script that validates an answer schema without network access or secrets."},{"id":366,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"How should skills be versioned and tested?","text":"How should skills be versioned and tested? Version the package, referenced resources, scripts, dependencies, and supported tool contracts. Evaluate whether the skill is selected and whether it improves task completion. Maintain positive, negative, and conflict-trigger examples, plus an end-to-end task suite. Record the loaded skill version in traces. A changed tool schema can invalidate an unchanged skill. Compatibility metadata adds effort and enables reproducible use. Release a revised skill against both old and new tool contracts and document the boundary."},{"id":367,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"How do you prevent skill sprawl?","text":"How do you prevent skill sprawl? Consolidate overlapping methods only when they share a real responsibility. Give each skill a clear trigger and measurable outcome rather than creating one for every small preference. Review retrieval collisions, unused skills, and duplicated policy. Keep references task-oriented and remove obsolete instructions through a controlled review. Overlapping skills can introduce conflicting workflows and waste context. Broad skills are easy to discover and may load unnecessary detail. Audit five overlapping writing skills and define the smallest useful set."},{"id":368,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"How should secrets and customer data be handled in skills?","text":"How should secrets and customer data be handled in skills? Store neither credentials nor live customer examples in reusable skill files. Use runtime secret injection and synthetic or approved redacted examples. Treat uploaded resources and referenced URLs as potentially untrusted. Scope any fetched data to the current principal and task. A private repository does not prevent accidental secret exposure in model context or logs. Redaction reduces diagnostic detail and supports safer reuse. Scan a skill package for embedded tokens, real identifiers, and unsupported external fetches."},{"id":369,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"Can one skill be portable across agent frameworks?","text":"Can one skill be portable across agent frameworks? The procedure may be portable while discovery metadata, filesystem conventions, tool binding, and execution policy remain host-specific. Separate the reusable method from integration adapters. Document required capabilities and provide framework-specific wrappers only when needed. Test the same task contract in each supported host. Claiming portability without testing can conceal missing tools or different instruction precedence. A common core reduces duplication while adapters add maintenance. Port a read-only review skill between two runtimes without copying secret configuration."},{"id":370,"slug":"skills-layers","chapter":"Skills and layered agent capabilities","title":"What should a skills portfolio demonstrate?","text":"What should a skills portfolio demonstrate? Show task selection, progressive loading, safe tool use, controlled scripts, measurable quality improvement, and conflict handling. A directory of instruction files alone proves little about outcomes. Publish synthetic fixtures, before-and-after evaluations, a version manifest, and a short failure walkthrough. Overfitting a skill to demonstration prompts can inflate apparent quality. A narrow well-evaluated skill is easier to trust than a broad unsupported capability claim. Build one skill and demonstrate a task it deliberately refuses or escalates."},{"id":371,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"When is a fixed workflow better than an autonomous agent?","text":"When is a fixed workflow better than an autonomous agent? Prefer a fixed workflow when the valid sequence is known and business rules dominate. Use an agent where choosing among tools or exploring uncertain evidence adds measurable value. Put deterministic validation and writes in application services. Limit model discretion to decisions that benefit from language understanding or flexible planning. An autonomous loop can add latency and unpredictable paths to an otherwise simple operation. Fixed flows are easier to verify and less adaptable to unexpected tasks. Compare a password-reset workflow with an open-ended technical investigation."},{"id":372,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How should orchestration state be modeled?","text":"How should orchestration state be modeled? Represent goal, principal reference, completed steps, pending actions, evidence IDs, budget, and terminal status explicitly. Keep durable operation outcomes separate from model-generated plans. Use typed transitions and immutable identifiers. Persist checkpoints at recoverable boundaries and validate loaded state before resuming. A checkpoint can be stale relative to the business database. Explicit state adds modeling work and makes restart behavior understandable. Resume after a crash between a completed tool call and the next model call."},{"id":373,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"What is fan-out and fan-in orchestration?","text":"What is fan-out and fan-in orchestration? Fan-out runs independent tasks concurrently; fan-in combines their results under a declared policy. Parallel execution is useful only when tasks do not require each other's outputs. Bound concurrency and define whether aggregation requires all results, a quorum, or best-effort partial output. Preserve per-branch errors and provenance. One slow branch can delay completion or cause unbounded task growth. Parallelism reduces latency and raises peak load and coordination cost. Search three authorized corpora concurrently and report one failed branch without hiding it."},{"id":374,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How should dependencies be represented?","text":"How should dependencies be represented? Use a directed dependency graph when tasks require earlier outputs. Validate that the graph is acyclic unless a bounded loop has deliberate semantics. Execute ready nodes, propagate typed outputs, and make failure handling explicit per edge. A task plan does not establish permission. Incorrect dependency declarations can execute a write before validation. Graphs improve visibility while increasing orchestration machinery. Model extract, validate, review, and submit with a required approval edge."},{"id":375,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How does durable execution differ from retrying the whole agent?","text":"How does durable execution differ from retrying the whole agent? Durable execution resumes from recorded progress instead of blindly repeating every prior action. It requires deliberate handling of nondeterministic work and external side effects. Persist operation keys and results, and make repeated execution safe at each resumable boundary. Checkpoint behavior depends on the runtime and version. Replaying a node can duplicate a write if the business operation is not idempotent. Durability adds storage and state evolution work. Demonstrate a crash after external execution but before checkpoint persistence."},{"id":376,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"What should an orchestrator do when one tool fails?","text":"What should an orchestrator do when one tool fails? Classify failure by cause and effect: invalid input, denied access, transient outage, uncertain completion, or terminal business rejection. Select retry, clarification, status lookup, fallback, or escalation accordingly. Apply a remaining-deadline budget and preserve the original operation ID across uncertain retries. Treating every exception as retryable can multiply writes or provider load. Detailed policies require maintenance and avoid broad unsafe retries. Write a failure table with an allowed recovery action for each category."},{"id":377,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How should human approval be represented in a workflow?","text":"How should human approval be represented in a workflow? Approval is a server-verified event bound to a specific principal, operation, parameters, expiry, and revision. A waiting workflow state should reference that approval requirement. Resume only after checking the approval and current business state. Reject stale approvals after material parameter changes. A graph interrupt or chat message is not an authorization proof. Explicit approvals add latency and auditability. Modify an approved operation and verify that the workflow returns to review."},{"id":378,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How should shared memory work across multiple agents?","text":"How should shared memory work across multiple agents? Share only scoped facts and artifacts that each participant needs. Use a coordinator-owned task record and explicit output contracts instead of a writable universal scratchpad. Track who produced each fact and which service can verify it. Merge conflicts through rules rather than last-writer-wins prose. One agent's unsupported assumption can spread to all others. Restricted sharing reduces context reuse and limits error propagation. Have two agents disagree on a version and require evidence-based reconciliation."},{"id":379,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How do you prevent orchestration cost explosions?","text":"How do you prevent orchestration cost explosions? Bound total model calls, tool calls, elapsed time, parallel tasks, and spend per user task. Include retries and critic loops in the same budget. Stop at an explicit terminal state and explain incomplete work using recorded outcomes. Apply fleet-level quotas in addition to local semaphores. Nested agents can each obey a local limit while exceeding the parent task budget. Tight budgets reduce task coverage and control operational risk. Build a parent budget that every child operation must reserve before execution."},{"id":380,"slug":"agent-orchestration","chapter":"Agent orchestration and durable workflows","title":"How should orchestration be evaluated?","text":"How should orchestration be evaluated? Measure end-to-end outcomes, permission invariants, valid state transitions, recovery behavior, latency, and total task cost. Inspect trajectories to explain failures. Use deterministic replay where possible, sandbox external effects, and inject crashes at durable boundaries. Matching one reference sequence can reject alternative valid solutions. Invariant-based evaluation accepts valid diversity and requires thoughtful specifications. Evaluate two different successful tool sequences against the same business invariants."},{"id":381,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"What is the ReAct pattern?","text":"What is the ReAct pattern? ReAct interleaves model decisions with external actions and observations. The host executes permitted tools and returns outcomes so the next decision can use actual evidence. Bound the loop and retain typed observations. Evaluate whether the agent chooses suitable tools and reacts correctly to failures. A loop can repeat unproductive actions or mistake a proposed call for completion. Flexible exploration costs more than a fixed path. Implement a three-step read-only loop and stop when the evidence contract is satisfied."},{"id":382,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"When should you use a router pattern?","text":"When should you use a router pattern? A router sends a task to a specialized workflow or capability based on intent and required authority. Routing can be deterministic, model-assisted, or hybrid. Validate route labels and provide an ambiguous or unsupported path. Evaluate routing independently from specialist quality. A routing error can send sensitive data to the wrong service. Specialization improves focus while increasing handoff and monitoring needs. Route billing, product, and unsupported requests with a confidence-independent fallback rule."},{"id":383,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"What is a planner-executor pattern?","text":"What is a planner-executor pattern? A planner proposes work, while an executor applies validated steps and returns observations. This separates flexible decomposition from constrained action. Restrict executable step types, enforce budgets, and revise the plan when an observation invalidates an assumption. A polished plan can still contain impossible or unauthorized steps. Planning improves visibility and may be wasteful for short tasks. Reject a plan step that references a tool absent from the permitted registry."},{"id":384,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"When does a supervisor-worker pattern help?","text":"When does a supervisor-worker pattern help? A supervisor coordinates specialists that solve bounded subproblems with explicit inputs and outputs. It helps when specialist responsibilities genuinely differ. Give workers narrow tools, independent budgets, and artifact contracts. The supervisor verifies outputs before aggregating them. The supervisor can become a bottleneck or accept unsupported worker summaries. Specialist depth adds calls, latency, and integration complexity. Compare one capable agent with three specialists on the same investigation fixtures."},{"id":385,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"How does a handoff differ from delegation?","text":"How does a handoff differ from delegation? Delegation keeps coordination with the original agent while a worker performs a subtask. A handoff transfers responsibility or interaction control to another participant under a defined protocol. Preserve task identity, user-visible ownership, and authorization context across the boundary. Define who communicates completion and failure. Ambiguous ownership can leave a user waiting while both agents assume the other will respond. Handoffs improve specialization and complicate continuity. Design a handoff record with the next owner, reason, evidence, and pending action."},{"id":386,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"What is an evaluator-optimizer pattern?","text":"What is an evaluator-optimizer pattern? A generator proposes output and an evaluator checks it against an explicit rubric, producing targeted revision feedback. The loop stops when acceptance criteria or a budget is reached. Use a critic with appropriate evidence and calibrate it against known results. Keep the final gate independent when possible. Correlated generator and critic errors can create false confidence. Iteration can improve quality while increasing cost and over-editing risk. Measure factual correctness before and after revision instead of measuring stylistic change."},{"id":387,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"When is a parallel ensemble appropriate?","text":"When is a parallel ensemble appropriate? Use an ensemble when independent approaches provide useful diversity and you have a reliable aggregation or verification method. More agents alone do not guarantee better reasoning. Vary evidence or method deliberately and compare outputs under a shared factual rubric. Count total cost and disagreement. Identical models with identical context may repeat the same mistake. Diversity improves robustness in some workloads and adds latency or cost. Compare one solver against two independent approaches on ambiguous cases."},{"id":388,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"What is a tool-first deterministic pattern?","text":"What is a tool-first deterministic pattern? Resolve exact facts and validated operations through deterministic services before asking a model to explain them. The model handles language while the backend remains the authority. Route known identifiers to owner-scoped lookups and use generation only after receiving a typed result. A tool can still return stale or incorrectly scoped data if its backend contract is weak. Deterministic paths reduce flexibility and support strong guarantees. Answer a request-status question without semantic search or model-selected resource IDs."},{"id":389,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"What are saga and outbox patterns useful for in agent systems?","text":"What are saga and outbox patterns useful for in agent systems? A saga coordinates multi-step business changes with compensating actions; an outbox records durable downstream work in the same transaction as a business change. They solve different distributed consistency problems. Keep model decisions separate from the transaction. Make workers idempotent and compensation semantics explicit. Compensation may not fully reverse real-world effects such as a sent email. Eventual consistency improves availability and requires recovery tooling. Model a reservation plus notification workflow and identify which failures can be compensated."},{"id":390,"slug":"agent-patterns","chapter":"Agent design patterns and trade-offs","title":"How should you choose an agent pattern?","text":"How should you choose an agent pattern? Start from the task's uncertainty, authority, state, latency budget, and recovery needs. Choose the simplest structure that meets measured requirements. Compare candidates on the same fixtures and document why added coordination improves outcomes. Patterns can be combined at clear boundaries. Architecture diagrams can look sophisticated without improving completion rate. Simplicity limits flexibility and reduces operational burden. Defend one fixed workflow and one agent pattern using failure and cost evidence."},{"id":391,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"What is an agent harness?","text":"What is an agent harness? A harness is the surrounding execution environment: model invocation, tool dispatch, context assembly, state, permissions, budgets, event handling, observability, and recovery. The model is one component inside it. Make the run lifecycle explicit and give each task a durable ID. Keep authoritative writes in application services reachable only through validated dispatch. A powerful model in an unconstrained harness can amplify errors through its tools. Strong runtime controls take engineering effort and improve predictability. Draw the harness around a model and identify every external effect it can initiate."},{"id":392,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should stop conditions be designed?","text":"How should stop conditions be designed? Stop on verified completion, denied action, exhausted budget, cancellation, unrecoverable failure, or a request for necessary clarification. Completion must reference observable results. Check limits before each model and tool operation. Detect repeated equivalent calls and preserve a final reason code. Natural-language declarations of done can precede actual persistence. Conservative stopping reduces runaway work and may interrupt recoverable tasks. Trigger each terminal state in a synthetic run and inspect its user-facing explanation."},{"id":393,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should tool dispatch enforce policy?","text":"How should tool dispatch enforce policy? Validate the requested tool against the run's permitted registry, parse arguments, bind trusted identity, check resource access, and apply a deadline before execution. Record a typed outcome and operation identifier. Treat the model's arguments as proposals rather than trusted execution parameters. A schema validator cannot establish ownership or approval. Centralized dispatch adds a consistent policy boundary and can become a bottleneck. Submit a valid tool schema with another user's record ID and verify denial."},{"id":394,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should shell or code execution be sandboxed?","text":"How should shell or code execution be sandboxed? Use an isolated environment with restricted files, network, process capabilities, credentials, runtime, and output size. Review whether the task needs execution at all. Supply synthetic fixtures or scoped artifacts and export only approved results. Tear down the environment and redact outputs before tracing. Containerization alone does not prove isolation from every host resource or network destination. Strong isolation reduces convenience and limits blast radius. Run a harmless script that attempts to read an unmounted file and verify rejection."},{"id":395,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should budgets be implemented across nested calls?","text":"How should budgets be implemented across nested calls? Use a shared task budget with reservations for model calls, tools, elapsed time, and estimated spend. Reconcile estimated and actual usage after each operation. Children inherit remaining limits and cannot reset the parent's budget. Stop scheduling new work when the deadline or capacity is exhausted. Per-agent counters do not bound a fleet of nested agents. Accurate accounting adds complexity and prevents hidden cost multiplication. Simulate two children competing for the final allowed model call."},{"id":396,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should harness state survive restarts?","text":"How should harness state survive restarts? Persist task status, completed operation IDs, checkpoint versions, and pending work in a durable store. Reconstruct context from verified state after restart. Use schema versions for checkpoints and reject unsupported migrations. Recover uncertain operations through their authoritative status endpoint. A memory-only checkpoint disappears during deployment or process failure. Durable state adds operational cost and supports recovery. Restart immediately after a write commits and verify that it is not executed again."},{"id":397,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should cancellation propagate?","text":"How should cancellation propagate? Carry a cancellation signal and absolute deadline through model requests, tools, child tasks, and stream writers. Record what work stopped and what already completed. Cancel cooperative tasks, close connections, and retain operation IDs for uncertain effects. Provide a task-status query independent of the stream. Cancelling a coroutine does not roll back an external transaction that already committed. Waiting for cleanup increases cancellation latency and reduces orphaned work. Disconnect the browser during a tool call and inspect both server state and the next retry."},{"id":398,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should a harness defend against injected instructions?","text":"How should a harness defend against injected instructions? Keep permissions, approved destinations, tool availability, and secret access outside model control. Minimize untrusted context and evaluate attacks at every input channel. A policy gate checks the proposed action against trusted principal and task state. Sensitive actions require bound approval. A classifier or safety prompt can miss an attack and should not be the only control. Layered controls add false rejections and prevent high-impact unauthorized effects. Place an exfiltration request in an OCR result and verify outbound restrictions remain active."},{"id":399,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"How should runtime events be designed?","text":"How should runtime events be designed? Emit typed, ordered events with task, run, sequence, timestamp, and version fields. Separate model text, tool progress, durable completion, errors, and cancellation. Persist only events required for recovery or audit, and create a user-safe projection for streaming. Never expose private chain-of-thought as a progress feed. Raw internal events can contain secrets and misleading intermediate claims. Stable public events require adaptation when providers change. Translate two provider event formats into one documented UI contract."},{"id":400,"slug":"agent-harness","chapter":"Agent harnesses, runtime control, and secure execution","title":"What should a production harness dashboard show?","text":"What should a production harness dashboard show? Show active and queued tasks, completion and failure rates, policy denials, uncertain operations, budget exhaustion, p95 latency, spend, and provider health. Drill down through safe trace identifiers. Distinguish infrastructure failures from business rejections and quality failures. Alert on sustained symptoms tied to user impact. A green HTTP-success graph can hide tools returning domain errors. Detailed categories increase instrumentation work and improve diagnosis. Demonstrate a run that returns HTTP 200 but fails its task invariant."},{"id":401,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"Why does an agent need evaluation even when its answers look good?","text":"Why does an agent need evaluation even when its answers look good? An agent can write convincing text while choosing the wrong tool, exposing data, repeating a transaction, or failing to finish. Evaluation checks outcomes and the process that produced them. Define task success and forbidden effects before implementation. Combine deterministic assertions, human review, and calibrated model judging where appropriate. A style-only score can reward unsafe behavior. Broad evaluation costs effort and prevents expensive hidden failures. Create a fluent answer paired with a duplicate write and ensure it fails the suite."},{"id":402,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How should evaluation datasets be partitioned?","text":"How should evaluation datasets be partitioned? Separate development examples, prompt-tuning data, held-out release cases, and ongoing production monitoring samples. Keep adversarial and regression sets visible as distinct categories. Track provenance and versioning, prevent duplicate leakage across splits, and retain hard cases after fixes. Repeatedly tuning against the release set turns it into training data. Larger held-out sets improve confidence and cost more to curate. Deduplicate a dataset by task template and source document, not only exact text."},{"id":403,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"What is the difference between outcome and trajectory evaluation?","text":"What is the difference between outcome and trajectory evaluation? Outcome evaluation checks the final answer or durable business result. Trajectory evaluation checks tool choices, arguments, ordering constraints, permissions, and recovery behavior along the way. Use invariants when multiple sequences are valid. Reserve exact sequence matching for workflows whose order is actually required. A correct final record may hide an unauthorized read that happened earlier. Trajectory checks improve diagnosis and require richer event capture. Accept two valid search sequences but reject either if it crosses a tenant boundary."},{"id":404,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How should model judges be calibrated?","text":"How should model judges be calibrated? Compare judge labels with reviewed human labels across representative failures. Measure agreement, systematic bias, and sensitivity to irrelevant wording before trusting the score. Use an explicit rubric and structured reasons, randomize answer order, and keep judged content untrusted. Recalibrate after judge changes. A judge can favor its own style or follow injected scoring instructions. Human calibration is costly and anchors automation. Build a disagreement table and revise the rubric before increasing judge volume."},{"id":405,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How do Ragas, DeepEval, and Promptfoo fit different needs?","text":"How do Ragas, DeepEval, and Promptfoo fit different needs? Ragas provides RAG-oriented evaluation components, DeepEval supplies model-testing abstractions and metrics, and Promptfoo supports configuration-driven comparisons and assertions. Their metrics and defaults are not interchangeable. Choose the library that fits your workflow, inputs, privacy requirements, and required assertions. Pin a compatible version and document your judge configuration. A library name or metric label does not establish measurement validity. Multiple tools can fill gaps and duplicate infrastructure. Run the same small dataset through two tools and explain score differences from their contracts."},{"id":406,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How do tracing platforms support evaluation?","text":"How do tracing platforms support evaluation? Traces reveal actual inputs, tools, timing, and outcomes so evaluation failures can be diagnosed. Platforms such as LangSmith, Phoenix, and TruLens connect observation with experiment or feedback workflows in different ways. Capture safe trace identifiers and attach evaluation labels to immutable run versions. Evaluate retention and data export policies before sending payloads. Rich traces can leak private user or document content. Hosted workflows simplify collaboration and add privacy and cost decisions. Diagnose one failed task using metadata-only traces and identify what additional redacted evidence is necessary."},{"id":407,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How should performance and quality be reported together?","text":"How should performance and quality be reported together? Report completion, unsupported-answer rate, policy violations, p50 and p95 latency, and total cost per completed task. Include sample sizes and uncertainty rather than one blended score. Use paired fixtures when comparing releases and segment by task difficulty and user context. Count retries and failed tasks in cost. Average latency can hide severe tail delays or one vulnerable subgroup. Multi-dimensional reporting is harder to summarize and supports honest trade-offs. Build a comparison table where one variant is cheaper but fails more difficult tasks."},{"id":408,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How should side-effecting agents be evaluated safely?","text":"How should side-effecting agents be evaluated safely? Use sandbox services, synthetic users, isolated databases, and explicit effect ledgers. Assert that only permitted writes occur and reset fixtures between runs. Separate replayed model decisions from live provider experiments. Never point a benchmark at real payment or notification systems. A mocked success response can conceal real transaction or concurrency failures. Realistic isolated tests require infrastructure and establish stronger behavior evidence. Race two confirmation requests against a disposable database and count durable records."},{"id":409,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"How should online feedback differ from offline evaluation?","text":"How should online feedback differ from offline evaluation? Online feedback reflects real user experience but is selective and often incomplete. Offline evaluation provides controlled repeatability but may miss new task distributions. Use both. Review sampled failures, add approved examples to datasets, and monitor changes in task mix and rejection rates. Positive feedback is not proof of factual correctness or privacy compliance. Production learning improves relevance and requires careful data governance. Define a process for converting a redacted support incident into a reproducible test fixture."},{"id":410,"slug":"evaluation-engineering","chapter":"Evaluation engineering and release decisions","title":"What should block an agent release?","text":"What should block an agent release? Block on breached safety invariants, unacceptable task failure, degraded recovery, or a measured performance regression beyond the agreed threshold. Define these gates before seeing results. Require immutable release manifests and a rollback path. Distinguish critical deterministic failures from noisy judge-score fluctuations. Choosing a threshold after evaluation invites cherry-picking. Strict gates slow release and protect known obligations. Write a release policy with one absolute invariant, one statistical quality target, and one latency budget."},{"id":411,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"Where should an agent sit in a backend architecture?","text":"Where should an agent sit in a backend architecture? Place the agent behind authenticated API boundaries and in front of narrowly scoped application services. The model proposes decisions while the services own validation and state changes. Share domain services across web routes, background workers, and tool adapters. Keep framework-specific event objects out of domain models. Allowing a model to access persistence directly bypasses consistent policy. Adapters add code and allow the runtime to evolve independently. Draw a dependency graph that prevents a tool from importing the web framework's request object."},{"id":412,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How should authenticated identity reach a tool?","text":"How should authenticated identity reach a tool? Derive identity from verified backend authentication and pass it through trusted runtime context or explicit service arguments. Never ask the model to supply its own tenant authority. Bind a principal at the run boundary, reset task-local context reliably, and authorize each resource inside the service. A shared global variable can leak identity across concurrent requests. Explicit identity arguments are verbose and make ownership visible. Run two users concurrently and assert that each tool sees only its own scope."},{"id":413,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How do tools reuse business services without duplicating rules?","text":"How do tools reuse business services without duplicating rules? A tool adapter parses model arguments, calls a domain service with trusted identity, and translates a typed result. The service performs business validation and transaction ownership. Keep the same service contract for HTTP and agent callers. Test permissions once at the service boundary and adapter behavior separately. Independent validation copies can diverge after a business rule changes. Reuse constrains adapter flexibility and preserves consistent outcomes. Implement one request-preparation service with both an API route and a tool wrapper."},{"id":414,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How should long-running agent work be exposed over HTTP?","text":"How should long-running agent work be exposed over HTTP? Accept a task, return a durable task ID, process it under worker supervision, and expose status plus an optional event stream. Completion should survive a disconnected browser. Persist task state and use owner-scoped status and cancellation endpoints. Distinguish acceptance from completion in response codes and UI text. Holding one request open is not durable task storage. Jobs add infrastructure and support recoverable long operations. Submit a task, close the browser, and retrieve its final status later."},{"id":415,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How do transaction boundaries interact with model calls?","text":"How do transaction boundaries interact with model calls? Keep slow model and network work outside database lock windows. Revalidate current state inside the short transaction that applies a business change. Prepare intent first, then lock or compare versions during commit and record downstream jobs atomically. Data can change between preparation and commit, so an earlier validation is insufficient. Revalidation adds complexity and avoids prolonged locks. Change a record after planning and verify commit rejects the stale revision."},{"id":416,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How should agent-side idempotency work?","text":"How should agent-side idempotency work? Use a durable operation identity tied to the intended business effect. Repeated requests should return or recover the same authoritative result when the contract permits. Enforce a uniqueness constraint or conditional transition in the database and keep request-level model-call deduplication separate. A short-lived in-memory lock cannot establish exactly-once business effects. Durable ledgers cost storage and enable safe retries. Retry after a simulated response loss and verify only one business record exists."},{"id":417,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"What should a typed tool result contain?","text":"What should a typed tool result contain? Include operation ID, outcome category, safe data, retryability, and next permitted action. For writes, include the authoritative record or task reference when completed. Translate exceptions into stable categories while retaining private diagnostic details only in protected logs. Returning success true after merely scheduling work can mislead the UI. Explicit result types require more branches and prevent ambiguous completion. Model prepared, accepted, completed, rejected, unavailable, and uncertain outcomes separately."},{"id":418,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How should rate limits be applied to agent workloads?","text":"How should rate limits be applied to agent workloads? Limit requests, concurrent runs, model tokens, tool calls, and downstream operations according to tenant and service capacity. A single HTTP request can initiate substantial work. Use shared fleet-wide quotas where needed and propagate an absolute deadline across retries and child tasks. Local process counters multiply when workers scale. Shared accounting adds latency and coordinates real capacity. Calculate the worst-case tool load from ten requests with nested agent loops."},{"id":419,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How should provider adapters be designed?","text":"How should provider adapters be designed? Normalize model invocation, cancellation, usage, errors, and public event projections behind an interface, while preserving provider-specific limitations. Keep capability flags and compatible configuration explicit. Evaluate the same task contract when switching providers. A common API can hide differences in tool calls, structured outputs, or streaming semantics. Portability reduces coupling and may limit access to specialized features. Switch between two stub providers with different event sequences without changing the UI contract."},{"id":420,"slug":"backend-agents","chapter":"Backend integration with agents","title":"How should backend integration be demonstrated in a portfolio?","text":"How should backend integration be demonstrated in a portfolio? Show one complete journey with verified identity, tool use, durable writes, streaming updates, cancellation, and recovery. Include the failure path and measured evaluation results. Provide architecture diagrams, synthetic fixtures, a release manifest, and a recorded restart demonstration. A chat screenshot does not prove authorization or transactional correctness. A narrow robust scenario is less flashy and stronger engineering evidence. Demonstrate the same operation through HTTP and an agent, then explain the shared service boundary."},{"id":421,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"When should an AI application use SSE?","text":"When should an AI application use SSE? Server-Sent Events suit a server-to-browser stream of text or task progress over HTTP. Use a separate request to submit work or cancel a task when bidirectional messaging is unnecessary. Define a stable event schema and stream the safe projection of run state. SSE is a transport for events, not a durable task engine. An open connection does not prove that work is persisted or authorized. SSE fits HTTP infrastructure and offers only one-way messaging on that connection. Compare a chat answer stream with a collaborative voice interaction."},{"id":422,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How is an SSE message framed?","text":"How is an SSE message framed? An event consists of text fields followed by a blank line. Data may span multiple data lines, which the receiver combines according to SSE rules. Network chunks do not align with event boundaries. Decode UTF-8 incrementally, accumulate lines, and dispatch only at the event delimiter. Normalize supported line endings and impose limits. Parsing each received chunk as JSON fails when characters or messages split across chunks. Correct incremental parsing adds state and avoids intermittent corruption. Split a Unicode event at every byte boundary and verify equivalent decoding."},{"id":423,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How should EventSource and fetch streaming be compared?","text":"How should EventSource and fetch streaming be compared? Browser EventSource handles an SSE GET connection and reconnection behavior. Fetch streaming gives control over methods, headers, and request bodies but requires application parsing and retry logic. Choose based on authentication, request semantics, and resume requirements. Do not assume EventSource accepts arbitrary authorization headers. Putting bearer tokens in query parameters can expose them in logs and history. Fetch offers flexibility while EventSource supplies useful protocol behavior. Design a cookie-authenticated EventSource flow and a header-authenticated fetch flow."},{"id":424,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"What should a public AI event schema contain?","text":"What should a public AI event schema contain? Include schema version, task ID, ordered sequence, event type, and typed payload. Distinguish text deltas, tool activity, final results, recoverable notices, and terminal errors. Convert provider events through an adapter. The UI should reduce these events into explicit states rather than concatenate every payload into answer text. Provider-internal messages can leak private arguments or confuse partial output with completion. Stable schemas add adapter maintenance and protect the UI from provider churn. Document how a tool error changes state differently from an answer delta."},{"id":425,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How should reconnection and replay work?","text":"How should reconnection and replay work? Give events durable sequence IDs and define a retention window. Resume from the last acknowledged or processed event, and deduplicate repeated sequence numbers. If replay history expired, return a snapshot or a clear resume failure. Revalidate ownership on every connection. Reconnecting without a replay contract can lose events or duplicate displayed text. Durable event storage adds cost and enables recovery. Disconnect after event seven and resume without repeating the final business operation."},{"id":426,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How do heartbeats and proxy buffering affect streams?","text":"How do heartbeats and proxy buffering affect streams? Intermediaries may buffer output or close idle connections. Heartbeats can keep an otherwise idle stream active, but infrastructure behavior must be measured. Use appropriate content type and cache policy, disable buffering where supported, and send bounded periodic comments. Check actual arrival times through the production proxy. Application flushes alone do not establish that the browser receives each event promptly. Frequent heartbeats consume connections and bandwidth. Record server-send and browser-receive timestamps behind your actual ingress."},{"id":427,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How should cancellation work with SSE?","text":"How should cancellation work with SSE? Closing a stream should stop unnecessary generation according to product policy, while durable tasks may continue independently. Expose a task cancellation endpoint for explicit control. Propagate cancellation to providers when supported and record the final task state. Preserve committed operation IDs for later lookup. A disconnected stream does not prove that external effects were cancelled. Separating task and stream lifetimes adds complexity and enables background completion. Close the stream during a pending tool call and inspect the authoritative task status."},{"id":428,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How should backpressure be handled?","text":"How should backpressure be handled? Bound producer-to-writer queues and prevent slow clients from retaining unlimited memory. Decide which progress events can be coalesced and which terminal events must be retained. Await writable capacity where the framework supports it, monitor queue depth, and disconnect consumers that cannot keep up under the contract. Dropping arbitrary text deltas corrupts the answer. Strong limits may disconnect slow clients and protect the server. Simulate a client reading one event per second while the producer emits many more."},{"id":429,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"What should completion mean in an SSE stream?","text":"What should completion mean in an SSE stream? A completion event should identify the persisted result or authoritative final task state. A finished model token sequence is not necessarily a completed application operation. Persist the final record before emitting completion and support owner-scoped result retrieval after disconnect. A crash after sending done but before commit creates a false success. Persist-before-complete adds final latency and strengthens recovery. Crash at the completion boundary and verify the UI can recover from the result endpoint."},{"id":430,"slug":"sse-streaming","chapter":"SSE and resilient HTTP streaming","title":"How should SSE be tested?","text":"How should SSE be tested? Test UTF-8 boundaries, multiline data, line endings, comments, empty data, malformed payloads, replay, authorization, disconnects, and slow consumers. Test through the real proxy as well as in process. Keep transport decoding separate from JSON schema validation and UI reduction. Assert both event order and terminal state. A happy-path mock stream misses buffering and network fragmentation. Real transport tests cost more and catch difficult intermittent failures. Build a replay fixture containing a duplicate event and an expired resume token."},{"id":431,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"When should WebSockets replace SSE?","text":"When should WebSockets replace SSE? Use WebSockets when the client and server need low-latency bidirectional messaging during an active session, such as audio frames or collaborative control. SSE remains sufficient for many one-way progress streams. Compare required message direction, infrastructure, binary data needs, reconnect behavior, and authentication. Choosing WebSockets does not automatically improve application latency. Duplex sessions add lifecycle and routing complexity. Specify the messages that genuinely require a persistent bidirectional channel."},{"id":432,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"How should WebSocket authentication work?","text":"How should WebSocket authentication work? Authenticate the handshake and authorize the session's resources, then recheck permissions for sensitive messages and long-lived sessions. Validate allowed origins for browser clients. Use an explicit token or cookie strategy supported by the client and server. Handle expiry and revocation without logging credentials. A valid connection at startup can outlive the user's permission. Frequent revalidation adds work and limits stale access. Revoke a user's access during an active session and verify the next protected action is denied."},{"id":433,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"What belongs in a WebSocket message envelope?","text":"What belongs in a WebSocket message envelope? Include schema version, session ID, message ID, sequence, type, correlation ID, and typed payload. Distinguish commands, acknowledgments, events, errors, and terminal states. Validate message size and schema before dispatch. Use binary frames for audio where appropriate and explicit metadata for format. An untyped message bus invites accidental interpretation and oversized payload abuse. Verbose envelopes add bandwidth and improve evolution and diagnosis. Design a cancel command correlated with the generation it targets."},{"id":434,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"How do acknowledgments and delivery guarantees work?","text":"How do acknowledgments and delivery guarantees work? TCP provides ordered bytes while connected; application acknowledgments establish which logical messages were processed. Reconnection needs explicit replay and deduplication rules. Persist critical commands or outcomes with stable IDs and make repeated handling idempotent. Receiving a frame is not proof that a business transaction committed. Stronger delivery semantics add storage and protocol complexity. Disconnect between processing a command and sending its acknowledgment."},{"id":435,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"How should flow control work for WebSockets?","text":"How should flow control work for WebSockets? Bound inbound and outbound queues, limit frame sizes, and define how producers pause or shed nonessential progress. Standard browser WebSocket APIs do not provide automatic application backpressure. Monitor buffered bytes and processing latency. Prioritize control messages such as cancellation over bulk content. An audio producer can overwhelm a slow consumer and cause memory growth or large delays. Dropping stale audio reduces latency and may affect intelligibility. Simulate a slow network and ensure cancellation still reaches the server promptly."},{"id":436,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"What are ping, pong, and application heartbeats for?","text":"What are ping, pong, and application heartbeats for? Transport-level ping and pong help detect connection liveness where exposed. Application heartbeats can verify end-to-end processing and carry session status. Set timeouts compatible with proxies and mobile networks, and distinguish temporary silence from failure. A healthy connection does not prove that the agent or downstream service is progressing. Aggressive timeouts reduce dead sessions and create false disconnects. Pause the application worker while leaving the socket open and inspect health signals."},{"id":437,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"How should reconnecting clients recover state?","text":"How should reconnecting clients recover state? Reauthenticate, present a resume token or last processed sequence, and receive replay or a state snapshot. The server decides whether the old session can safely resume. Expire resume capabilities, bind them to the owner, and deduplicate commands and events. Replaying every outbound client message can repeat a side effect. Durable recovery costs state storage and supports unstable networks. Reconnect after an ambiguous write and recover through its operation ID."},{"id":438,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"How do WebSocket servers scale horizontally?","text":"How do WebSocket servers scale horizontally? Maintain durable session and task state outside one process, route active connections deliberately, and distribute events through a shared channel when needed. Capacity-plan open connections, memory, bandwidth, worker count, and draining behavior. Sticky routing does not replace durable recovery. A process-local registry loses sessions on restart and cannot coordinate a fleet. Shared infrastructure adds latency and enables scale and recovery. Drain one instance during active sessions and verify the expected reconnect path."},{"id":439,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"How should binary audio and text events coexist?","text":"How should binary audio and text events coexist? Define a protocol that identifies audio format, stream, timestamps, and sequence while using typed control events for transcripts and state. Keep ordering assumptions explicit. Validate sample rate, channels, codec, and payload size. Maintain separate playback and control queues when useful. Misinterpreted encoding can produce noise, broken timing, or excessive memory use. A richer protocol adds implementation work and supports multimodal sessions. Change the declared audio format midstream and verify a controlled rejection."},{"id":440,"slug":"websocket-streaming","chapter":"WebSockets and bidirectional systems","title":"What should a WebSocket production test include?","text":"What should a WebSocket production test include? Test authorization, origin checks, oversized frames, expired sessions, malformed messages, reconnects, duplicate commands, slow consumers, network loss, and deployment draining. Measure end-to-end command latency and queue depth under realistic concurrency. A local echo server does not establish reliability of a distributed agent session. Load tests require representative infrastructure and reveal connection capacity limits. Publish a capacity report with connection count, memory use, and p95 control-message latency."},{"id":441,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How does a cascaded voice pipeline differ from speech-to-speech?","text":"How does a cascaded voice pipeline differ from speech-to-speech? A cascaded system uses speech recognition, a text-based agent, and speech synthesis. A speech-to-speech model processes and generates audio more directly, with provider-specific capabilities. Compare transcript control, latency, language support, observability, and tool integration. Evaluate the complete spoken conversation rather than only transcript quality. Direct audio generation can make textual audit and output filtering harder. Cascades add stage latency and offer clearer intermediate boundaries. Diagram both designs for the same support task and identify their dominant delays."},{"id":442,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"What are ASR, STT, TTS, and VAD?","text":"What are ASR, STT, TTS, and VAD? ASR and STT refer to speech recognition or speech-to-text; TTS converts text to speech; VAD detects speech activity. They address different parts of a voice interaction. VAD segments activity, recognition produces hypotheses, the agent resolves intent, and synthesis produces playback audio. Track timing and errors at each stage. VAD can confuse background noise with speech or clip quiet words. Separate components improve diagnosis and increase integration complexity. Record a noisy phrase and identify which stage first introduced an error."},{"id":443,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How should partial transcripts be handled?","text":"How should partial transcripts be handled? Treat partial recognition as revisable hypotheses. Only finalized or explicitly confirmed content should drive consequential actions. Attach utterance IDs and revisions to transcript events and replace earlier hypotheses rather than appending them as independent facts. Acting on an unfinished phrase can invert intent when the user adds a negation. Waiting for finalization adds latency and improves intent stability. Stream I want to cancel followed by not the order, the reminder and inspect tool behavior."},{"id":444,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"What is turn detection and why is it difficult?","text":"What is turn detection and why is it difficult? Turn detection decides when the user has finished speaking and when the agent should respond. Silence alone may reflect hesitation, language patterns, or network delay. Combine acoustic activity with configurable timeouts and conversational signals. Let users correct premature interruptions. Overly aggressive endpointing can cut off names, numbers, or accessibility-related speech patterns. Longer waits improve completeness and increase perceived delay. Evaluate turn boundaries across slow speech, pauses, background noise, and multiple languages."},{"id":445,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How should barge-in and interruption work?","text":"How should barge-in and interruption work? When the user interrupts, stop or duck playback, cancel obsolete generation where possible, and update conversation state to reflect what was actually delivered. Use generation IDs to reject stale audio after cancellation. Track played audio duration separately from generated audio. The agent may assume the user heard content that remained in a playback buffer. Precise interruption handling adds state and improves conversational control. Interrupt halfway through a confirmation and verify that no action is authorized by unplayed content."},{"id":446,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How should a voice agent confirm sensitive actions?","text":"How should a voice agent confirm sensitive actions? Present the material parameters in a clear review and obtain a valid confirmation under the product's authorization policy. Recognition confidence alone is insufficient for consequential intent. Bind confirmation to the exact operation and require a stronger authenticated channel when appropriate. Background speech, spoofed audio, or transcript errors can trigger false intent. Stronger confirmation adds friction and protects high-impact operations. Change an amount after spoken review and verify the old confirmation cannot execute it."},{"id":447,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How should audio latency be measured?","text":"How should audio latency be measured? Measure capture-to-transcript, end-of-turn-to-first-response-audio, tool delay, synthesis delay, jitter, and playback buffering. User-perceived latency spans all stages. Correlate monotonic timestamps across client and server where possible, and report p95 delays under realistic networks. A fast model token metric can hide slow endpoint detection or playback buffering. More instrumentation increases complexity and identifies the real bottleneck. Build a timing waterfall for one interrupted spoken conversation."},{"id":448,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How do WebRTC and WebSockets differ for voice?","text":"How do WebRTC and WebSockets differ for voice? WebRTC provides media-oriented transport and real-time communication features; WebSockets provide a general ordered duplex channel. The best choice depends on client, provider, and infrastructure support. Evaluate audio encoding, network behavior, media controls, authentication, and deployment complexity. Use short-lived credentials for direct client sessions when supported. A long-lived provider secret must not be embedded in browser code. WebRTC can suit interactive media while requiring signaling and session setup. Explain the credential flow for a browser-to-provider audio connection."},{"id":449,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How should voice privacy and accessibility be designed?","text":"How should voice privacy and accessibility be designed? Decide whether raw audio, transcripts, and derived summaries are retained, for how long, and who can access them. Provide a usable text alternative and visible recording state. Minimize collection, redact sensitive content where appropriate, and evaluate speech quality across accents, noise, and assistive needs. Voice can reveal identity and sensitive traits beyond the spoken words. Reduced retention limits diagnosis and exposure. Design a deletion request that covers audio artifacts, transcripts, and searchable derivatives."},{"id":450,"slug":"voice-agents","chapter":"Voice agents and realtime audio","title":"How should voice agents be evaluated?","text":"How should voice agents be evaluated? Measure task completion, transcription errors affecting intent, interruption quality, unsafe actions, latency, and accessibility across representative speakers and conditions. Use consented or synthetic fixtures and human listening review for aspects text metrics miss. Validate durable outcomes in sandbox services. Word error rate alone cannot measure whether the correct task was completed. Audio evaluation costs more and captures real interaction failures. Compare two agents on noisy numeric inputs, corrections, interruptions, and failed tool calls."},{"id":451,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How does OCR differ from document understanding?","text":"How does OCR differ from document understanding? OCR recognizes text from images. Document understanding also identifies structure, fields, tables, relationships, and meaning needed for a task. Vision-language models may assist but require validation. Preserve the original artifact and attach page, region, and extraction provenance to text and fields. Correct characters in the wrong reading order can still yield an incorrect conclusion. Rich structure improves downstream retrieval and costs more processing. Compare plain OCR text with a layout-aware extraction of a two-column document."},{"id":452,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should scanned files enter an ingestion pipeline?","text":"How should scanned files enter an ingestion pipeline? Validate file type and size, quarantine untrusted uploads, scan according to policy, extract pages, perform OCR where needed, and index only reviewed derived content. Keep upload, extraction, indexing, and publication states separate. Use scoped artifact IDs instead of trusting user-supplied filesystem paths. Malformed documents can exploit parsers or exhaust resources. Isolation adds latency and protects the ingestion service. Submit an oversized synthetic PDF and verify rejection occurs before expensive processing."},{"id":453,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"Why do bounding boxes and page references matter?","text":"Why do bounding boxes and page references matter? They connect recognized text to a location in the original document, enabling source review and visual verification. A plain text answer loses that spatial traceability. Store page number, normalized coordinates, extraction version, and source revision with relevant spans. Re-rendering or cropping without updating coordinates can make citations point to the wrong region. Spatial metadata increases storage and supports reliable review. Open a cited field in the source viewer and highlight the exact recognized region."},{"id":454,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should tables be extracted?","text":"How should tables be extracted? Preserve row, column, header, merged-cell, unit, and footnote relationships. Flattening table cells into prose can pair a value with the wrong label. Use structured extraction plus deterministic validation for totals, units, and required headers. Retain page and cell provenance. A plausible table reconstruction may silently invent missing cells. Higher-fidelity parsing costs more and improves numerical tasks. Extract a table containing a footnote that changes one row's meaning."},{"id":455,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should OCR confidence be used?","text":"How should OCR confidence be used? Treat confidence as an extractor-specific signal, not a universal probability that a business field is correct. Calibrate thresholds against reviewed examples for the target documents. Combine confidence with format checks, cross-field consistency, and human review for high-impact values. A confidently recognized wrong digit can still pass a superficial threshold. More review reduces automation throughput and catches consequential errors. Build a reviewed dataset of dates and amounts and measure false acceptance at several thresholds."},{"id":456,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How can document text inject instructions into an agent?","text":"How can document text inject instructions into an agent? OCR and vision outputs are untrusted source content. A document can contain instructions aimed at changing the agent's behavior or leaking data. Label extracted content as evidence, restrict tools independently, and evaluate attacks placed inside visible text, tables, and images. Converting an image into text does not make the text trusted. Layered controls add work and keep document content from becoming authority. Place a harmless malicious-instruction fixture in an image and verify no unauthorized action occurs."},{"id":457,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should multimodal retrieval combine text and images?","text":"How should multimodal retrieval combine text and images? Index text, captions, and image representations under a shared document identity while preserving each representation's provenance and access scope. Choose representations based on the task. Retrieve candidates using suitable models and validate the relevant original region before a consequential conclusion. A generated caption can omit a decisive visual feature or add unsupported detail. Multiple representations improve recall and increase cost and synchronization work. Compare a text-only search with a mixed representation search on diagram-heavy documents."},{"id":458,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should extracted fields be validated?","text":"How should extracted fields be validated? Apply types, allowed formats, ranges, cross-field consistency, and business rules after extraction. Preserve missing and ambiguous states rather than filling them with guesses. Separate raw recognized text from normalized values and retain transformations such as date interpretation or currency conversion. A locale mismatch can swap day and month while producing a valid date. Strict normalization causes review cases and reduces silent corruption. Parse ambiguous dates under two locales and require an explicit resolution policy."},{"id":459,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should document revisions and deletions propagate?","text":"How should document revisions and deletions propagate? Track original and derived artifacts under a revision identity, then invalidate text, embeddings, summaries, caches, and citations when policy requires. Publish a new index snapshot only after the replacement is complete. Keep retention rules separate from search visibility. Removing the original file alone leaves private derived data accessible. Full lineage costs storage and supports coherent lifecycle management. Delete a synthetic document and verify every derived retrieval path stops exposing it."},{"id":460,"slug":"ocr-document-ai","chapter":"OCR and document intelligence","title":"How should an OCR system be evaluated end to end?","text":"How should an OCR system be evaluated end to end? Measure character or word errors where useful, field accuracy, table structure, citation location, downstream task success, and human-review burden. Segment by document type and scan quality. Include rotated, blurred, multilingual, handwritten, and adversarial examples that match the product scope. High OCR accuracy can coexist with poor business-field accuracy. Broad fixtures require effort and reveal practical coverage limits. Publish a confusion analysis showing which document types need human review."},{"id":461,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"What makes an agent live or realtime?","text":"What makes an agent live or realtime? A live agent reacts to ongoing inputs and changing state under a latency target. It may process speech, events, sensors, or user interaction rather than one isolated request. Define the required response deadline and which state changes can invalidate active work. Use an explicit session and task lifecycle. Calling a system realtime without a measured latency contract hides important product limitations. Lower latency can reduce deliberation and increase infrastructure cost. Specify a latency target for interruption handling separately from final task completion."},{"id":462,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How should events trigger agent work?","text":"How should events trigger agent work? Validate and normalize external events, deduplicate them, check scope, and schedule bounded tasks. The model should not directly subscribe to an unrestricted event firehose. Use an event envelope with ID, source, subject, revision, and timestamp. Record why an event created or updated a task. Duplicate or reordered events can repeat actions or restore stale state. Event infrastructure adds complexity and supports responsive workflows. Deliver the same update twice and verify only one task effect occurs."},{"id":463,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How do you handle stale work when new events arrive?","text":"How do you handle stale work when new events arrive? Associate work with a state version or generation ID. Before publishing a result or committing a write, verify that the assumptions remain applicable. Cancel or supersede obsolete generation and use conditional writes for authoritative state. An old model response can overwrite a newer user instruction if arrival order is treated as authority. Version checks can discard useful computation and preserve correctness. Send a correction while the agent is working and reject the earlier draft as stale."},{"id":464,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"What is event-time versus processing-time reasoning?","text":"What is event-time versus processing-time reasoning? Event time describes when something occurred; processing time describes when the system handled it. Delayed delivery makes them different and can change applicability decisions. Retain both timestamps and define ordering and lateness policies per event type. Use monotonic clocks for local duration measurement. Sorting only by arrival can misrepresent the user's actual sequence of actions. Lateness handling adds state and supports unstable networks. Process a late event and decide whether it updates history, current state, or neither."},{"id":465,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How should long-lived session memory be bounded?","text":"How should long-lived session memory be bounded? Keep a compact working state, recent events, durable operation references, and relevant retrieved memory. Archive detailed history under controlled retention instead of appending forever. Periodically checkpoint and compact using validated fields. Rebuild essential facts from authoritative systems after restart. Unbounded histories increase token cost and retain unnecessary private data. Compaction reduces detail and controls operational growth. Run a synthetic session through hundreds of events and measure memory and prompt size."},{"id":466,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How should human escalation work for live agents?","text":"How should human escalation work for live agents? Create a durable handoff with owner, reason, urgency, safe summary, evidence, completed actions, and unresolved questions. Tell the user the actual handoff state. Integrate with a real queue or ticket service and expose acceptance and resolution events. Saying a specialist has been contacted without a recorded handoff is false completion. Human review adds delay and handles ambiguity beyond automation scope. Simulate an unavailable escalation service and show an honest fallback."},{"id":467,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How do presence and connection state differ from task state?","text":"How do presence and connection state differ from task state? Presence indicates whether a client is connected or recently active. Task state records accepted work, progress, and outcomes. A disconnected user may still have a running task. Keep separate connection, session, and task records with distinct expiry and recovery rules. Tying task lifetime blindly to one socket can lose durable work on a network interruption. Separate lifecycles add modeling and improve recovery. Close the client, complete a background task, and reconnect to its result."},{"id":468,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How should live agents handle overload?","text":"How should live agents handle overload? Use admission control, bounded queues, priority policies, and explicit degraded behavior. Prefer rejecting new work clearly over silently accumulating unbounded delay. Reserve capacity for cancellation, status, and safety controls. Track queue age and deadline expiry. Treating every request equally can starve critical controls behind bulk generation. Priority policies add fairness decisions and keep the system operable. Flood nonessential events and verify that cancellation remains responsive."},{"id":469,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"What should live-agent observability emphasize?","text":"What should live-agent observability emphasize? Track event lag, queue age, active sessions, stale-result rejection, reconnect rate, cancellation latency, task outcomes, and cost per active period. Correlate events, tasks, model runs, and operations with stable IDs and safe metadata. A healthy socket count can conceal a stalled worker or outdated responses. Fine-grained metrics increase instrumentation effort and reveal real responsiveness. Create an alert for rising event lag while HTTP health remains green."},{"id":470,"slug":"live-agents","chapter":"Live agents, event-driven work, and realtime state","title":"How should live-agent reliability be demonstrated?","text":"How should live-agent reliability be demonstrated? Show duplicate delivery, out-of-order events, network loss, user interruption, process restart, provider outage, and overload recovery under a declared contract. Use synthetic scenarios with expected final states and record timing evidence. Review both user-visible behavior and durable effects. A short low-load demo cannot establish long-running reliability. Failure demonstrations take time and provide strong engineering evidence. Record a session that survives a restart without duplicating an approved action."},{"id":471,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"What should a complete capstone include?","text":"What should a complete capstone include? Build one scoped assistant with a UI, authenticated API, retrieval, a limited agent, durable business operations, evaluation, tracing, and recovery. Add voice or document AI only when the use case needs it. Define a small set of useful tasks and publish synthetic fixtures, architecture decisions, and a reproducible local setup. Adding many features can hide weak core guarantees. Narrow scope is easier to evaluate and may look less broad. Choose three user journeys and one explicit out-of-scope request before coding."},{"id":472,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"How should the learning journey be sequenced?","text":"How should the learning journey be sequenced? Learn backend and data fundamentals, retrieval and evidence, agent tool use, context and prompts, durable orchestration, evaluation, then multimodal and live delivery. Revisit earlier layers as failures emerge. Build a working increment at each stage and defend its design trade-offs. The roadmap is a dependency path, not a fixed calendar guarantee. Reading all chapters without implementing leaves integration gaps undiscovered. Incremental projects take more setup and produce stronger understanding. Map each chapter to one artifact or demonstrated behavior."},{"id":473,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"What should a public portfolio say about AI assistance?","text":"What should a public portfolio say about AI assistance? Explain your role in design, implementation, review, and validation, including where AI helped. Claims of competence should be supported by decisions and working evidence. Publish a short engineering log describing a failure you found, how you diagnosed it, and how you verified the fix. Presenting generated explanations as verified production results undermines trust. Transparency may reveal limits and makes your work easier to assess. Write a project description separating demonstrated behavior from planned extensions."},{"id":474,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"How should project documentation be structured?","text":"How should project documentation be structured? Lead with the user problem, supported tasks, architecture, setup, operating constraints, evaluation results, and known limitations. Link deeper explanations from this overview. Keep commands reproducible with synthetic data and clearly scoped dependency versions. Show one success and one failure walkthrough. A large README without a runnable path can make a strong project hard to assess. Concise onboarding requires separate detail pages and improves accessibility. Ask a new reader to run one task without verbal guidance."},{"id":475,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"Which measurements belong in a portfolio report?","text":"Which measurements belong in a portfolio report? Report task completion, unsupported-answer rate, authorization invariants, recovery outcomes, p95 latency, and cost under a described workload. Include dataset size and environment. Distinguish measured numbers from targets and estimates. Retain configuration and release IDs so a comparison can be reproduced. Benchmark claims without task mix or hardware context are difficult to interpret. Honest limitations may look less impressive and provide credible evidence. Publish a small result table with a baseline, one improvement, and one regression."},{"id":476,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"How should an architecture decision record be written?","text":"How should an architecture decision record be written? Describe the concrete problem, constraints, considered options, chosen approach, consequences, and conditions that would trigger reconsideration. Keep it tied to evidence. For example, justify SSE over WebSockets through message direction and reconnect needs rather than popularity. A retrospective justification can conceal alternatives that were never evaluated. Decision records cost time and preserve engineering context. Write one record for transaction ownership and one for agent orchestration."},{"id":477,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"How should a capstone release be secured?","text":"How should a capstone release be secured? Remove secrets and customer data, constrain tools and outbound access, validate identity and ownership, and isolate evaluation side effects. Include dependency and artifact provenance. Use a reviewed deployment manifest and a rollback path. Keep administrative actions separate from ordinary agent capabilities. A public demo can expose backend endpoints even if the repository is private. Hardened deployment adds friction and supports credible public access. Threat-model the demo as an anonymous user and an authenticated malicious user."},{"id":478,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"How should interviews use the roadmap?","text":"How should interviews use the roadmap? Explain a complete journey, then zoom into one failure boundary and defend a trade-off. Use diagrams and actual measurements to distinguish understanding from terminology recall. Practice concise answers first, then expand into transaction scope, context design, permissions, and evaluation when asked. Memorized framework definitions do not demonstrate reasoning under changed constraints. Deep specialization reduces breadth and produces stronger technical discussions. Answer how your design changes when traffic grows tenfold or a provider fails."},{"id":479,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"What should completion of a chapter mean?","text":"What should completion of a chapter mean? You can explain the mechanism, implement a small example, reproduce a relevant failure, and justify a trade-off. A learned checkbox is a personal study signal. Use the chapter lab acceptance criteria and write your own explanation before revealing the detailed answer. Marking everything complete without independent work gives misleading progress. Higher standards slow apparent progress and deepen practical competence. Revisit one learned topic and solve its exercise without viewing the answer."},{"id":480,"slug":"portfolio-capstone","chapter":"Build, evaluate, and publish your AI engineering portfolio","title":"How should the roadmap continue after the capstone?","text":"How should the roadmap continue after the capstone? Choose a real weakness revealed by evaluation: retrieval quality, multilingual behavior, privacy, latency, model serving, accessibility, or operational recovery. Deepen that area with a controlled experiment. Keep a baseline and compare one intervention at a time. Add a new framework only when it solves a defined problem. Collecting technologies can fragment the system without improving user outcomes. Focused depth limits feature count and builds durable engineering judgment. Propose your next experiment with a hypothesis, dataset, metric, budget, and stopping rule."}];
